$\Pp_x$-almost surely. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:52:32.343885+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c14d337c30a1483913145d0ca68991b0f16e5b8c47d39066ae1de34549ffd466","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def weighted stable nodal family of arXiv:2609.04023, 'Characterizing Contractions and Weighted Blowdowns'] \begin{enumerate}
    \item Define input data to be a tuple $(g,\mathscr{A}):=(g,a_1,\dots,a_n)$ consisting of an integer $g\ge 0$ and the {\em weight data} $(a_1,\dots,a_n)\in \mathbb{Q}^n$ satisfying $0<a_j\leq 1$ for $j=1,\ldots,n$ and $2g-2+a_1+a_2+\dots +a_n>0$.
    \item A family of nodal curves with marked points $(C,s_1,\ldots,s_n)\xrightarrow{\pi}B$ is stable of type $(g,\mathscr{A})$ if
    \begin{itemize}
        \item the sections $s_1,\ldots,s_n$ lie in the smooth locus of $\pi$,
        \item for any $\{i_1,\dots, i_r\}\subseteq [n]$ such that $\bigcap_{j=1}^{r}s_{i_j}(B)\neq\emptyset$, we have $a_{i_1}+\dots +a_{i_r}\leq 1$ and,
        \item $K_{\pi}+a_1s_1+a_2s_2+\dots +a_ns_n$ is $\pi$-ample.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

