{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:41.019867+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c16b0e54344bf29f4e45e152b55bdabda6b44fd554792fd2815d44acfee68b4b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 2-l05 of arXiv:2412.04015, 'Linear fluctuation of interfaces in Glauber-Kawasaki dynamics'] There exists a finite constant ${\mathfrak c}({\boldsymbol u}^N)$, depending only on
${\boldsymbol u}^N$, $A$ and the dimension $d$, uniformly bounded in $N$
($\sup_N {\mathfrak c}({\boldsymbol u}^N) < \infty$), such that
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
& K\, \int  {\mathcal W}^{(2)}_\ell \, f\; d\nu^N \; \le\;
\delta\, N^2 \, I^E_{N}(f; \nu^N)
\; +\; {\mathfrak c}({\boldsymbol u}^N)  \, K\, \sum_{x\in{\mathbb T}_N^d}
\int (\omega^{\ell} _{x})^2 \, f \; d\nu^N
\\
