{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:30.066485+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c1f18e5d81047077271021b0aba655dc42785670c7a4912bc6e7a117d6679c0e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 (Action of $L^X$ on monomials) of arXiv:2606.00781, '2606.00781'] The generator $L^X$ acts on the $n$-th monomial $f_n(x):=x^n$ via
   \begin{equation} 
      L^Xf_n(x)\ =\ \sum_{k=1}^n \varphi(k,n)x^{n-k}-2\varphi(n)x^n,
      \qquad n\in\nz_0,
   \end{equation}
   where $\varphi(k,n)$ is defined via (\ref{lambda_rep}) and
   the total rate $\varphi(n)$ is defined via
   \begin{equation} 
      \varphi(n)
      \ :=\ \sum_{k=1} ^n\varphi(k,n)
      \ =\ dn+\kappa+\int_{(0,1)}\frac{1-(1-t)^n}{t}\,\Lambda({\rm d}t),
      \qquad n\in\nz_0,
   \end{equation}
   with drift $d:=\Lambda(\{0\})$ and killing rate $\kappa:=\Lambda(\{1\})$. In particular, $L^X$ acts on the identity $f_1$ via $L^Xf_1(x)=(1-2x)\Lambda([0,1])$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

