Then, almost surely, $\mathcal S^{\mathrm b}=\mathcal S^*$ in the sense of~(equation eq:def_dual_+ of the paper). In particular, $\{(\mathcal S^*_{-r,-x}(a),\,x\ge r)\}_{(a,r)\in I \times {\mathbb R}}$ forms a stochastic flow on $I\times{\mathbb R}$ with boundary conditions $\eta^*$, and $(\mathcal S^*)^*=\mathcal S$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:32:23.662689+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2da472232e2fff50254fbc38ad3f336d31346fa3a1b873cc5fc0ddafcffccd8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem ratio TSAW is ratio G of arXiv:2603.17858, 'Decay of correlations and zeros for the hard-core model'] Let $G=(V,E)$ be a graph, let $v$ be a vertex of $G$ and let $(\lambda_u)_{u\in V}$ be a vector of variables. 
    Then there exists a vector of variables $(\tilde \lambda_w)_{w\in V(T_{\mathrm{SAW}(G,v)})}$ with $\tilde \lambda_w\in \{\lambda_u\mid u\in V\}$ for each vertex $w$ of $T_{\mathrm{SAW}(G,v)}$ such that
    \begin{equation}
        R_{T_{\mathrm{SAW}(G,v)},v}(\tilde\lambda_w)=R_{G,v}(\lambda_u).  
    \end{equation}
    Moreover, for any vertex $u$ of $T_{\mathrm{SAW}(G,v)}$, the induced subtree $(T,u)$ of $T_{\mathrm{SAW}(G,v)}$, with those vertices $w$ such that $u$ is on the unique path from $w$ to $v$, is the self avoiding walk tree of an induced subgraph of $G$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:41:28.169329+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2daf02a4503c3015c0bccd8b003dda9e8421c5730a0c05db5b8d3f516acc3ac","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D_SB of arXiv:2510.26724, 'Sharp Fuss-Catalan thresholds in graph bootstrap percolation'] A graph $H$ is {\it strictly balanced} if 
\begin{equation}
\frac{e(F)-1}{v(F)-2} < \lambda(H),
\end{equation}
for every subgraph $F\subset H$ 
with $3\leqslant v(F)<v(H)$, 
where
\begin{equation}
\lambda(H)=\frac{e(H)-2}{v(H)-2}
\end{equation}
is its {\it $\lambda$-density} 
(extending \ (equation E_lambda of the paper) above).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:18.993083+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2e15a73679406db9d344f93e6773d01ac956d8e06e11baf6c666210c9b43644","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma HLP of arXiv:2609.02541, 'A note on the norm of the embedding of $\text{PW}^2$ into $\text{PW}^q$'] Assume that $f,g: [0, \infty) \xrightarrow{} \mathbb{R}^{+}$ are non-increasing functions with 
    \begin{equation}

    \int_0^t f(x) dx \leq \int_0^t g(x)dx
    \end{equation}
    for all $0<t< \infty$.
    Then 
    $$\int_0^{\infty} \Phi(f(x))dx \leq \int_0^{\infty} \Phi(g(x))dx$$
    holds for every increasing convex function $\Phi: \mathbb{R}^{+} \xrightarrow{} \mathbb{R}^{+}$ such that $\Phi(0) = 0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:22:08.521535+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2e33b1fbe3e41c262152bd348fc880ecf484333c823292379a912ad14e6878f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:PTASEP-H4 of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] The dressed observable satisfies the mixed diamond
equation
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\bigl(q\mathcal{M}_{t+1, \mathbf{a}+\mathbf{1},
\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{1}}^{-1}
+ p\mathcal{M}_{t+1, \mathbf{a}+\mathbf{2},
\mathbf{n}}^{-1}\bigr)
\mathcal{M}_{t+2, \mathbf{a}+\mathbf{1},
\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{1}} \\
&\qquad - \mathcal{M}_{t, \mathbf{a}+\mathbf{1},
\mathbf{n}}^{-1}
\bigl(q\mathcal{M}_{t+1, \mathbf{a}+\mathbf{1},
\mathbf{n}}
+ p\mathcal{M}_{t+1, \mathbf{a},
\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{1}}\bigr) = 0,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
at every $(t, \mathbf{a}, \mathbf{n})$ with
$a_i < h_{n_i+1}(t+1) - 1$ for all~$i$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:50.706151+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2e515e79f483d3434e4d2aa9ba801837fe64d5825e1120b065dd02a1fdec11e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop strong inclusion of arXiv:2609.00436, 'A counterexample to the stable forking conjecture'] Let $Q \subseteq Q'$ be division rings with $Q$ a subring of $Q'$, and $M \subseteq M'$ with $M$ a left $Q$-module and $M'$ a left $Q'$-module, such that $M$ is a submodule of $M'$ with the induced $Q$-module structure. Then the following are equivalent:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item Every linearly independent set in $M$ over $Q$ remains linearly independent over $Q'$.

        \item Some basis for $M$ over $Q$ remains linearly independent over $Q'$.
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:04:59.425789+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2eed7e5531bacde2822cdfbb7ce940ad3f77032e30ccb872fbe9526318b02f8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop Etildeillbehaved of arXiv:2605.18744, 'Lattice random-field Widom--Rowlinson models'] There are $c,C >0$ such that for all $z/\varepsilon>C$, it holds that
