where $\msf L(\partial D)$ is the length of the curve
$\partial D$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:48:43.520151+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2fee1c24210eacc1cf1ed8b7ff3995323fc2c571452bc7ae35f2d6e62b7320e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.06646, 'Three-dimensional stochastic wave equation with non-Lipschitz coefficients'] Assume $u_0\in C^2\lr{\R^3}$. Suppose $u_0$, $\nabla u_0$, and $v_0$ are bounded and let $u_0$, $\Delta u_0$, and $v_0$ be H\"{o}lder continuous with exponents $\alpha_1$, $\alpha_2$, and $\alpha_3$, respectively. We also assume Assumption \ref{A:CovAssumption} and that $b$ and $\sigma$ are locally Lipschitz with the following growth condition.
    \begin{equation}
        b(z)=O\lr{|z|\lr{\log |z|}^{\theta_1}}\quad\text{and}\quad \sigma(z)=O\lr{|z|\lr{\log |z|}^{\theta_2}}\quad\text{as $|z|\to \infty$}\,,
    \end{equation}
    with $0<\theta_1<  2$, $0<\theta_2<\frac{\bar{\nu}+1}{2}$ and $\bar{\nu}=\min\{\nu,\nu_1,\nu_2\}$, where $\nu,\nu_1,\nu_2$ are given by Assumption~\ref{A:CovAssumption}. Then there exists a unique global solution $u$ to~\eqref{E:SWE}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:42:57.183726+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c300083ef9161a2810a14f6bf85045155784c6353d0a9bc890283665de880d46","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:distance_across_rectangle of arXiv:2609.04140, 'The conformally invariant metric on $\CLE_4$ III:\\ uniqueness'] {kkmt2026cle4_part2}}]
	For each $r > 0$, write
	\begin{equation*}
		V_r \defeq \left\{z \in \BC : 0 < \Re(z) < r, \ 0 < \Im(z) < 1\right\};
	\end{equation*}
	write $T_r$ (resp.\ $B_r$) for the top (resp.\ bottom) side of $V_r$; let $\Gamma_{V_r}$ be a non-nested $\CLE_4$ in $V_r$. (By convention, $V_r$ is of conformal modulus $1/r$.) Then there is a deterministic constant $c \in (0, 1)$ such that 
	\begin{equation*}
		\BP\!\left\lbrack D_{\Gamma_{V_r}}^{V_r}(T_r, B_r) \le 1/r\right\rbrack \ge c
	\end{equation*}
	for all sufficiently large $r > 0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:52:32.332917+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c300df17ce7dceb0033d8335325eb88e5aba248d9194575621f1bdb17f80701e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2609.03959, 'The combinatorial Hopf algebra of signed graphs'] Given a connected signed graph $\Sigma = (V,E, \sigma)$ and $S\subseteq V$, define $I_\Sigma(S)$ to be the set of edges $e\in E$ satisfying one of the following conditions:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\sigma(e)=+$ and the endpoints of $e$ are in $S$
\item $\sigma(e) = +$ and the endpoints of $e$ are in $V\backslash S$ 
\item $\sigma(e) = -$ and $e$ has one endpoint in $S$ and the other one in $V\backslash S$.
\end{itemize}
Then define $$\zeta_x(\Sigma) := \sum_{A \subseteq V} x^{rk(I_\Sigma(A))}. $$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.764652+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3120cea891cae44da11c7a925b1dc30402a55a328748e872fdf70047ed77689","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 56 of arXiv:2607.21374, 'Local structure at the maximum and sharp persistence asymptotics of rough fractional Brown'] For every \(\eta>0\),
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{R\to\infty}\sup_{T\geq2}
  \widehat{\PP}_T\left(
    \int_R^{T-\sigma_T}
      e^{-(M_T-B_{\sigma_T+s})}\,\mathrm ds>\eta
  \right)=0,

\end{equation}
where the integral is zero when its lower endpoint exceeds its upper
endpoint.  Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{R\to\infty}\sup_{a\geq2}
  \widehat{\PP}_a\left(
    \int_R^{a-U_a}e^{-R_a(s)}\,\mathrm ds>\eta
  \right)=0.

\end{equation}
Under \(\mu_H\),
\begin{equation}
  \int_0^\infty e^{-R(s)}\,\mathrm ds<\infty
  \quad\text{almost surely},
  \qquad
  \int_R^\infty e^{-R(s)}\,\mathrm ds\longrightarrow0

\end{equation}
almost surely and in probability.
The equivalence of the two finite-horizon formulations is the
self-similar identification \eqref{eq:long-horizon-scaling}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:11.638038+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c31bf325542493849ea8e249d982e2c6209d84c0549d0e7af66890d720b85ced","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 29 of arXiv:2605.31300, 'Cone-Additive Functions for Random Walks on Free Products of Graphs'] Assume that $\mathscr{R}>1$. Let $W$ be a weight function as above such that (\ref{eq: ass of weights}) is satisfied.
