{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.850118+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c321855f2c2ee9b3138771106f64449514805fd7816d5f1769594e74160f4e12","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 13 (\cite{IM2017}) of arXiv:2609.03869, '\bf Sharp asymptotics for a transport model with a nonlocal condition of the Fisher-KPP ty'] Assume that the Laplace transform $\widetilde f(\lambda)$  of a function $f(t)$ has an isolated pole at $\lambda_0$, with the Laurent series around $\lambda_0$:
	\begin{equation*}
		\widetilde f(\lambda)=\sum_{n=-m}^{+\infty}c_n(\lambda-\lambda_0)^n,~~~~~\lambda\in\mathbb{C}.
	\end{equation*} 
	Also assume  the existence of $\boldsymbol{\delta}< \textnormal{Re}\lambda_0$ such that  $\lambda_0$ is the only  pole of $\widetilde{f}(\lambda)$ with  $\textnormal{Re}\lambda>\boldsymbol{\delta}$ and such that 
	\begin{equation}
		\lim_{|\lambda|\to+\infty, \boldsymbol{\delta}<\textnormal{Re}\lambda}\widetilde f(\lambda)=0,~~~~	\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} |\widetilde f(\boldsymbol{\delta}+\mathbf{i} y)|\md y<+\infty,
	\end{equation}
	then, 
	\begin{align}
		f(t)&=e^{\lambda_0 t}\sum_{n=1}^mc_{-n}\frac{t^{n-1}}{(n-1)!}+\frac{1}{2\pi \mathbf{i}}\int_{\boldsymbol{\delta}-\mathbf{i}\infty}^{\boldsymbol{\delta}+\mathbf{i}\infty}e^{\lambda t}\widetilde f(\lambda)\md \lambda,~~~~~t>0.
	\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:42:47.763469+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c322834e43419d07ae058aaf10de0881aba314dbafca8ea6e299eadc6f23fabf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 9 of arXiv:2604.12334, 'On additive averaging kernels for finite Markov chains'] For any $\pi$-reversible $P$, $S \neq \mathcal{X}, \emptyset$ and $\mathcal{X} = S \sqcup S'$, we have that for any fixed $\alpha \in (0,1),$ the non-trivial set $S \subset \mathcal{X}$ that minimises $\| A_\alpha(S) - \Pi\|_{F, \pi}^2$ maximises the function 
     \begin{equation} 
         g(S) = g(S,P) := \frac{1}{\pi(S) \pi(S')} \sum_{\substack{x \in S \\ y \in S'}} \pi(x) P(x,y).
     \end{equation}

     The other direction holds as well. Concretely, we have that 
     \begin{align*}
         \argmin_{S \neq \mathcal{X}, \emptyset} \|A_\alpha(S) - \Pi\|_{F, \pi}^2 &= \argmax_{S \neq \mathcal{X}, \emptyset} g(S),\\
         \argmax_{S \neq \mathcal{X}, \emptyset} \|A_\alpha(S) - \Pi\|_{F, \pi}^2 &= \argmin_{S \neq \mathcal{X}, \emptyset} g(S).
     \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:52:51.769745+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c322ebfafa12dee0f46f9c787bb7d7f10bc77679dc072699840f5e4a9f74f02f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi eq: 2.1.3 local conv of arXiv:2605.03151, 'Giants through Higher-Order paths in Random Simplicial Complexes'] We say that a sequence of $d$-dimensional finite random simplicial complexes $(X_n)_{n \geq 1}$ \textit{converges locally weakly in probability} to a $(d-1)$-rooted random simplicial complex $(X, o)$ with (possibly random) law $\mu$, when, for every $r \geq 0$ and $H_* \in \mathcal{S}_{d-1}$,
\[  \tag{2.1.3}
\frac{1}{s_{d-1} \left( X_n \right)} \sum_{\sigma \in S_{d-1}(X_n)} \1 \left\{ B_{X_n}(\sigma; r) \cong H_* \right\} \overset{\mathbb{P}}{\to} \mu \left( B_X(o; r) \cong H_* \right).
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.758422+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c32a1bfd1ea3025254001c3426f84a830f8a0402fbfa5b2e77c1ad85b3fa047d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma claim_estimate_remainder_terms of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] For any admissible sequence of operations ${\mathcal{O}}_1,\ldots,{\mathcal{O}}_T$, there exists a constant $C>0$ that does not depend on $\varepsilon$ such that
        \begin{equation}\nonumber
            \begin{aligned}
