    \le M'.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.907426+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c347fc6f2729a12e09488cb91a0a0eb2d4621b286261a12d526662ba971b0f9b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:power_mean_loewner of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] Fix $\varepsilon>0$ and $\mathbf{X}\in\R^{d_1\times d_2}$ 
	with singular value decomposition $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{U}_{\mathbf{X}} \,\diag(\boldsymbol{\sigma})\,\mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{X}}^\top$ as in (def:optimalweightoperator of the paper). Define $
	\widetilde{\sigma}_i:= \max(\sigma_i,\varepsilon)^{-1}$ for $i \in [d]$ and $\widetilde{\sigma}_i:= \varepsilon^{-1}$ for $i \in [D] \setminus [d]$. For each $q\in[-\infty,\infty]$, 
	let $\mathbf{H}^{(q)}_{\boldsymbol{\sigma},\varepsilon}\in\R^{d_1 \times d_2}$ 
	be given entrywise for $i \in [d_1]$ and $j \in [d_2]$ by
	\begin{equation} 
	\bigl(\mathbf{H}^{(q)}_{\boldsymbol{\sigma},\varepsilon}\bigr)_{ij}
	:=
	\mathcal{M}_q(\widetilde{\sigma}_i,\widetilde{\sigma}_j),
	\end{equation}
	where $\mathcal{M}_q(\cdot,\cdot)$ denotes the $q$-power mean from (eq:def:powermean of the paper). 
	Let $\W{\mathbf{X}}{\varepsilon}^{(q)}$ be the corresponding weight operator defined as in (eq:W:operator:action of the paper), i.e., the operator mapping any $\mathbf{Z}\in\R^{d_1 \times d_2}$ to
	\begin{equation} 
	\W{\mathbf{X}}{\varepsilon}^{(q)}(\mathbf{Z})
	:=\mathbf{U}_{\mathbf{X}}\Bigl(\mathbf{H}^{(q)}_{\boldsymbol{\sigma},\varepsilon}\circ(\mathbf{U}_{\mathbf{X}}^\top \mathbf{Z}\mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{X}})\Bigr)\mathbf{V}_{\mathbf{X}}^\top.
	\end{equation}
	Then for any $-\infty\le q\le q'\le \infty$, 
	one has the Loewner ordering $
	\W{\mathbf{X}}{\varepsilon}^{(q)}  \preceq\ \W{\mathbf{X}}{\varepsilon}^{(q')}
	$ and the associated quadratic models $Q_\varepsilon^{(q)}$ and $Q_\varepsilon^{(q')}$ (cf. (eq:smoothedell1:IRLSmajorizer of the paper)) satisfy for all $\mathbf{Z}\in\R^{d_1 \times d_2}$ that
	\begin{equation} 
	Q_\varepsilon^{(q)}(\mathbf{Z}\mid\mathbf{X}) \leq Q_\varepsilon^{(q')}(\mathbf{Z}\mid\mathbf{X}).
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.570296+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c349dcd94fd9b88f739f43b29337243d33533e315fce6a7ccc464ac75b584741","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary corollary_riccati_with_source of arXiv:2606.29622, 'Affine Structure of the Brownian Signature'] Consider a Riccati equation 
    \begin{equation}
    \begin{cases}
        \bm \dot{\bm{\psi}}_t + \bm{\psi}_t|_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{0}}}} + \frac{1}{2}\, \bm \bm{\psi}_t|_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{11}}}} + \frac{1}{2}\, \bm \bm{\psi}_t|_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}}}^{\mathrel{\sqcup \mkern -3mu \sqcup} 2} + \bm{\psi}_t|_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}}}\mathrel{\sqcup \mkern -3mu \sqcup}\bm{\gamma} + \bm f = 0,\quad t \in [0, T]\\
        \bm \bm{\psi}_T = \bm{p},
    \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
    where $\bm{p}, \bm{\gamma}, \bm f \in \TA[2]$. If the coefficient 
    $$
    \widetilde\bm{p} := \bm{p} + \left(\bm f - \dfrac{1}{2}\bm{\gamma}|_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}}} - \dfrac12\bm{\gamma}^{\mathrel{\sqcup \mkern -3mu \sqcup} 2}\right){\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{0}}} + \gamma{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}} \in \mathcal{B},
    $$
    then there exists a solution $\bm{\psi}$ and $r > 0$ such that
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item For any ${\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v}}} \in V\,$, the function $t \mapsto \bm \psi_t^{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v}}}}$ is $C^1$ on $[0\,, T]\,$.
        \item For any ${\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v}}} \in V\,$, $\bm \psi_t|_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v}}}} \in \mathcal{A}_{\widetilde\bm{p}, r}$.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.337325+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c34a15e681f00ef9f9869aa3489d95ad968beff671776a8908bf82dd1f999291","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2607.14948, 'Graph alignment in sparse inhomogeneous models via self-overlap'] Let $(p_e)_{e \in E}$ be a random vector of elements of $[0,1]$. An \textbf{inhomogeneous random graph} (sampled from $(p_e)$) is a graph $K$ such that, conditional on $(p_e)_{e\in E}$, the variables $(\1_{e\in K})_{e\in E}$ are independent and satisfy
\begin{equation}
    \1_{e\in K} \sim \operatorname{Ber}(p_e).
\end{equation}

If, for $\mathbf{e} \in E$ chosen uniformly at random, the sequence of random variables $(n p_{\mathbf{e}})_{n \geq 1}$ is uniformly integrable, we say that $K$ is \textbf{weakly inhomogeneous}.","status":"unverified"}

