{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.051292+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c35de0814ee8e1e3e093999dd0a48d4729c900b3bfbeedc1695d7bce3e77d1e4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2606.16164, 'A Low-Regularity Semigroup Sewing Lemma via Quotient Structures'] Let $\tau\in[0,T]$, $\beta\in\mathbb R$, and $\gamma\in(0,1]$. Then there exists a linear map
\[
\mathcal I_\tau^{\mathrm d}: \mathcal A^{\gamma}(\Delta_\tau^2;B_\beta) \to C([0,\tau];B_\beta),
\]
obtained from a dyadic recursion on $\mathcal D^\tau$ and continuous extension to $[0,\tau]$, such that, for every $A\in \mathcal A^{\gamma}(\Delta_\tau^2;B_\beta)$, $\mathcal I_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)_0=0$. If we define the dyadic remainder $\mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A):=A-\delta\mathcal I_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)$, then
$
\delta \mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)=\delta A.
$
Moreover, if $\gamma\in(0,1)$, then
\begin{equation}

\mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)\in C^{\gamma}(\Delta_\tau^2;B_\beta)\ \text{\, and \, }\ \|\mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)\|_{\gamma,\beta;\tau}\le C_\gamma\|\delta A\|_{\gamma,\beta;\tau};
\end{equation}
if $\gamma=1$, then
\begin{equation}

\mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)\in C_{\log}^{1}(\Delta_\tau^2;B_\beta)\ \text{\, and \, }\ \|\mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)\|_{1,\log;\tau,\beta}\le C_{T}\|\delta A\|_{1,\beta;\tau}.
\end{equation}
while, for every $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$, $\|\mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)\|_{1-\varepsilon,\beta;\tau}\le C_{\varepsilon,T}\|\delta A\|_{1,\beta;\tau}$. Here the constants can be chosen independently of $\tau\in[0,T]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.789022+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c360b000461ce55f8ed95236a2e68ef62ed70010dfe51c3ecbf6e3a6bd97c259","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem E.g., Lemma 2.8 of~\cite{MaTo} of arXiv:2605.08052, 'Rapid phase ordering of Ising dynamics on $\mathbb Z^2$'] Starting from a domain $\Lambda$ with boundary conditions $\tau$, if we change the boundary conditions on a subset $\Delta \subset \partial \Lambda$ of size $|\Delta|$ to get boundary conditions $\tau^{\Delta}$, then 
    \begin{align*}
        t_{\textsc{mix}}(\Lambda, \tau) \le  4\beta |E(\Lambda)|\log 2\cdot e^{4\beta |\Delta|}\cdot  t_{\textsc{mix}}(\Lambda, \tau^\Delta)
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:41.028144+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3627c5969fab13e5ae50e16a8cf05d803d797d07e63a0604bd4937c7b9cefe1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 2-l03 of arXiv:2412.04015, 'Linear fluctuation of interfaces in Glauber-Kawasaki dynamics'] Recall the definition of $M^{p,\ell}_{k,x}$ introduced in
(equation 2-06b of the paper). There exists a finite constant ${\mathfrak c}({\boldsymbol u}^N)$,
uniformly bounded in $N$, such that
\begin{equation*}
\begin{gathered}
\sum_{k=1}^d \sum_{x\in{\mathbb T}_N^d}  M^{p, \ell}_{k,x} (\eta)^2
\;\le\; {\mathfrak c}({\boldsymbol u}^N) \, s_d(\ell)\, \frac{K}{N^2}\, 
\sum_{x\in{\mathbb T}_N^d}  H^{(\ell)}_{1,x} (\eta)^2 \;, 
\\
\frac{\sqrt{K}}{N}\,
