{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.296775+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c5fdfa0da90e7d6c41c2c8054e7bd133318743432803e324efad97df3da664ab","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thmDou2022 of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] For any $t_0\in (0,T)$, there exists a positive constant $\mathcal{C}=\mathcal{C}(t_0)$ such that for some generic constant $\gamma = \gamma(t_{0}, T) \in (0,1)$  and for any $M>0$, for all $ y_0, \hat y_0\in U_{M,1} $,
letting
\begin{align}

    \beta(\eta) =
    \mathcal{C} \eta
    +
    \mathcal{C} M^{2} \operatorname{exp}  [-  3^{-\gamma} \ln^{\gamma} (\eta^{-1}) ]
\end{align}
it holds that

\begin{equation}
| y(t_0)-\hat y(t_0)|^{2}_{L^2_{\mathcal{F}_{t_0}}(\Omega;H^{1}(G))} \leq \beta\Big( | y(T) - \hat{y}(T) |_{L^{2}_{\mathcal{F}_{T}}(\Omega; L^{2}(G))} \Big),
