where $y(\cdot)$ (resp. $\hat y(\cdot)$) is the solution to (sp-eq1 of the paper) with respect to the initial datum $y_0$ (resp. $\hat y_0$). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:42:08.473874+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c605d3e51110a1ea97ec6f2014f4987be314d4c7d0fa03faec3ee54e0272cbe6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary corollary2 of arXiv:2511.19391, 'Central limit theorem for supercritical Crump-Mode-Jagers processes counted with non-indiv'] Let $(u_{n})_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ be any admissible ordering of $\mathcal{I}$ and let $\varphi$ be an $h$-dependent descendant characteristic, for some $h \in \mathbb{N}_{0}$, satisfying (C1 of the paper)-(C2 of the paper). Then, for every fixed $t \in \mathbb{R}$, we have that, for $i \geq 2$ and $u \in \mathcal{I}$ such that $u_{i} \preceq u$ and $|u|-|u_{i}|\leq h$,
\begin{align}
\mathbb{E}[\varphi_{u}^{(h+k+|u_{i}| -|u|)}(t-S(u)) \mid \mathcal{G}_{i-1}^{(h)} ] \stackrel{a.s.}{=}  \varphi_{u}^{(h+|u_{i}| -|u|)}(t-S(u)), \quad  \text{for} \quad k=0,1.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.827199+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c608bd48c7a20d926ef0eb1603268fb29f1f05d84622f25f720ce4a2f89c703a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:conditional-limit of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Fix $k\geq3$, and take the limits first as $q\to\infty$ and then as
$h\to\infty$.  If $A$ is uniform in $\binom{[q]}k$, conditional on $A$
not being a $B_h$-set, then
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\tau(A)}h\ \Longrightarrow\ X_k,
 \qquad \mathbb P(X_k\leq t)=t^{k-2}\quad(0\leq t\leq1).
\end{equation}
Equivalently, $X_k$ has the $\operatorname{Beta}(k-2,1)$ law.  Moreover,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{|hA|}{M_{h,k}}\ \Longrightarrow\
 Y_k:=1-(1-X_k)^{k-1},
\end{equation}
whose distribution function is
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P(Y_k\leq y)=
 \left[1-(1-y)^{1/(k-1)}\right]^{k-2}
 \qquad(0\leq y\leq1).
\end{equation}
Furthermore,
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E Y_k
 =1-\frac{(k-2)!(k-1)!}{(2k-3)!}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

