{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.772824+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c617cd066d819a1de01d2713558a924b0fae7d50f57f0530f89a22c74b946763","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:EstimatesH of arXiv:2609.01464, 'Removability of non-isolated singularities for Einstein metrics and RCD spaces'] There is a symmetric matrix valued function $H$, and a positive semidefinite matrix valued Radon measure $\nu$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$ such that we have 
\begin{equation}
 H_k\to H
 \qquad\hbox{strongly in }L^q_{\mathrm{loc}}\quad\text{for every } 1< q<\frac{n}{n-1},
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
 \nu_k\rightharpoonup\nu
 \qquad\hbox{locally weakly-* as measures}.
\end{equation}
These convergences are understood in a fixed proper metric space
into which the pointed measured Gromov--Hausdorff convergence is
isometrically realized. 

Moreover, we also have 
\[
\fint_{B_1(0)}|H|\,\mathrm{d}\mu_\infty=1,
\]
\[
\fint_{B_R(0)}|H|\,\mathrm{d}\mu_\infty\leqslant 2R^{-\kappa}, \qquad \operatorname{tr}\nu(B_R(0))\leqslant C(n,\kappa)R^{n-2-\kappa}
 \qquad\forall R\geqslant1,
\]
and the equality 
\begin{equation}
   \Delta_{\mathbb R^n}H=\omega_n\nu
\end{equation}
holds distributionally on $\mathbb R^n$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

