as $n \to \infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:10.027393+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c62e83ce9a85ab887f6a092eedb3f359bc6274d7a061093a1d19724e4f4427e7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 36 of arXiv:2608.17374, '\textbf{On the Pseudo-Mixing of Kac's Walk}'] Fix $i\neq j$ and define  $q=1-\frac{2m(d-m)}{d(d-1)}$, $q_3=1-\frac{3m(d-m)}{d(d-1)}$, and $q_4=1-\frac{4m(d-m)}{d(d-1)}+\frac{4m(m-1)(d-m)(d-m-1)}{d(d-1)(d-2)(d-3)}$. For each $t\ge 0$ and any $I\subseteq[d]$ with $|I|=k$,
