{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.780782+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c69e14885c3594e59f51bcaf7de6b93eaddf7fc2ee28fa965929b7db42acb052","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Main Theorem Statement of arXiv:2411.01633, 'On the Limit of the Tridiagonal Model \\for $\beta$-Dyson Brownian Motion'] Fix a $k \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $\{a_j\}_{j=1}^n, \{b_j\}_{j=1}^{n-1}$ be the tridiagonal entry (processes) as in (equation eq:tridiagonalmatrix of the paper), from the $n\times n$ tridiagonalized GOE process. Define 
\begin{equation*}
E_n(t):=\left(a_1(t),\dots, a_k(t), \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}(b_1(t)^2-n),\dots ,\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}(b_k(t)^2-n)\right).
\end{equation*} 
Then, $E_n(t)$ converges weakly in $C[0,\infty)$ to a vector process $E(t)=(A_1(t),\dots,A_k(t),B_1(t),\dots,B_k(t))$ as $n\to\infty$, where
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}E(t) \sim \mathbf{OU}_{2k}(1,3,\dots,2k-1,2,4,\dots,2k).
\end{equation*} 
More explicitly, the components $A_j(t) \in C[0,\infty)$ and $B_j(t) \in C[0,\infty)$ are jointly-independent centered Ornstein--Uhlenbeck processes with covariances given as $\mathrm{Cov}(A_j(t),A_j(s))=2e^{-(2j-1)|t-s|}$ and $\mathrm{Cov}(B_j(t),B_j(s))=2e^{-2j|t-s|}$, for $j=1,\dots,k$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

