{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.369989+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c6ff43985704f8a4afcffaf7f039735c78b69444bd08e800b6bd3cbae01cf9f0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma last two rows are equal of arXiv:2507.10294, 'Guessing Strategies for Shelf-Shuffling Machines'] We have $p_{n-1,j}=p_{n,j}$ for all $1\leq j\leq n$.
    \begin{proof}
        We present a combinatorial argument. Card $n$ is drawn first and placed by itself. Now card $n-1$ goes on top, respectively on the bottom, of card $n$ with probability $1/2$. Hence, cards $n$ and $n-1$ move as a block in the shuffle with both orderings equally likely. Therefore, given a position, they are equally likely to end up there. One can also argue by computing the transition probabilities and using Pascal's identity and symmetry.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

