{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:15:46.817888+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c7ebdbc7f1221dcc95550c4bb77bac3f0b2f09314557c8624416127c1da690c9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[OPEN PROBLEM - not a proved fact. Stated in arXiv:2507.08909, 'Annealed almost periodic entropy' (prob environment labelled eq:tuple-of-Phis).] When $\phi$ is unital, Theorem~(thm:pre-LDP) says that the value $E_n$ gives the rate function in the large deviations principle obeyed by the random element
\[(\Phi^\pi_V)[B_n] \in \S^\rm{u}_k(B_n).\]
The values $E_n'$ should have an analogous interpretation, but this time for the random $S$-tuples
\begin{equation}
\big((\Phi^\pi_V)[B_n\cup sB_n]:\ s \in S\big)
\end{equation}
taking values in the space
\[\Big\{(Q_s:\ s \in S) \in \prod_{s \in S}\S^\rm{u}_k(B_n\cup sB_n):\ (Q_s)[B_n]\ \hbox{is the same for all $s$}\Big\}.\]
If this is true, then both inequalities in Lemma~(lem:1-2-ineqs) become instances of the contraction principle.

I believe one can prove this along the same lines as Theorem~(thm:pre-LDP) without any really new ideas, but working with the the tuples in~(eq:tuple-of-Phis) is sure to be more complicated than working with $(\Phi^\pi_V)[B_n$ alone. (Verbatim from the paper's TeX; symbols are the source paper's. Stated as open/conjectural; no resolution of it was found in the paper's own text by the rules of problem_index.py - a paper may still prove special cases of a conjecture it states.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:19:06.750711+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c7efd20ee91910f2eb6035571417a374b27d0f0bb902b1dfa4ef2e32ec34c5f5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:circle-KP-nondegeneracy-paper of arXiv:2606.08683, 'Exact Fourier dimensions of dyadic Mandelbrot cascades under minimal integrability'] Assume that \(W\ge0\) and \(\mathbb{E} W=1\). If
\begin{equation*}
