{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:45:27.984968+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c86d84723f38ff9264ec4ccd18b88eaa5872ea78be077a9c8bb9e2815ac2cc94","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:orth_tub_coord of arXiv:2607.04514, 'Boundary-layer asymptotics for Gaussian-smoothed singular measures'] Assume (ass:standing of the paper)~(ass:standing:chart of the paper). There are an open neighborhood \(\mathcal{U}\subset\mathbb R^d\) of
  \(\mathcal{K}_{\mathcal{S}}\), an open neighborhood \(\mathcal W\subset\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{out}}\times \mathbb
  R^c\times\mathbb R^k\) of the zero section over \(\mathcal{K}_{\mathcal{S}}\), and mutually inverse
  \(C^{r}\) diffeomorphisms
  \begin{equation*}
    \mathcal Z:\mathcal{U}\to\mathcal W,\ y\mapsto (x,u, \eta),
    \quad \text{and}\quad \mathcal T:\mathcal W\to\mathcal{U},\ (x,u,\eta) \mapsto y
  \end{equation*}
  of the form
  \begin{equation*}
    \mathcal Z(y)=\bigl(\pi(y),u(y),\eta(y)\bigr),
    \qquad \mathcal T(x,u,\eta)=x+\mathbf{C}(x)u+\mathbf{N}(x)\eta.
  \end{equation*}
  Here \(\pi:\mathcal{U}\to\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{out}}\), \(u:\mathcal{U}\to\mathbb R^c\), and
  \(\eta:\mathcal{U}\to\mathbb R^k\) are \(C^{r}\), and \(\pi(y) = \pi_{\mathcal S}(y)\) is the
  nearest-point projection onto \(\mathcal{S}\) in this tubular neighborhood.

  Consequently, for every \(y\in\mathcal{U}\),
  \begin{equation*}
    y = \pi(y)+\mathbf{C}(\pi(y))u(y)+\mathbf{N}(\pi(y))\eta(y), \quad \mathbf{Q}(\pi(y))^\top\bigl(y-\pi(y)\bigr)
    = [0_{m-c};u(y);\eta(y)].
  \end{equation*}
  In particular,
  \begin{equation*}
    \|y-\pi(y)\|^2=\|u(y)\|^2+\|\eta(y)\|^2
  \end{equation*}
  implying that \(u(y)=0_c\) and \(\eta(y)=0_k\) for every \(y\in\mathcal{S}\cap\mathcal{U}\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:29:21.898583+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c86eef9e58862f586e7117b37ea9e9731c923daa5ec3401827739ce89ed0cfff","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2608.00449, 'First-order phase transitions in the hard-core model'] \label[definition]{def:finitebasics}
    For a finite graph $G$ and $\lambda\ge 0$, the \emph{hard-core model on $G$ at fugacity $\lambda$} is the probability measure $\mu_{G,\lambda}$ on $\cI(G)$ such that
    \[ \mu_{G,\lambda}(I) = \frac{\lambda^{|I|}}{Z_G(\lambda)}. \]

    When the graph and fugacity are clear from context, we consider $I\sim\mu_{G,\lambda}$, identify a vertex $u$ with the indicator random variable for the event $u\in I$, and write $\overline{u}$ for the indicator of $u\notin I$. Then $\mu_{G,\lambda}(u)$ is the marginal of $u$ and $\mu_{G,\lambda}(\overline{u})=1-\mu_{G,\lambda}(u)$.

    In keeping with statistical physics terminology, we refer to the state of a vertex $u$ being in or out of an independent set as the \emph{spin} of $u$. One can think of an independent set $I\in\cI(G)$ as an assignment of spins in $\{0,1\}$ to the vertices of $G$ such that vertices in $I$ receive spin $1$ and vertices in the complement of $I$ receive spin $0$.
    For convenience when working with this formulation, we write $\Omega(G)$ for the set of functions $\sigma:V(G)\to\{0,1\}$ such that $\sigma^{-1}(1)\in\cI(G)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:02:34.840075+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c870f208a96454ad2577e6aefb1b7a719a246240bfd6fa61ff0602e688657012","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 2 of arXiv:2607.20714, 'High Minima of Gaussian Processes: Overshoots and Minimizer Locations'] The following conditions are equivalent:
\begin{align*}
 \textnormal{(i)}\quad &\mathcal E_R(\mu)>0\quad\text{for every }\mu\in\cM(K);
 &\textnormal{(ii)}\quad &\sigma_*^2>0;\\
 \textnormal{(iii)}\quad
 &\Pp\{M>u_0\}>0\quad\text{for some }u_0\geq0;
 &\textnormal{(iv)}\quad
 &\Pp\{M>u\}>0\quad\text{for every }u\in\mathbb R.
