{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.643791+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca022c2b440a51d35745de9bb00d8080f3384bb6af17ae8e59f1478cca2c36ae","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:part-fun of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] Let $\mu=\mu_n$ be such that $\mu\gg1/\sqrt{n}$. Then the partition function $Z_n$ as defined in~(equation eq:part fun of the paper) behaves according to the following regimes.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[$\mathbf{(1)}$] If $1/\sqrt{n}\ll\mu\ll n^{1/4}$, we have
        \begin{align*}
            Z_n=\big(1+o(1)\big)4^ne^\mu(e^\mu-1)\frac{\pi^{5/6}}{2^{1/3}3^{1/2}}\frac{\mu^{1/3}}{n^{5/6}}\exp\left(-3\left(\frac{\pi^2\mu^2n}{4}\right)^{1/3}\right)\,.
        \end{align*}
        \item[$\mathbf{(2)}$] If $(\log^{3/2}n)/\sqrt{n}\ll\mu\ll n$, we have
        \begin{align*}
            Z_n=\big(1+o(1)\big)4^ne^{\mu}(e^\mu-1)\frac{\mu}{2n}e^{-n\lambda_{\mu/n}(t_{\mu/n})}\sum_{m\in\mathbb Z}\exp\left(-\left(\frac{3}{2}+o(1)\right)\left(\frac{\mu^4}{2\pi^2n}\right)^{1/3}(m-t_{\mu/n})^2\right)\,.
        \end{align*}
        \item[$\mathbf{(3)}$] If $n^{1/4}\ll\mu$, we have
        \begin{align*}
            Z_n=\big(1+o(1)\big)4^ne^{2\mu}\sum_{m=\max\{\lfloor t_{\mu/n}\rfloor,3\}}^{\lceil t_{\mu/n}\rceil}\frac{\tan^2\left(\frac{\pi}{m}\right)}{m}e^{-n\lambda_{\mu/n}(m)}\,.
        \end{align*}
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.569092+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca0a7483cd6b3ae37c2c993cf67e45288021aa9f82415847e5abe6b0bd304ee6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:cox of arXiv:2412.04589, 'Inverting the Markovian projection for pure jump processes'] Let $(\lambda^k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}^*}$ be a sequence of measurable processes, satisfying $c \leq \lambda^k \leq C$ $(\mathbb{P} \otimes dt)$-a.e., $\forall\, k \in \mathbb{N}^*$, for some constants $0 < c < C$. Suppose that $\Omega$ supports a sequence of i.i.d.\ $\operatorname{Exp}(1)$ random variables $(E_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}^*}$ and a sequence of i.i.d.\ non-vanishing integrable random variables $(J_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}^*}$ such that $\mathcal{F}_\infty$, $(E_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}^*}$ and $(J_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}^*}$ are independent. Let $\tau_0 \coloneqq 0$. For $k \in \mathbb{N}^*$, inductively define the random time
	\begin{equation*}
		\tau_k \coloneqq \inf\biggl\{t > \tau_{k-1}: \int_{\tau_{k-1}}^t \lambda^k_s \,ds \geq E_k\biggr\},
	\end{equation*}
	and define the filtration $\mathbb{A}^k = (\mathcal{A}^k_t)_{t \geq 0}$ by $\mathcal{A}^k_t \coloneqq \sigma(J_k \bm{1}_{\{\tau_k \leq s\}}; s \leq t)$. Let $\mathbb{G}^0 \coloneqq \mathbb{F}$, $\mathbb{G}^k \coloneqq \mathbb{F} \newtriangledown (\newbigtriangledown_{i=1}^k \mathbb{A}^i)$ for $k \in \mathbb{N}^*$ and $\mathbb{G} \coloneqq \mathbb{F} \newtriangledown (\newbigtriangledown_{i=1}^\infty \mathbb{A}^i)$. Suppose that each $\lambda^k$ is progressively measurable with respect to $\mathbb{G}^{k-1}$. Then, the pure jump process $X_t \coloneqq \sum_{k=1}^\infty J_k \bm{1}_{\{\tau_k \leq t\}}$, $t \geq 0$, is well-defined and the process
	\begin{equation*}
		M_t \coloneqq X_t - \mathbb{E}[J_1] \int_0^t \sum_{k=1}^\infty \bm{1}_{\{\tau_{k-1} < s \leq \tau_k\}} \lambda^k_s \,ds,\quad t \geq 0,
	\end{equation*}
	is a $\mathbb{G}$-martingale.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:55:57.002451+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ca0d8c1e1dfe1d3fbb824213bb406de2f63b6b4264978da3f6411afac77e5787","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2609.02790, 'Full-Model Optimality for Tunable Linear Generative Priors in Compressed Sensing'] Let \( G_n = \operatorname{diag}(\sigma_1, \dots, \sigma_n) =\begin{pmatrix}
G_k & 0 \\
