{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.479490+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_caa393bfee521e921b546103e44051d66a061262de097f122a97eee1208401fe","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def Def_strongly_convex_3 of arXiv:2606.22183, 'Expected loss of quasi-arithmetic means of exchangeable random variables'] Let $I\subseteq \mathbb{R}$ be a nondegenerate interval and $\mu\in\mathbb{R}_+$.
A function $L:I\to\mathbb{R}$ is called strongly convex with modulus $\mu$ if
 \begin{align*}
  L(tx+(1-t)y) \leqslant t L(x) + (1-t)L(y) - \mu \cdot t (1-t) (x-y)^2,\qquad t\in[0,1], \; x,y\in I.
 \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.094471+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_caa620231663af27b9d3cd21d798da84654f2c009ea34430b345a0df79d8e2b0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi 37 of arXiv:2608.28148, 'Flows and homotopies of Banach Lie algebroids'] A map $a \colon [0,1]\times[0,1]\to A$ such that $a_\varepsilon := a(\varepsilon,\cdot)$ is an admissible curve for every $\varepsilon \in [0,1]$ is called a \emph{variation of admissible curves} if $\g_\varepsilon:=\pi\circ a_\varepsilon \colon [0,1]\rightarrow M$ satisfies\begin{itemize}
