{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:00:46.818294+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_caaaaf56bf333f278f9c506c2a2729e380761d2803849c36e737d84990fe345f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 33 of arXiv:2608.12804, 'Scaling Limits for Ising Models on Inhomogeneous Random Graphs and Applications'] Suppose that $\{X_N\}_{N\ge 1}, \{Y_N\}_{N\ge 1}$ are sequences of random variables all defined on a common probability space $\Omega$. Suppose that there exists $\delta>0$ such that for every $t\in (-\delta,\delta)$,  $$\E \left(e^{tX_N}\Big |Y_N\right) \xrightarrow{P} \E e^{tV}$$ for some random variable $V$. Then,
