 Here $\mathbb{I}$ is the identity matrix and $J$ is the counter-identity matrix. In both cases $Q^TQ=\mathbb{I}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.557682+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cb191488340f760839ab356a269f2b3f330eba1c6d1410c85c9c03694bff978c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition {\cite{MR773445}} of arXiv:2604.04882, 'On a Problem of M. Kac on Laplace Distributions'] A characteristic function $f$ is said to be \textit{geometrically infinitely divisible} if for every $p\in(0,1)$ there exists a characteristic function $g_p$ such that
