Equality in the upper bound holds if and only if $q(V)=(1/3,1/3,1/3)$ almost surely. Moreover, the value is unchanged when every dissimilarity is transformed by a common strictly increasing function; in particular, it is invariant to global metric rescaling.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.748866+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cb95d27cff87759fe337ffd6000a5ece58346b46949cf7043815dd13598474eb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 22 (Brownian functional limit in the finite-variance regime, see~{\cite[Theorem 7.4.1.) of arXiv:2607.28865, 'First-Passage Percolation on Spread-out line graphs: Microscopic Regime'] {whitt02}}]
Assume that $\ell\ge 2$ is fixed, $\go$ is not deterministic, and $\E\sfP_1^2 + \E\sfT_1^2 + \E\sfH_1^2 < \infty$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:k-moment}, this integrability condition holds if either Assumption~\textnormal{A.\ref{ass:A1}} holds (\ie\ $\E\go^2 < \infty$), or Assumption~\textnormal{A.\ref{ass:A2}} holds with $\gc > 2/\lc$. Then, in the Skorokhod space $D([0,\infty),\dR^{2+\ell})$ equipped with the $J_1$ topology, the centered joint skeleton process converges to a $(2+\ell)$-dimensional Brownian motion:
\[
    \left(
    \frac1{\sqrt n}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        S_{\sfN(nt)}^{\sfT}-nt\cdot \mu_{\sfT}/\mu_{\sfP}\\[2mm]
        S_{\sfN(nt)}^{\sfH}-nt\cdot \mu_{\sfH}/\mu_{\sfP}\\[2mm]
        \mvS_{\sfN(nt)}^{\sfJ}-nt\cdot \mvmu_{\sfJ}/\mu_{\sfP}
    \end{pmatrix}
    \right)_{t\ge 0}
    \Longrightarrow
    (\vB_t)_{t\ge0},
\]
where $\vB_\cdot$ is a centered $(2+\ell)$-dimensional Brownian motion with covariance function $\cov(\vB_s,\vB_t) = \min\{s,t\}\cdot \Sigma$, and $\Sigma$ is the $(2+\ell)$-dimensional covariance matrix from Corollary~\ref{cor:geo-stat}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.353730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cb9e2301d2224a582d4693eb34e5e9e2f09a50a97a631607e5c3ba5c3a7be563","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem.coupling.discrete of arXiv:2605.14149, 'The mean-field limit of online stochastic vector balancing'] Fix $n\in{\mathbb N}$, $\bm{x}\in{\mathbb R}^n$, and $k_0 \in \{0,1,\dots,n - 1\}$, and write $t_0=k_0/n$. Let $\bm{B}=(\bm{B}(t))_{t_0\leq t \leq 1}$ be an $n$-dimensional Brownian motion and denote its canonical filtration by $\mathbb{F}=(\mathcal{F}(t))_{t_0\leq t\leq 1}$. Also, define the filtration $\mathbb{G} = (\mathcal{G}(k))_{k=k_0,\dots,n}$ by $\mathcal{G}(k)=\mathcal{F}(\tfrac{k}{n})$. Suppose we are given random vectors $\bm{b}(k_0),\dots,\bm{b}(n-1)\in{\mathbb R}^n$ such that
    \begin{equation*}
        |\bm{b}(k)|_2\leq \frac{c_0}{\sqrt{n}},\qquad \bm{b}(k)\text{ is ${\mathcal G}(k)$-measurable,}\qquad\text{for all $k=k_0,\dots,n-1$.}
    \end{equation*}
    Then, there exist processes $\bm{Z}=(\bm{Z}(k))_{k=k_0+1,\dots,n}$ and $\varepsilon=(\varepsilon(k))_{k={k_0+1,\dots,n}}$ such that the following properties hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item The triple $(\mathbb{G}, \bm{Z}, \varepsilon)$ satisfies the assumptions of Lemma~(lem.dpp of the paper) with $\nu=\gamma$ and $k_1=n$.

        \item We have
        \begin{align} 
            \bm{b}(k) = {\mathbb E}\big[\varepsilon(k+1) \bm{Z}(k+1)\,\big|\,{\mathcal G}(k)\big], \qquad k = k_0,\dots,n-1.
        \end{align}
        In particular, we have the martingale decomposition
        \begin{align}
            \bm{Y}(k) \coloneqq \bm{x} + \sum_{\ell=k_0+1}^k \varepsilon(\ell)\bm{Z}(\ell) = \bm{x} + \sum_{\ell=k_0 + 1}^k \bm{b}(\ell - 1) + \sum_{\ell= k_0 + 1}^k \bm{\sigma}(\ell),
        \end{align}
        where $\bm{\sigma}(\ell) \coloneqq \varepsilon(\ell) \bm{Z}(\ell) - {\mathbb E}\big[\varepsilon(\ell) \bm{Z}(\ell)\,\big|\,\mathcal{G}(\ell-1)\big]$ and $k=k_0,\dots,n$.

        \item For every $p > 2$ and $k_1=k_0+1,\dots,n$,
        \begin{align}
            {\mathbb E}\bigg|\sum_{k=k_0+1}^{k_1}\bm{\sigma}(k) - \big(\bm{B}(\tfrac{k_1}{n}) - \bm{B}(\tfrac{k_0}{n})\big)\bigg|_\infty
            &\leq C_p n^{\frac14} \max_{k_0 \leq k < k_1} \bigg(\sum_{i=1}^n \|b_i(k)\|_p^p\bigg)^{\frac{1}{2p}}, 
        \end{align}
        where $C_p>0$ is a constant that depends only on $p$.

        \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:18:19.413441+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cba8f44b04a36ae95f1e5b81c145e27327b53316b5e1be263ae4545ded2a3036","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:qRacah_ensemble_and_tilings of arXiv:2507.22011, 'Random Lozenge Waterfall:\\Dimensional Collapse of Gibbs Measures'] {borodin-gr2009q} and [dimitrov2019log]}]

	Fix $T,S,N$. In the zones (equation eq:hexagon_zone_1 of the paper) and (equation eq:hexagon_zone_2 of the paper), the distribution of $X(t)$ (equation eq:nonintersecting_path_ensemble_X_t_defn of the paper) under the $q$-Racah measure ((def:qRacah_tilings of the paper)) is given by the $N$-particle $q$-Racah OPE on $\{0,1,\ldots,M\}$. In the zones (equation eq:hexagon_zone_3 of the paper) and (equation eq:hexagon_zone_4 of the paper), the distribution of the shifted configuration
