{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:57:16.068379+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbfb585b262732b1599ade780c8014ad8f158c524acacb43ba774add6f221b65","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:unitary of arXiv:2506.18673, 'Asymptotic Expansions of Gaussian and Laguerre Ensembles at the Soft Edge III: Generating '] There holds, for any fixed non-negative integer $m \leq m_*$,\footnote{We restrict to $m\leq m_*$ for two {\em independent} reasons: Step 1 of the proof relies on a verification that the kernel expansion yields certain finite-rank correction kernels, while Step 3 hinges on the solvability of certain over\-determined linear systems over ${\mathbb Q}[s]$; both issues can currently be settled only by an explicit casewise inspection (however, a Riemann--Hilbert analysis, as in [arXiv:2309.06733], would probably dispense with the first one). Using a computer algebra system, we have confirmed the necessary conditions for all $m\leq 10$.}
\begin{subequations}
\begin{equation}
E_{2,n}(x;\xi) |_{x=\mu_{n} + \sigma_{n} s} = F_2(s;\xi) + \sum_{j=1}^m G_{2,j}(s,\tau_{n};\xi)h_{n}^j + h_{n}^{m+1}O\bigl({\rm e}^{-2s}\bigr),\qquad n\to\infty,
\end{equation}
uniformly for $0\leq \xi \leq 1$ while $s$ stays bounded from below and, in the Laguerre case, $\tau_n$~stays bounded away from zero -- the Gaussian case being encoded with $\tau_n=0$. Preserving uniformity, the expansion can be repeatedly differentiated with respect to the variables $s$ and $\xi$.
The leading term is given by the Airy kernel determinant\/\footnote{For $n\to\infty$, and a fixed Laguerre parameter $\alpha = p-n>-1$ in the Laguerre case, the pointwise limit
\[
\lim_{n\to \infty} E_{2,n}(\mu_{n} + \sigma_{n} s;\xi) = \det(I- \xi K_{\operatorname{Ai}}) |_{L^2(s,\infty)}
\]
was established by Forrester [MR1236195]; see also [MR2641363]. $\operatorname{Ai}$ is the standard Airy function [MR2723248].}
\begin{equation}
F_2(s;\xi) = \det(I- \xi K_{\operatorname{Ai}}) |_{L^2(s,\infty)},\qquad K_{\operatorname{Ai}}(x,y) = \frac{\operatorname{Ai}(x)\operatorname{Ai}'(y)-\operatorname{Ai}'(x)\operatorname{Ai}(y)}{x-y},
\end{equation}
and the correction terms take the multilinear form
\begin{equation}
G_{2,j}(s,\tau;\xi) = \sum_{k=1}^{2j} P_{2,j,k}(s,\tau)\cdot \partial_s^k F_2(s;\xi)
\end{equation}
\end{subequations}
with certain polynomials $P_{2,j,k} \in {\mathbb Q}[s,\tau]$ that are independent of the dummy variable~$\xi$ -- the concrete instances up to $j=4$ are displayed in Table~{\rm (tab:1 of the paper)}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:51:44.767771+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cbfe7fa95d1bddd4124c971b7889568a54d1143e96ee4fc340165b4560ad62f1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Ingredient 19 of arXiv:2608.26803, 'On bubbling of Fueter maps'] Let $(X, g_X, I_1, I_2, I_3)$ be a closed hyperk\"ahler manifold. Fix a real codimension $2$ submanifold $Z \subset X$ and a unit vector field $v \in \Gamma(\mathbb{S}TM)$. For $p>1$ and $m, k$ positive integers satisfying $k-3/p>2$ and $m \geq k+1$ the \textbf{universal space of Fueter maps with $n$ incidence conditions}
    \begin{equation*}
        \cN^m_n(v, Z) \coloneq
        \Bigg\{ (f, \bx, H ) \in W^{k, p}(M, X) \times \Conf_n (M) \times \cP^m \ \Bigg|\
        \begin{array}{l}
            \cF_H f = 0 \, \text{ and for } \bx = (x_1 , \dots, x_n)\\
            f(x_j) \in Z \text{ and } T_{x_j}f(v) \in T_{f(x_j)}Z \\
            \text{for all } j= 1, \dots, n
        \end{array}
        \Bigg\}.
    \end{equation*}
    is a $C^1$-Banach manifold. Moreover, the natural projection map
    \begin{equation*}
        \pi^m: \cN^m_n (v, Z) \to \cP^m: \quad (f, \bx, H ) \mapsto H
    \end{equation*}
    is Fredholm of index $-n$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:02:28.587756+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cc01abe01863d12168668e71f54956d3bf614c4e86f6e29864851a4edfc705c2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2609.01346, '2609.01346'] Suppose a solution of \eqref{eq:profile-system} approaches, as $s\to s_0$,
\[
 (x,r,\theta)\longrightarrow(x_0,r_0,\theta_0),
 \qquad r_0>0,
 \qquad \theta_0\in\{\pi/2,-\pi/2\},
\]
and assume
\begin{equation}
 x_0\sin\theta_0\neq0.
\end{equation}
Then the solution has a unique local continuation through $s_0$ in the class of
nondegenerate crossings.  If $u=(\theta-\theta_0)^k$, then $u$ is strictly
monotone near $s_0$, and
\begin{equation}
 \theta(s)-\theta_0
 \sim
 \left(\frac{k r_0^{k-1}x_0\sin\theta_0}{A}\right)^{1/k}
 (s-s_0)^{1/k},
\end{equation}
where the real $k$-th root is understood.  In particular,
\begin{align}
 x(s)-x_0&=O(|s-s_0|^{(k+1)/k}),\\
 r(s)-r_0&=O(|s-s_0|),\\
 \left|\frac{d\theta}{ds}(s)\right|&\sim
 \frac1k\left|\frac{k r_0^{k-1}x_0\sin\theta_0}{A}\right|^{1/k}
 |s-s_0|^{-(k-1)/k}.
\end{align}
Writing $\gamma=(x,r)$, there are constants $C>0$ and $\eta>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \left|
 \frac{d\gamma}{ds}(s)-\frac{d\gamma}{ds}(t)
 \right|
 \leq C|s-t|^{1/k}
 \qquad
 \text{whenever }|s-s_0|<\eta\text{ and }|t-s_0|<\eta.
\end{equation}
Consequently the profile curve is $C^{1,1/k}$ near the crossing, and the
exponent $1/k$ is optimal.  It is also locally $W^{2,p}$ for every
$1\leq p<k/(k-1)$, but it is not $W^{2,k/(k-1)}$ at the crossing.","status":"unverified"}

