{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.472768+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cc263c6e0b7b7a2fd751af86aa89b77ac99b842d66ad9e8d4c8e65ea5dd02a60","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:lowerboundpt of arXiv:2505.12187, 'Speeding up quantum Markov processes through lifting'] Under the same assumptions as in (thm:flowpoincare of the paper), it holds that for any observation period $T > 0$, and the initial condition $x_0 \in \mathcal{F}^\perp$, 
\begin{align} 
    \frac{1}{T}\int_t^{t + T} \lVertP_s x_0\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2 \,\mathrm{d} s \le e^{- 2\nu t} \lVertx_0\rVert_{\mathcal{H}}^2\,,
\end{align}
with the convergence rate: 
\begin{equation} 
    \nu = \frac{\gamma}{\gamma^2 C_0(T) + C_1(T)}\,,
\end{equation}
where $C_0(T)$ and $C_1(T)$ are the flow Poincar\'e constants in (thm:flowpoincare of the paper). 
Moreover, the convergence rate estimate (equation eq:ratel2 of the paper) is maximized at 
\begin{align} 
    \gamma_{\rm max} = \sqrt{C_1(T)/C_0(T)} \quad \text{with rate}\quad \nu_{\max} = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{C_0(T) C_1(T)}}\,.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.074750+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cc33c6bcae78e63cf17d051ca02b72fb9cffeee6fdb8eb80b4c84e3a0c68306f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:erm-fragile of arXiv:2608.20480, 'When Clean Data Hurts: Learning with Monotone \\ Corruptions Beyond Binary Classification'] There is a countable, closed class $\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{frag}}$ of finite coordinatewise range, with $d_{\mathrm{DS}}(\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{frag}})=1$, that is ordinarily PAC learnable by a proper ERM.  Nevertheless, for every fixed $\alpha>0$ and every possibly randomized ERM rule $A$, an oblivious adversary using at most $\alpha n$ additions can force
\[
    \liminf_{n\to\infty}
    \sup_{D,h^\star,\mathsf{Adv}}
    \mathbb{E} L_D(A(T),h^\star)
    \geq
    c_\alpha>0,
\]
where the supremum ranges over oblivious adversaries of budget at most $\alpha n$, and $c_\alpha$ may be taken of order $e^{-1/\alpha}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:47:40.731953+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cc4d2c615e8d966c60410a87432c6dd256e67ce9af44f239e4e6878bf5172fc7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:free-energy-spiked of arXiv:2604.20008, 'Mixing times of Langevin dynamics for \\ spiked matrix models'] Fix $\theta>1$ and let $ \alpha >0$. The free energy of the spiked matrix model is given by:
\begin{align} 
F(\alpha,\theta) := \lim_{N \to \infty}  \frac{1}{N} \log \int_{\mathbf{S}_N} e^{\beta H_N(x)} dx = 
\begin{cases}
    \frac{\alpha^2}{4\theta^2} &\text{if} \ \alpha <1 \\
 \frac{\alpha}{2\theta} (\theta + \frac{1}{\theta}) - \frac{1}{4\theta^2} - \frac{1}{2} \log(\alpha) - \frac{1}{2}         &\text{else} 
\end{cases} \, ,
\end{align}
where the limit exists $\mathbb{P}_M$ almost surely. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:10:05.676641+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cc506358d401ba8485c2e4c484d9f9abaa66f7f5581878083fb5c1b1791c290b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2608.16752, 'Absorption Probabilities for Random Convex Hulls: Distribution-Freeness via the Wall-Cross'] For integers $n,d\geq 1$ we have
$$
\sum_{\ell=1}^{n}
\stirling{n}{\ell}\,
2^{\,n-\ell}
\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}
\binom{\ell-1}{j}
=
\sum_{q\geq 0}
\stirlingb{n}{d-1-2q},
$$
where the numbers $\stirlingb{n}{k}$ with $n\in \{1,2,\ldots\}$, $k\in \mathbb Z$,  are defined by the generating function
$$
\sum_{k\in \mathbb Z} \stirlingb{n}{k} t^k
=
(t+1)(t+3)\cdots(t+2n-1).
$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.370913+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cc50fd59fe97d3f5492353f9c15d85361078d89eb3be4aca439b1ab415e48cfb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 of arXiv:2606.03661, '2606.03661'] Let $\alpha \in (0,1)$, $\nu \in \mathbb{R}$ and $m \in \mathbb{N}$. Then, the function $\Psi_\alpha^\nu(-\kappa t)$ admits the asymptotic expansion
\begin{equation}

\Psi_\alpha^\nu(-\kappa t) =  -\frac{G(1+\nu)}{G(2+\nu-\alpha)}
\sum_{k=1}^m \rho_k  
(\kappa t)^{-(\nu+k)} 
 + \mathcal{O}\left(|\kappa t|^{-(\nu+m+1/2)}\right),
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}

\rho_k = \underset{\varepsilon=0}{\operatorname{Res}} \left[\frac{(\kappa t)^{-\varepsilon} \Gamma(\nu+k+\varepsilon)G(2-\alpha-k-\varepsilon)}{  G(1-k-\varepsilon)} \right], \qquad (k = 1,2,\ldots,m).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.714438+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cc562220532fca8db64ab237b5344ba727d9402492fd38adfbfe1cbdfacf97e9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:comparaison_esperances of arXiv:2605.30999, 'Functional central limit theorems for $U$-statistics of $\beta$-mixing data'] Let $\left(X_j\right)_{j\geqslant 1}$ be a strictly stationary sequence of random variables taking values in a measurable space $\pr{S,\mathcal{S}}$. Let $f\colon S^m\to \mathbb R$ be a measurable function, where $S^m$ is endowed with the product $\sigma$-algebra. 
Let $\left(m\left(u,v\right)\right)_{\substack{1\leqslant u\leqslant \ell\\ 1\leqslant v\leqslant r_u}}$ be integers such that 
\begin{equation*}
    m\left(1,1\right)<\dots<m\left(1,r_1\right)<m\left(2,1\right)<\dots< m\left(2,r_2\right)<\dots< m\left(\ell,1\right)<\dots<m\left(\ell,r_\ell\right).
\end{equation*}
Let $r=\sum_{u=1}^\ell r_u$. Let $\left(\xi_j\right)_{j=1}^r$ be a sequence of random variables such that for each $j$, $\xi_j$ has the same distribution as $X_1$ and 
\begin{multline*}
    \mathcal{L}\pr{\pr{\xi_{m\left(1,1\right)},\dots,\xi_{m\left(1,r_1\right)},\xi_{m\left(2,1\right)},
\dots,\xi_{m\left(2,r_2\right)},\dots,\xi_{m\left(\ell,1\right)},\dots,\xi_{m\left(\ell,r_\ell\right)}  }}\\
=\mathcal{L}\pr{X_{m\left(1,1\right)},\dots,X_{m\left(1,r_1\right)} }
\otimes \dots\otimes \mathcal{L}\pr{X_{m\left(\ell,1\right)},\dots,X_{m\left(\ell,r_\ell\right)} }.
\end{multline*}
Suppose that there exists a constant $R$ such that for each $x_1,\dots,x_m\in S$,  $\left|f\left(x_1,\dots,x_m\right)\right|\leqslant R$.
Then the following inequality takes place
\begin{multline}
    \abs{\E{f\pr{X_{m\left(1,1\right)},\dots,X_{m\left(\ell,r_\ell\right)} }}-
    \E{f\pr{\xi_{m\left(1,1\right)},\dots,\xi_{m\left(\ell,r_\ell\right)} }}}
\\    \leqslant 2\sum_{i=1}^{\ell-1}
    \beta\left(m\left(i+1,1\right)-m\left(i,r_i\right)\right)R.
\end{multline} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:31:14.333160+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cc568ebf53edec744551bdda06445dfa48c77cd5ba0e3bb201e52729cd79e94a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma circular_law of arXiv:2606.16609, 'On stability of outliers from the circular law'] Let $\{ \lambda_1, \dots, \lambda_N \}$ the spectrum of a complex Ginibre matrix conditioned on $\{ \lambda_1 = z \}$ with fixed $z \in \mathbb{C}$; almost surely, the empirical spectral distribution
$$
