{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.631739+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ccba57f3f171955230c381d835cb502788da11ea155c850c6979b9fbcbcc7c98","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:lerw of arXiv:2411.13505, 'Capacity of loop-erased random walk'] Let $\omega\in\Omega$ be a one-sided nearest-neighbour, transient path in $\mathbb{Z}^d$. The loop-erasure $\LE(\omega)$ of $\omega$ is the path obtained by erasing loops from $\omega$ chronologically. To be precise, $\LE(\omega)_i=\omega_{\ell_i}$, where the times $(\ell_i)_{i\geq0}$ are defined inductively as $\ell_0=0$ and $\ell_{i+1}=1+\max\{k\colon\omega_k=\omega_{\ell_i}\}$. When $S$ is a simple random walk on $\mathbb{Z}^d$ for $d\geq3$, we write
\begin{equation}
	\eta:=\LE(S[0,\infty)).
\end{equation}
This is well-defined, since simple random walk is almost surely transient for $d\geq3$.","status":"unverified"}

