{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.423722+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ccd8b9a061391d2ef6127169ef8801f0d68892af74c34326df6c797fc73d1e0a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary eq:growing-L-pressure of arXiv:2607.27613, 'Regularized Bulk Universality versus Bounded-Disorder Nonuniversality\\for Annealed Comple'] \label[corollary]{cor:growing-cutoff-regimes}
In the setting of (thm:growing-point-cutoff of the paper), suppose
\[
 r_N=L_N\sqrt{\frac{\log N}{N}},
 \qquad L_N\longrightarrow\infty,
 \qquad r_N\le1.
\]
Then the cutoff is Haar-full.  Pressure universality holds if
\begin{equation}
 L_N=o\left(
 \frac{N^{(p-2)/(2(p-1))}}{\sqrt{\log N}}
 \right),
\end{equation}
and the expectation-ratio conclusion holds if
\begin{equation}
 L_N=o\left(
 \frac{
 N^{\frac{p-2}{2(p-1)}
 -\frac1{(m-1)(p-1)}}}
 {\sqrt{\log N}}
 \right).
\end{equation}
The latter permits a growing \(L_N\) precisely when
\((m-1)(p-2)>2\).

Equivalently, for \(r_N=N^{-a}\), the cutoff is Haar-full and pressure
universal whenever
\begin{equation}
 \frac1{2(p-1)}<a<\frac12.
\end{equation}
It is Haar-full and satisfies the ratio conclusion whenever
\begin{equation}
 \frac{m+1}{2(m-1)(p-1)}<a<\frac12.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

