{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:15:16.652472+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdbb46f302d9237b86caf7652a37721e194d383a36ec5ea916abdcde4ae9c3c7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 3 of arXiv:2608.30752, '2608.30752'] Let \(u_0:\Sigma\to S\) be an orientation-preserving covering map
between closed surfaces, and assume that \(u_0\) satisfies the filling
hypothesis. Let \(X_g\in\mathcal T(\Sigma)\) be the conformal structure
determined by the fixed domain metric \(g\), and regard \(u_0^*\Delta_q\) as
the pullback measured foliation on \(\Sigma\). Then
\begin{equation}

  \mathscr E_{\Delta_q}(u_0)
  =
  \frac12\,
  \operatorname{Ext}_{X_g}\!\left(u_0^*\Delta_q\right),
\end{equation}
and consequently
\begin{equation}

  \lim_{t\to\infty}
  \frac{\mathcal E_{u_0}(X_t)}{t^2}
  =
  8\,\operatorname{Ext}_{X_g}\!\left(u_0^*\Delta_q\right).
\end{equation}

If moreover, \(u_0\) has degree \(d\) and \(X_g=u_0^*X_0\), then
\begin{equation}

  \lim_{t\to\infty}
  \frac{\mathcal E_{u_0}(X_t)}{t^2}
  =
  8d\,\operatorname{Ext}_{X_0}(\Delta_q).
\end{equation}
Equivalently, let
\(\Phi_{\Delta_q,X_0}\) be the Hubbard--Masur differential whose vertical
measured foliation is \(\Delta_q\), and let \(M_i\) be the conformal modulus
of its characteristic cylinder with core curve \(\delta_i\). Then
\begin{equation}

  \lim_{t\to\infty}
  \frac{\mathcal E_{u_0}(X_t)}{t^2}
  =
{8d}  \sum_{i=1}^{r}\frac{1}
  {M_i}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.913372+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdc17c77416a517a8ed2ae665add4d0b5f708c56d5be284e42a3ab384094f7c7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:FK theorem of arXiv:2411.00663, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of invariant capacities and applications'] Let $\Phi$ be defined, by (equation eq:star of the paper), on the capacity preserving system $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, V,T)$, where $V$ is an upper probability.  If the generator $L:\Omega\to \mathbb{R}^{d\times d}$ of $\Phi$ is a measurable function such that $\log\|L(\omega)\|\in B(\Omega,\mathcal{F})$, then 
