{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.943658+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdddc984e4aecc3d419dc240aa61e8944726ee1e6528e4493dd12719c32b77b7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor limit_shape of arXiv:2506.12171, 'The multinomial dimer model'] (Limit shape.) 
Fix $\Lambda$ and $(R,b)$ as in Theorem~(thm:ldp of the paper). First, the rate function $I$ has a unique minimizer $\omega_{\min}\in \text{AF}(\Lambda,R,b)$. Second, given $\varepsilon>0$ define the event 
    \begin{align*}
        V_\varepsilon = \{ \omega: d_W(\omega_{\min},\omega)>\varepsilon\}. 
    \end{align*}
    For any sequence of multinomial measures $\rho_{n,N}$ as in Theorem (thm:ldp of the paper), dimer covers sampled from $\rho_{n,N}$ concentrate exponentially fast on $\omega_{\min}$ in the iterated limit as $N$ and then $n$ go to infinity. In other words there is a constant $C>0$ determined by $(R,b)$ such for $n,N$ sufficiently large,
    \begin{align}
        \rho_{n,N}(V_\varepsilon) < C^{-Nn^d}. 
    \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

