{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.859373+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce6aeab2f7def6360399f0adf52ee26574e53ac2c7c1d4b9f6f4ff30a5a35156","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor pers-det-trees of arXiv:2410.24170, 'Persistent hubs in CMJ branching processes with independent increments and preferential at'] Suppose that $(\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$ is a generalised preferential attachment tree, with deterministic values $(f(j))_{j \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\sum_{j=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{f(j)^2} = \infty$, then $(\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$ almost surely does not have a persistent hub.
\item  If $\sum_{j=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{f(j)^2} < \infty$ and for some $\lambda > 0$, 
\begin{equation} 
\sum_{i=0}^{\infty} \prod_{j=0}^{i}\frac{f(j)}{f(j) + \lambda} < \infty,
\end{equation} 
then $(\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$ has a unique persistent hub almost surely. 
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

