{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:41:19.331118+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce93454e2a8d82909b75af7afe29db2f9a4d7d80f8821a2e849a7b83473669e0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:frac_per_bound of arXiv:2609.04045, 'Heat content and spectrum for subordinated sub-Laplacians'] Let $\Omega$ be any measurable subset of $\mathbb{G}$. Then for all $0<\alpha<1$,
    \begin{align}
        \operatorname{Per}^{(\alpha)}_{\mathcal H}(\Omega)\leqslant C_\alpha \operatorname{Vol}(\Omega)^{1-\alpha} \operatorname{Per}_{\mathcal H}(\Omega)^\alpha
    \end{align}
    with 
    \begin{align*}
        C_\alpha=\frac{\left(2Q\right)^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}}{(1-\alpha)\Gamma(1-\frac{\alpha}{2})} ,
    \end{align*}
    where $Q$ is the homogeneous dimension of $\mathbb G$, and $m$ is the dimension of the first layer of the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}$.
     As a result, $\operatorname{Per}^{(\alpha)}_{\mathcal H}(\Omega)<\infty$ for all $\alpha\in (0,1)$ whenever $\operatorname{Vol}(\Omega)<\infty$ and $\operatorname{Per}_{\mathcal H}(\Omega)<\infty$. In particular, when $\Omega$ is bounded with a $C^1$ boundary, $\operatorname{Per}^{(\alpha)}_{\mathcal H}(\Omega)<\infty$ for all $\alpha\in (0,1)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.583084+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce9882b6d592a1c8fe7d388723504e261de8efe03f864d4eb9af8090d197b1fd","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi def:cpls of arXiv:2509.08659, 'Gap metrics for stationary point processes and quantitative convexity of the free energy'] For two stationary probability measures $\Pi_i\in \mathcal P_{s,1}( \mathsf{S})$, $i=0,1$ we denote by $ \mathsf{Cpl}_s(\Pi_0,\Pi_1)$ the set of all couplings of $\Pi_0$ and $\Pi_1$, which are invariant under the diagonal shifts 
