Furthermore,  \eqref{eq:GoodControlASEP} continues to hold when $(\xi_t)_{t\geq 0}$ is defined the as a full-space extended colored ASEP with reference frame $\mathcal{I}^{\rho}_{N}\big(\theta,1+\theta\big)$ for two full-space ASEPs, i.e., we obtain $(\xi_t)_{t\geq 0}$ as in Definition~\ref{def:ExtendedCouplingASEP} with respect to two ASEPs on the integers, which evolve under the (extended) basic coupling along edges in $\mathcal{I}^{\rho}_{N}\big(\theta,1+\theta\big)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:57:16.072585+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf0da31ab1a15518169e2520d00bc05f667dbe4fcfb39ef52e4100a1f3915c35","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:CPRE-BDS of arXiv:2507.00329, 'Oriented bond-site percolation in random environment and contact processes with periodic r'] Let $\varDelta>0$ be a random variable, with $\mathbb{E}[\varDelta^{1+\varepsilon}]<\infty$ for some $\varepsilon>0$, and fix $\delta>0$ and $p\in(0,1)$. Let $(\varDelta_x)_{x\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq0}}$ be an i.i.d.\ family of random variables distributed according to $\mathcal{L}(\varDelta)$, the law of $\varDelta$. Consider the generalised contact process on $\mathbb{Z}_{\geq0}$ with $\mathbb{X}\sim\mathrm{PPP}(1)$ and recovery times  $(\mathbb{Y}_x)_{x\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq0}}$ given by independent copies of the following Cox point processes:
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item With probability $p$ (independent of everything else), let $\mathbb{Y}_x\sim\mathrm{PPP}(\delta)$.  
        \item Otherwise, let $\mathbb{Y}_x\sim\mathrm{PPP}(\varDelta_x)$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, there exists some $\delta_c:=\delta_c(p,\mathcal L(\varDelta))>0$ such that, for every $\delta<\delta_c$, the generalised contact process has a positive probability of survival. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.054131+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf122dde80793c51d198f9c3fa697310d805e5fe2c203a3a663088adf6d30a7e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma stirling_multinom of arXiv:2607.13302, 'Extending an Order-Statistic Characterization of the Exponential Distribution'] For Stirling numbers of the second kind it holds that
