{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.343529+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf7a710fac88691121e0267d62ebfc53d93be6c8795610881d4d3bf5a0f77e99","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 22 of arXiv:2607.14948, 'Graph alignment in sparse inhomogeneous models via self-overlap'] Let $\eps, \eta > 0$; then, the following holds with high probability. For any $\sigma \in \Sym_n$, for any $X \subseteq \Der \sigma$ with size at least $\eps n$, 

    \begin{equation}
        \left|(K \land_\sigma K)\left[X \leftrightarrow (X \sqcup \Fix \sigma)\right]\right| \leq \left( \frac{1}{2 \alpha -1} + \eta \right) |X|.
    \end{equation}
    ie. the number of edges with one endpoint in $X$ and the other endpoint within $X \cup \Fix \sigma$ is bounded by $\left( \frac{1}{2 \alpha -1} + \eta \right) |X|$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.779688+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf7aac9abb8b90f4df66e3325bde5f09ece12152351a0c9d080d9ab399ff2682","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn Ornstein-Uhlenbeck Process of arXiv:2411.01633, 'On the Limit of the Tridiagonal Model \\for $\beta$-Dyson Brownian Motion'] The normalized stationary scalar Ornstein--Uhlenbeck process $\mathrm{OU}(c)$, is the unique solution to
    \begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
        &dx(t)=-m x(t)\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{t} +\sqrt{2m} \mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}{W(t)} \\
        &x(0) \sim N(0,1)
    \end{split}
\end{equation}
for a standard one dimensional Brownian motion $W(t)$. Or equivalently, $\mathrm{OU}(m)$ is a stationary Gaussian process for all time with covariances given by $\mathbb{E}[x(t)x(s)]=e^{-m|t-s|}$. Furthermore if $m=(m_1,...,m_n)$ denote by $\mathbf{OU}_n(m)$ the vector valued Ornstein--Uhlenbeck process that has independent $\mathrm{OU}(m_i)$ entries. If $c$ is scalar, the notation $\mathbf{OU}_n(m)$ will be short for $\mathbf{OU}_n((m,...,m))$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:02:11.269572+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cf8ee3a86d5ba0de50dc649488d2b4a78b1cc876725fda91da6366c95b00f69d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:clt-rw2mc of arXiv:2509.10225, 'Phase Transitions for Elephant Random Walks \\ with Two memory Channels'] The fluctuations of the walk around the almost sure limit exhibit the following central limit behavior based on the value of $p$. 
\begin{enumerate}
\item[a)] For $p \in \left(0, \frac{11}{16}\right)\cup\left(\frac{113+\sqrt{97}}{128}, 1\right)$,
\begin{align}
    \sqrt{n}\left(\frac{{S}_n}{{n}} - \Lambda_p\right) \stackrel{d}{\to} N\left(0,\Sigma^{(1)}_p\right),
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
   \Sigma^{(1)}_p = &{\begin{cases}{\frac{2}{11-16p}}, &{p \in \left(0, \frac{11}{16}\right)}, \\[.1cm] \Sigma^{(1)}_{\alpha, \beta, p}, &{p \in \left(\frac{113+\sqrt{97}}{128}, 1\right)},\end{cases}}
\end{align}
with $\alpha, \beta$ given by (equation eq:al-be of the paper) and the random variable $\Sigma^{(1)}_{\alpha, \beta, p}$ given by (equation eq:sig1 of the paper).
\item[b)] For $p \in \left\{\frac{11}{16}, \frac{113+\sqrt{97}}{128}\right\}$,
\begin{align}
    \sqrt{\frac{n}{\log n}}\left(\frac{{S}_n}{{n}} - \Lambda_p\right) \stackrel{d}{\to} N\left(0,\Sigma^{(2)}_p\right),
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
   \Sigma^{(2)}_p = &{\begin{cases}{\frac{2}{3}}, &{p = \frac{11}{16}}, \\[.1cm] \Sigma^{(2)}_{\alpha, \beta, p}, &{p = \frac{113+\sqrt{97}}{128}}.\end{cases}}
\end{align}
with $\alpha, \beta$ given by (equation eq:al-be of the paper) and the random variable $\Sigma^{(2)}_{\alpha, \beta, p}$ given by (equation eq:sig2 of the paper).
\item[c)] For $p \in \left(\frac{11}{16}, \frac{7}{8}\right)\cup\left(\frac{7}{8}, \frac{113+\sqrt{97}}{128}\right)$,
\begin{align}
    n^{y_p}\left(\frac{{S}_n}{{n}} - \Lambda_p\right) \stackrel{a.s.}{\to} L_{p},
