{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.782805+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cfecd06f97f1e3607fce243e02ddbbb1a76c4ee3b4a539fa819048025cb2370c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Coro of arXiv:2411.19543, 'Feller property and convergence for semigroups of time-changed processes'] Under assumptions of Theorem (Approximation of the paper), let $v\in C_0(F)$ and  let $u_\infty$ be the mild solution of the heat equation
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases} 
		- \frac{\partial u_\infty(t)}{\partial t} = \check{L}^\infty u_\infty(t),\\
		u_\infty(0) = v
\end{cases}

\end{equation}
and for each $n\in\mathbb{N}$  let $u_n$ be the mild solution of the  heat equation
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases} 
		- \frac{\partial u_n(t)}{\partial t} = \check{L}^n u_n(t),\\
		u_n(0) =P_Fv|_{F_n}.
\end{cases}

\end{equation}
Then 
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\rm(1)]
\[
\lim_{n\to\infty}\|u_n(t) - P_F u_\infty(t) \|_{\infty,F_n} = 0
\]
locally uniformly in $t\in [0,\infty)$.
\item[\rm(2)] If moreover  $F_n\subset F$ for all $n$ then 
\[
\lim_{n\to\infty}\| u_n(t) -  u_\infty(t) \|_{\infty,F_n} = 0
\]
locally uniformly in $t\in [0,\infty)$.
\item[\rm(3)] Whereas if $F\subset F_n$ for all $n$ then 
\[
\lim_{n\to\infty}\| u_n(t) -  u_\infty(t) \|_{\infty,F} = 0
\]
locally uniformly in $t\in [0,\infty)$.
\end{enumerate}
Here  $\|\cdot\|_{\infty,F}$, $\|\cdot\|_{\infty,F_n}$ denotes the supremum respectivly  on $F$ and $F_n$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.058911+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cff20f04d4e8174edb7807f4497731046ecf57a34ecfc407177e024bcc49ea9e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:multiclass-lb of arXiv:2608.20480, 'When Clean Data Hurts: Learning with Monotone \\ Corruptions Beyond Binary Classification'] There exist countable discrete spaces $\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y}$ and a countable
    hypothesis class $\mathcal{H}\subseteq\mathcal{Y}^\mathcal{X}$ such that:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \textbf{Clean proper learning.}
        There is a deterministic proper learner $\mathcal{L}$ such that for every
        $(\varepsilon,\delta)\in(0,1)^2$, distribution $\mathcal{D}$ over $\mathcal{X}$, and
        target hypothesis $h^\star\in\mathcal{H}$, with probability at least
        $1-\delta$ over
        $C=\{(x_i,y_i)\}_{i\in[n]}$, where $x_1,\ldots,x_n$ are drawn
        i.i.d.\ from $\mathcal{D}$ and $y_i=h^\star(x_i)$ for every $i$, it holds
        that
        $\operatorname{err}_{\mathcal{D},h^\star}(\mathcal{L}(C))\le\varepsilon$ whenever
        \begin{align*}
            n
            \ge
            100\cdot
            \frac{
                1+\log(1/\varepsilon)+\log(1/\delta)
            }{\varepsilon}.
        \end{align*}

        \item \textbf{Every learner foiled by some monotone adversary.}
        For every learner $\mathcal{L}$, possibly improper and randomized, and
        every $n$, there are a distribution $\mathcal{D}$, a target hypothesis
        $h^\star\in\mathcal{H}$, and a monotone adversary $\mathcal{A}$ that uses $n$
        clean and $m=n$ adversarial samples such that
        \begin{align*}
            \mathbb{E}_{S\sim
                \mathtt{Adv}_{\mathcal{D},h^\star,\mathcal{A}}(n,m)}
            \left[
                \operatorname{err}_{\mathcal{D},h^\star}(\mathcal{L}(S))
            \right]
            \ge
            \frac14.
        \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate}
    Moreover, $d_{\mathrm{DS}}(\mathcal{H})=2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:00.764728+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cffd0594577aa08cbcbb76ba65f98583c3ee83f9ce8ea4c407a2c6cf741dc9e2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2512.04807, 'Existence and uniqueness of the canonical Brownian motion in non-simple conformal loop ens'] Let $\resexp$ be the exponent in Theorem~\ref{thm:unique_metric}. Almost surely, the heat kernel of the \clekp{} Brownian motion satisfies
