{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.231659+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d03bef12934be956f16fa004529053517c07a0c271d149cce7b2b8f80e8bb9b7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 30 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] Let $\delta>0$ and $N\in \N$. For fixed $u=(u_1,u_2)\in \Z^2$ and $v=(v_1,v_2)\in \Z^2$ with $v \succeq u$, consider the set of lattice points
\begin{equation}
    (z_i)_{i\in \lbr \ell\rbr} := \{ i \delta N^{3/2}  \in [u_2,v_2] \text{ for some } i\in \N \} 
\end{equation} for some $\ell\in \N_0$, with the convention that $z_0=u_2$ and $z_{\ell+1}=v_2$, and $z_{i-1} \leq z_{i}$ for all $i\in \lbr \ell+1 \rbr$. 
    We say $\gper_{u,v}$ is $\boldsymbol{(\delta,N)}$\textbf{-good} if for all $i\in \lbr \ell +1 \rbr$ 
\begin{equation}
  \sup_{\varphi \in [z_{i-1},z_i]} \Big| \gamma^{\per,\pm}_{u,v}( \varphi ) - \gamma^{\per,\pm}_{u,v}(z_{i-1})  - m_{\ast}^{-1} (\varphi-z_{i-1}) N^{3/2}   \Big| \leq \frac{N}{32}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:23.337858+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d0461721b6731ee003338d32510267db379bd51271cad19678f364bd9bb6f2a7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 1 of arXiv:2512.05687, '$L^p$-Boltzmann-Gibbs principle \\ via Littlewood-Paley-Stein inequality'] For functions $F=F(\phi)$ and $G=G(\phi) \in C^2(\R^N)$, if all integrals
in the following converge, we have
\begin{align*} 
& \int_{\R^N} GL_0F d\hat\mu_{N,m} = \int_{\R^N} FL_0G d\hat\mu_{N,m}
= -\frac12 \int_{\R^N} \sum_{x\in \T_N} \partial_{\phi(x)} F \cdot \partial_{\phi(x)} G \, d\hat\mu_{N,m}, \\
& \int_{\R^N} G L_1 F d\hat\mu_{N,m} = -\int_{\R^N} F L_1 G d\hat\mu_{N,m},
\intertext{and}
& \Ga(F) = \sum_{x\in \T_N} \big(\partial_{\phi(x)} F\big)^2 =: |D_\phi F|^2,
\end{align*}
where $D_\phi F = (\partial_{\phi(x)} F)_{x\in \T_N}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.714000+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d047d19a7ad48576774e4dba7e19b92ea9b9501db98c585967f07891dfa215ba","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem l:1103.7 of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] Fix $u \geq 0$ and, for each $n \geq 1$, some deterministic subset $\widetilde{{\rm D}}_n \subseteq \mathbb{Z}^2$. In addition, let us set
	\begin{equation}
		{\rm W}^{\widetilde{{\rm D}}_n}_n(u) = {\rm W}_n(u) \cap \widetilde{{\rm D}}_n
		\quad ; \qquad 
		{\mathbf W}^{\widetilde{{\rm D}}_n}_n(u) := \big\{ z \in \mathbb{X}_{n-r_n} :\: {\rm W}^{\widetilde{{\rm D}}_n} \cap {\rm Q}(z;n-r_n) \neq \emptyset\} \,.
	\end{equation}
	Then,
	\begin{equation}
		\lim_{\delta \downarrow 0} \limsup_{n \to \infty}
		\mathbb{P} \bigg( \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}|{\mathbf W}^{\widetilde{{\rm D}}_n}_n(u)| > \delta^{-1} \sqrt{|{\rm D}_n^\circ \cap \widetilde{{\rm D}}_n|/|{\rm D}_n|} \bigg) = 0 \,.
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:06:39.319687+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d04941ea4eda0c7478197b647fb02aa92679917019b3bf78e09242a2e70019c7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:one_step_improvement_intro of arXiv:2607.15140, 'On the absence of blow-up in the 3D Navier-Stokes equations with transport noise'] There exists $\gamma_0>0$, a spatial scale $\eta\in (0,1/4]$ and a frequency $L_0\geq 1$ such that for every $N\geq L_0$, it holds that
\begin{align}

\Big({\mathbf E}\Big\|v^N-\fint_{\eta B} v^N\Big\|_{\underline{L}^2(\eta Q)}^r \Big)^{1/r}
&
\leq \eta^{\gamma_0}\Big({\mathbf E}\Big\|v^N-\fint_{B} v^N\Big\|_{\underline{L}^2( Q)}^r \Big)^{1/r}\\ 
\nonumber
&+ \mathscr{C}_Q[\pi^N,(\widetilde{\pi}^N_{k,\alpha})_{k,\alpha},F,G],
\end{align}
where the last term collects the contributions of pressures and forcing at the scale $Q$ (see Subsection (ss:role_pressure_intro of the paper) for details on the former). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

