{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.530768+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d16beda00e4e85cb2b26841237eb1596c21e8321836d6e2de73420e9b1ea185f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 26 of arXiv:2604.01852, 'The on/off Brownian snake'] Let $\mu_{\rm d}$, $\mu_{\rm a} \in \mathcal{M}_F(\mathbb{R}^d)$ and let $(\mathcal{Y}_t^{(\rm d)}, \mathcal{Y}_t^{(\rm a)})_{t \geq 0}$ be an $\text{ooSBM}(4, c, \Tilde{c})$ started from $(\mu_{\rm d}, \mu_{\rm a})$. Then $\mathbb{P}_{(\mu_{\rm d}, \mu_{\rm a})}$-a.s. there exist $(N_k, k \in \mathbb{N})$ finite constants such that for $k \in \mathbb{N}$ and for $s$, $t \in \mathbb{R}$ with $k \leq s < t < k+1$ we have
    \begin{equation*}
        \overline{\bigcup_{s \leq u \leq t} \text{supp}(\mathcal{Y}_u^{(\rm d)}) \cup \text{supp}(\mathcal{Y}_u^{(\rm a)})} \subseteq \text{supp}(\mathcal{Y}_s^{(\rm d)}) \cup \text{supp}(\mathcal{Y}_s^{(\rm a)})^{N_kH(t-s)}.
    \end{equation*}
    \begin{proof}
        Lemma \ref{lemma oosbm finite propagation}, along with the Markov property for ooSBM, establishes the claim for $k=0$. The general result can then be derived from the $k=0$ case and then again by the Markov property of ooSBM.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:35:04.213285+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d1805c875563d1341b3b3bf2dc78d570db2364a9453d2f3bff200874202d2ee6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 6 of arXiv:2608.03956, 'From Triangular Array Progression\\ to Near-Log-Concave State Transitions'] Let $\eta = [\eta_1,\ldots,\eta_{n+1}]$ for $n\geq 2$ be a sequence, where $\min(\eta) > 0$. Define 
\begin{align}
\Theta_{i}(\eta) = \max\left( \log(\eta_{i-1}) + \log(\eta_{i+1}) - 2\log(\eta_i) \,,\, 0 \right), 
\end{align}
for every $i\in\{2,\ldots,n\}$. Then,
\begin{align}
\Theta(\eta) = \sum_{i=2}^{n} \Theta_{i}(\eta), 
\end{align}
is called the \emph{log-concavity deviation} of $\eta$ under logarithmic scaling.","status":"unverified"}

