{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:54:57.568519+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d1aa2d805f3f4f9f6bd278c3748297dd4a22dbd3f246699c81e0d2fe74ca4d00","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2412.03366, 'Regularity of Weighted Tensorized Fractional Brownian Fields and associated function space'] For $0 \leq p, \, q \leq \infty$ and $s, \, \beta \in \mathbb{R}$, the Besov space with logarithmic scale is defined by 
\begin{equation}
 B^{s}_{p,q,\vert \log \vert^{\beta}}(\mathbb{R}^2) :  = \Big\{ f \in \mathcal{S}'(\mathbb{R}^2)~:~ \,  \sum_{j \in \mathbb{N}} j ^{-\beta q} 2^{jsq} \Vert \Delta_j f\Vert_p^q < \infty\big\}
\end{equation}
and we define a norm on $B^{s}_{p,q,\vert \log \vert^{\beta}}(\mathbb{R}^2)$ by
$$
\Vert f \Vert_{B^{s}_{p,q,\vert \log \vert^{\beta}}(\mathbb{R}^2)} = \left( \sum_{j \in \mathbb{N}} j ^{-\beta q} 2^{jsq} \Vert \Delta_j f \Vert_p^q \right)^{1/q}.
$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:05:57.778529+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d1aa7880679c804eee81c8b93c11ed9407ea268bf1c384afa48f953d4f39987e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 25 of arXiv:2608.30549, '\vspace{-1cm}Maximally Spread Out Measures and Implications\\{} for Phase Transitions in A'] Let $(\SC, \varrho)$ be a non-empty quasi-metric space.
 Let $\mu$ be a Borel probability measure on $\SC$, and let $s\in (0,\infty)$ and $t\in \RR$.
 Assume that there exist $r_0>0$ and $c_0>0$ such that
 \begin{equation*}
     \om{\mu} \bigl( \ball(x,r)\bigr) 
    \le \exp\biggl( - c_0 \cdot \Bigl( \frac{1}{r}\Bigr)^{s} \cdot \Bigl( \log_2 \Bigl(\frac{1}{r}\Bigr)\Bigr)^{t}\biggr)
    \quad \text{for all $x\in \SC$ and all $r\in (0,r_0)$.}
 \end{equation*}
 Then for every $\eps\in (0,r_0)$ there exists an $\eps$-separated subset $P_{\eps}$ with
 \begin{equation*}
   \num P_{\eps}
   \geq \exp
        \biggl(
          c_0
          \cdot \left(\frac{1}{\eps}\right)^s
          \cdot \left(\log_2 \left(\frac{1}{\eps}\right)\right)^{t}
        \biggr).
 \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:43:55.604271+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d1c5ad8a33167c69a3e9278495ec92bc75ae802a8211788c0748479f14b46d96","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t:hp of arXiv:2604.03979, 'Stationary Distributions in Monotone Markov Models: Theory and Applications'] Let $(P_t)_{t \in \mathbb T}$ be a transition probability function on $S$.
    If $(P_t)_{t \in
    \mathbb T}$ is increasing and there exists a positive $u \in \mathbb T$ such that $P_u$
    satisfies the MMC, then $(P_t)_{t \in \mathbb T}$ is globally stable on $\mathscr{P}(S)$
    under $\beta$ and convergence to the unique fixed point $\phi^*$ is
    exponential: 

    \begin{equation}
        \beta(\phi P_t, \phi^*) \leq C e^{-\alpha t}
        \quad \text{for all $\phi \in \mathscr{P}(S)$ and $t \in \mathbb T$},
    \end{equation}

    where $C \coloneq 2/(1-\epsilon)$ and $\alpha \coloneq \ln(1/(1-\epsilon))/u$.
    Moreover, when $\mathbb T = \mathbb{Z}_+$, each $P$-Markov process is monotone ergodic:

    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbb P
        \left\{
             \lim_{n \to \infty}
             \frac{1}{n} \sum_{t=0}^{n-1} h(X_t) = \phi^*(h)
        \right\} = 1
        \quad \text{for all } h \in ibS.
    \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:14:37.610474+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d1c5b5605e72e5977ff591992cd3902fd78201a7ea0376fea1b091976a563d67","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2608.19065, 'Boundedly exchangeable graphs'] Let $G$ be an undirected, unweighted graph without multiple edges, possibly with loops, whose vertex set $v(G)\subseteq S$ is locally finite. The adjacency measure $\xi_G$ associated with $G$ is given by 
