{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:28:53.670658+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d20cb0924e26aaf9849abad08feacc27cf1fe7c2dcb26039136ee5671056c2cc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (ergodicity of the sticky CIR
process) of arXiv:2605.13648, 'Sticky CIR process with potential: invariant measure and exact sampling'] Let \(u_t\) be the sticky CIR process defined by \cref{eq-CIR4}
with parameters \(\lambda, \beta, \mu > 0\) and \(\delta \in (1,2)\). 
\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}

\item There exists a unique invariant
  probability measure \(\pi_0\) on \([0,\infty)\) given by \[
  \pi_0(dx) = \frac{1}{Z} \left[ \frac{1}{\mu} \delta_0(dx) + \beta x^{\delta-1} e^{-\lambda\beta x^2/2} \, dx \right]
  \] where $Z = 1/{\mu} + \int_0^\infty \beta x^{\delta-1}
e^{-\lambda\beta x^2/2}\,dx<\infty$.
\item For any initial point \(x \in [0,\infty)\) and
  any bounded measurable function \(\phi\colon [0,\infty)\to \mathbb{R}\), \[
  \lim_{t \to \infty} P_t\phi(x) = \int_{[0,\infty)} \phi(y) \, \pi_0(dy).
  \]
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.288597+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d210a56695e838eabda28451249d94dd7b5365d1802efecc83d224b8c921ef93","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition pr:hextension of arXiv:2604.21142, 'Gaussian fluctuations for Internal DLA on cylinders'] For $2 \leq k \leq N$, let $q_k > 0$ be the positive real number defined by
	\begin{equation}
	 \cosh(q_k) = 2 - \l_k\,.
	 \end{equation}
(By irreducibility and laziness, $\l_k \in [0, 1)$ for $k \geq 2$, so $2 - \l_k \in (1, 2]$ and $q_k > 0$.) Then, for every $h \in {\ensuremath{\mathbb Z}} $ and every sequence $(\a_k(h))_{2 \leq k \leq N}$ of real numbers, the function
	\[ \psi(x, y) = \sum_{k=2}^N \a_k(h)\, f_k^N(x)\, e^{q_k(y - h)} \]
is discrete harmonic on ${\ensuremath{\mathbb G}} _N$, decays exponentially as $y \to -\infty$, and satisfies $\psi(x, h) = \sum_{k=2}^N \a_k(h)\,f_k^N(x)$ on the line $\{(x, h) : x \in V_N\}$.","status":"unverified"}

