{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.056949+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d27c01c7f2fbdba72dfc67797defaa7ff7cde739342e7a30af8829e7805fca97","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:maximal-hessian-realization of arXiv:2608.16119, 'Fully nonlinear parabolic equations under a fixed reference diffusion: weighted \texorpdfs'] Suppose that Assumption~(ass:reference-diffusion of the paper) holds.
Let $I=(r_0,r_1)$, let $w$ be regular, and let
$\mathscr D\subset\mathcal H_{w;I}$ be dense.  The maximal norm then satisfies
\begin{equation}
 C_{w,I}(X)
 :=\sup_{0\ne F\in\mathcal H_{w;I}}
   \frac{\left\lVert \mathcal A_IF\right\rVert_{w,g;I}}{\left\lVert F\right\rVert_{w;I}}
 =\sup_{0\ne F\in\mathscr D}
   \frac{\left\lVert \mathcal A_IF\right\rVert_{w,g;I}}{\left\lVert F\right\rVert_{w;I}}.

\end{equation}
If this common value is finite, $\mathcal A_I$ has a unique bounded
realization
\begin{equation}
 \mathsf T_{w,I}:\mathcal H_{w;I}\longrightarrow\mathcal K_{w;I},\qquad
 \norm{\mathsf T_{w,I}}=C_{w,I}(X).

\end{equation}
Here $X=X^{0,x_0}$ denotes the full reference diffusion, so the notation
records its horizon, initial point, transition family, occupation laws, and
Riemannian metric, rather than only its one-time marginals.  The full-interval
realization $\mathsf T_w$ exists whenever $C_w(X)<\infty$.
Moreover, for $J\subset I$ and $I_s=(s,r_1)$ with $s\in(r_0,r_1)$,
\begin{align}
 \mathrm R_{I_s}^I S_I
=S_{I_s}\mathrm R_{I_s}^I,\qquad
 \mathrm R_J^I S_I\mathrm E_J^I=S_J,
 \\
 \mathrm R_{I_s}^I \mathsf T_{w,I}
 =\mathsf T_{w,I_s}\mathrm R_{I_s}^I,\qquad
 \mathrm R_J^I \mathsf T_{w,I}\mathrm E_J^I=\mathsf T_{w,J},

\end{align}
where the identities in (equation eq:maximal-observation-compatibility of the paper) hold when
$C_{w,I}(X)<\infty$; in that case
\begin{equation}
 C_{w,J}(X)\leq C_{w,I}(X).

\end{equation}
Thus $S_I$ and $\mathsf T_{w,I}$ are backward causal.  Finally, for
regular $w_1,w_2$, whenever the two bounded realizations exist,
\begin{equation}
 \mathsf T_{w_1,I}F=\mathsf T_{w_2,I}F
 \quad\text{in distributions for every }
 F\in\mathcal H_{w_1;I}\cap\mathcal H_{w_2;I}.

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.053079+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d28bc09a557fba434ff128b416645d1a83ea91611b0d0d3822ef8575e5f061f5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 36 of arXiv:2606.16164, 'A Low-Regularity Semigroup Sewing Lemma via Quotient Structures'] Let $\alpha\in\R$ and let $\D^{\mathsf s}_{1}(\Delta_T^3;B_\alpha)$ be a critical admissible selection domain in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:admissible-selection-domain}.  Then the dyadic recursion above extends uniquely by continuity to a continuous linear map
\begin{equation*}
\hat\Lambda^{\mathsf s}_{S,1,\alpha}:\D^{\mathsf s}_{1}(\Delta_T^3;B_\alpha)\to C_{\log}^{1}(\Delta_T^2;B_\alpha), \quad H\mapsto \hat\Lambda^{\mathsf s}_{S,1,\alpha}H:= R^{\mathsf s}(H),
\end{equation*}
satisfying
\begin{equation}

\hdd\hat\Lambda^{\mathsf s}_{S,1,\alpha}H=H.
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}

\norm{\hat\Lambda^{\mathsf s}_{S,1,\alpha}H}_{1,\log;\alpha}\le C_{T,S,C_\ast,\eta,\mathsf s} \norm{H}_{1,\alpha}.
\end{equation}
For an individual defect outside a fixed linear domain, the same estimate holds with the right-hand side replaced by $C_{T,S,C_\ast,\eta}(\norm{H}_{1,\alpha}+C_H)$.  Consequently, for every $0<\varepsilon<1$,
\begin{equation}

\norm{\hat\Lambda^{\mathsf s}_{S,1,\alpha}H}_{1-\varepsilon,\alpha}\le C_{\varepsilon,T,S,C_\ast,\eta,\mathsf s}\norm{H}_{1,\alpha}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:30.015001+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d28d9c1571d0c1425ac9dd7cace8c8ad70756ab748e9302d78e262a0b8058823","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 of arXiv:2605.05388, 'The Efron-Stein inequality for identically distributed pairs'] Let \(\epsilon\in \mathbb R\), let \(X_i\) be uniform on \([0,2\pi]\) and let \(Y_i\) equal \(X_i+2\epsilon\) mod \(2\pi\). Then \(X_i\stackrel{d}{=}Y_i\). Assume that the pairs \((X_i,Y_i)\) are independent as \(i\) varies. Let \(f(x_1,\ldots x_n)=\sin(x_1+\ldots +x_n)\), and let \(Z_i=f(X_1,\ldots X_{i},Y_{i+1},\ldots Y_n)\). Then the random variables \(Z_i\) (and hence the ratio on the left-hand side of \eqref{e}) depend on \(\epsilon\), and we have
\begin{equation}
\lim_{\epsilon\to 0} \frac
{E(Z_n-Z_0)^2}{\sum_{i=1}^n E(Z_i-Z_{i-1})^2}=n.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:34.576144+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d293edcb3e5b3dbde440c9eba0f3953b94acc8598f06e3fa1ed4ef365baa32ef","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 1 of arXiv:2606.14812, 'Joint convergence in Wiener chaos via transport hierarchy and Malliavin covariances'] Let $ (f_{N}, N\geq 1)$ be a sequence  in $ H ^{\odot p}$ with $p\geq 2$. Assume	\begin{equation}
			f_{N} \otimes _{1} f_{N}\to _{N \to \infty} 0  
			\mbox{ in } H ^{\otimes 2p-2}.
		\end{equation} and that there exists $M>0$ such that 
		\begin{equation}
			\sup_{N\geq1 } \Vert f_{N} \Vert ^{2} _{ H^{\otimes p}}\leq M.
		\end{equation}
		Then 

		\begin{enumerate}
			\item 	For every $ g\in H ^{\odot p}$ we have 

			\begin{equation*}
				\langle f_{N}, g\rangle _{ H ^{\otimes p}} \to _{N \to \infty} 0.
			\end{equation*}
			\item Let $ (g_{N}, N\geq 1)$  be a sequence in $ H ^{\odot p}$ which converges in $ H ^{\otimes p}$. Then 
			\begin{equation*}
				\langle f_{N}, g_{N}\rangle _{ H ^{\otimes p}}\to _{N \to \infty }0.
			\end{equation*}

		\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

