{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:56:11.139796+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d2c262aff15611b7cd771faa58d7568e943913e0ea64df342d2b8f1054ee0bd4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 24 (One-sided endpoint domination) of arXiv:2608.10992, '2608.10992'] Fix $s\geq0$ with $\mathbb E[L_s]<\infty$ and $K\geq1$. Let
$q^{s,K}$ be the tail flow of Lemma~\ref{lem:tail-flow-general-data}
with initial data
\[
q_k^{s,K}(0)
:=
\mathbb P(L_s\wedge K\geq k).
\]
Then, for every $k\geq1$ and $t\geq0$,
\begin{equation}

q_k^{s,K}(t)
\leq
\mathbb P(L_{s+t}\geq k).
\end{equation}
Consequently, with $m_{s,K}:=\mathbb E[L_s\wedge K]$,
\begin{equation}

t_{\infty}^{\mathrm{iso}}
\leq
s+2\log\left(1+\frac1{m_{s,K}}\right).
\end{equation}
In particular,
\begin{equation}

X_t\preceq L_t,
\qquad
t_{\infty}^{\mathrm{iso}}
\leq t_{\infty}^{\mathrm{os}}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:10:05.827865+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d2c396994debfdf08f5d5694c6be6a2518a78245f04054db44051343db204f17","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 33 of arXiv:2608.17509, '2608.17509'] Let \(a>1\), and for \(m\geq1\) set
\(
        M_m(a):=\int_0^\infty r_a(\xi)^m\,\mathrm d\xi.
\)
Then
\begin{equation}

        M_m(a)
        =
        \frac{1}{2(a-1)}
        B\left(\frac{m}{a-1},m+1\right),
\end{equation}
where \(B\) is the Beta function. Equivalently,
\begin{equation}

        M_m(a)
        =
        \frac{1}{2}
        \sum_{j=0}^{m}
        (-1)^j
        \binom{m}{j}
        \frac{1}{m+(a-1)j}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, as \(m\to\infty\),
\begin{equation}

        M_m(a)
        \sim
        \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2a}}\,m^{-1/2}\rho_a^m.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.343726+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d2c530853991b0e7b744537f78c15546acf4b6eaa202bdb7456af6b5b9f3acf3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:concentration of arXiv:2412.01381, 'Ergodicity and mixing for locally monotone stochastic evolution equations'] Assume that Hypotheses~(hyp:hemicont of the paper),~(hyp:coerc of the paper),~(hyp:coerc-g of the paper),~(hyp:monotonicity of the paper),~(hyp:monotonicity-g of the paper), (hyp:growth of the paper), and (hyp:regularity of the paper) are valid.
Then any invariant probability measure $\mu_\ast$ has finite $H$-moments of order $\alpha+\beta$. In addition,
\begin{equation}
\int_{H} \|z\|^{\alpha+\beta}_H\,\mu_\ast(\mathrm{d} z)\le\frac{2(c_1+c_2)}{c_0^\alpha\lambda_0\delta_1(\beta+2)}.
\end{equation}
In particular, 
\begin{equation}
\int_{H} \|z\|_H^2\,\mu_\ast(\mathrm{d} z)\le \left(\frac{2(c_1+c_2)}{c_0^\alpha\lambda_0\delta_1(\beta+2)}\right)^{2/(\alpha+\beta)}.
\end{equation}
Furthermore, $\mu_\ast$ has finite $V$-moments of order $\alpha$, and, in particular,
\begin{equation}
\int_{H} \|z\|^{\alpha}_V\,\mu_\ast(\mathrm{d} z)\le\frac{\|B\|^2_{L_2(U,H)}}{\delta_1}.
\end{equation}
Here, the constants are as in Hypotheses~(hyp:coerc of the paper),~(hyp:monotonicity of the paper),~(hyp:growth of the paper),~and~(hyp:quant of the paper), respectively.","status":"unverified"}

