{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.812926+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d3a6be5f1d473bf1543f27a6303fe98417db47b7a5d1fe62fcbdd05189a8bbb3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:loc_lim of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] Consider the graph $G_n=(V_n,E_n)$ as a graph with types from $S=[k]$, with a vertex $v$ having type $i$ if $v \in \mathcal N^{(i)}$, and let $o_n$ be a randomly sampled vertex from $V_n$, independent of $G_n$. Then under Assumptions (assump:part_i_size of the paper), (assump:degree_regularity of the paper), (assump:offspring_degree_reg of the paper) and (assump:one_good_deg_greater_2 of the paper), the rooted graph with types $(G_n,o_n)$ converges \emph{locally in probability} to the infinite rooted tree with types $(\mathcal T_\infty,\varnothing)$, i.e., the following convergence in probability holds: for any finite rooted tree $(\mathbf t,o)$ with vertex types from $[k]$ and for any $r\geq 1$,
    \begin{align*}
    \frac{1}{n}\sum_{v \in V}\ind{B_{G_n}(v,r)\simeq_S (\mathbf t,o)}\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mathbb{P}}{\rightarrow}} \Prob{B_{\mathcal T_\infty}(\varnothing,r)\simeq_S (\mathbf t,o)}. \addtocounter{equation}{1}\tag{\theequation} 
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:15:34.405401+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d3a8a312c2949918817d0ce7e776ab8a7e7616f3bba5e60ad45e842b9d22fd8b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 10 of arXiv:2609.03268, 'Poincare Polynomials of Heavy-Light Hassett Spaces'] Let $p_n(x)$ be as defined in \cref{thm:LM-ppoly-deg2-recursion}; and let
                $$P(x,t)=\sum_{n\ge 0} p_n(x)\frac{t^n}{n!}\in \Q(x)[[t]]$$
                be their exponential generating function. Then $P(x,t)$ satisfies the differential equation 
                \begin{equation}

                    P_t=xP^2+(1-x)P
                \end{equation}
                and hence,
                \begin{equation}

                    P(x,t)=\frac{1-x}{e^{(x-1)t}-x}.
                \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.118093+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d3abf7a1a6b097bb7f26264a3b98cd1b37e3c31b1beab2dec4418ce534a1d29d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:reilly of arXiv:2608.17022, 'Sharp hypocoercive convergence estimates for underdamped Langevin dynamics with specular r'] Assume $\partial\Omega\in C^{2,1}$ (or $C^3$) and $U\in C^2(\overline\Omega)$. For every $h\in D(\mathscr{L}_{\mathrm{o}})$ as in (equation eq:neumann-domain of the paper),
\begin{equation}
\int_\Omega(\mathscr{L}_{\mathrm{o}} h)^2\,\mathrm{d}\mu_x
=\int_\Omega\|\nabla^2_xh\|_F^2\,\mathrm{d}\mu_x
+\int_\Omega\langle\nabla^2U\,\nabla_xh,\nabla_xh\rangle\,\mathrm{d}\mu_x
+\frac1{Z_x}\int_{\partial\Omega}\mathrm{II}(\nabla_xh,\nabla_xh)\,e^{-U}\mathrm{d}\sigma,
\end{equation}
where $\|\cdot\|_F$ is the Frobenius norm, $\mathrm{II}$ is the second fundamental form of $\partial\Omega$ with respect to the outward normal and $Z_x=\int_\Omega e^{-U}\mathrm{d} x$. In particular, since $\mathrm{II}\succeq0$ by convexity and $\nabla^2U\succeq-K\,\mathrm{Id}$,
\begin{equation}
\nrm[\mu_x]{\nabla^2_xh}^2\le\nrm[\mu_x]{\mathscr{L}_{\mathrm{o}} h}^2+K\,\nrm[\mu_x]{\nabla_xh}^2 .
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.640936+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d3b30142e06698c2c80e61c099550cc2b4c455ad7ccc4a66757af199ae1b535b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary BEln-cor-3 of arXiv:2411.13788, '\bf Gradient bounds and Liouville property for a class of hypoelliptic diffusion via coupl'] Let $f\in C^2\left(\mathbb{R}^{m_0}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{R}^{m_r}\right)$ be a  positive, globally Lipschitz  and bounded function.  Then for any fixed $t\ge0$, $x=(x^{(1)},\cdots,x^{(r+1)})\in \mathbb{R}^{m_0}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{R}^{m_r}$,  we have
\begin{equation}
2P_tf(x)\Gamma(\ln P_tf)(x)\le P_t\left(f\left\langle E(-t)AE^*(-t)\nabla \ln f,\nabla \ln f\right\rangle\right)(x).
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
P_tf(x)\left\langle E(t)AE^*(t)\nabla P_tf(x),\nabla P_tf(x)\right\rangle\le 2P_t\left(f\Gamma(\ln f)\right)(x).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

