        \mathbb{E} \bigg|\frac{\Delta_n}{n} - \frac{d - 2}{d}\bigg|^m \gtrsim n^{-m/2}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.797569+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d4904847dfc89634cb4555ad87fcc67d4242500fd567b70049adc1ac9b9ee4ea","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemme 56 of arXiv:2604.00635, 'Large deviations of the periodic Toda chain'] Let $\mu \in \mathcal{M}_1(\mathbb{R})$ and $\rho > 1$. Then $\op{s}_{\rho \#} \mu \to \mu$ as $\rho \searrow 1$ in the weak topology. Moreover,
\begin{align*}
   &\text{if } \int x \,  \mathrm{d} \op{s}_{\rho \#} \mu(x) =0   & &\text{ then } & & \int x \,  \mathrm{d} \mu(x) =0  \, , \\
   &\text{and if} \int |x|^\theta \,  \mathrm{d}\mu(x) < +\infty  & & \text{ then } & &  \int |x|^\theta \, \mathrm{d} \op{s}_{\rho \#} \mu(x) < +\infty \, .
\end{align*}
Furthermore,
if $\mu$ satisfies \eqref{cond1} then
\begin{align}

    1+\frac{2}{\ell} \Int{\mathbb{R}}{} \ln|x-y| \, \mathrm{d}(\op{s}_{\rho \#} \mu) (y) \, \geq \, 2\kappa := \frac{2}{\ell} \ln \rho > 0,\quad \op{s}_{\rho \#} \mu \quad a.s.

\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.567065+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d49281363188e4af38c04586d7349e705a41ada998a9b2dd7c05bbe51bb614e5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 33 of arXiv:2609.01460, 'On cost-induced Santal\'o-type inequalities \\ in Polish measure spaces'] Consider a family of functions $(R_s)_{s\geq 0}$, where each $R_s:[0,1]\to[0,1]$ is a function that satisfies
\begin{equation}
R_0(v)=v, \   R_s(v)\ge v,
\end{equation}
and the family has the property that $s\mapsto R_s(v)$ is non-decreasing and continuous for every $v \in [0,1]$. 

We say the family $(R_s)_{s\geq 0}$ satisfies an enlargement inequality for $(X,d,\mu)$ if
\begin{equation}

\mu(A_s)\ge R_s(\mu(A)).
\end{equation}
We call each $R_s$ an enlargement function. Moreover, we associate with $(R_s)_{s\geq 0}$ the function $\Theta: \R_+ \to [0,1/2]$ given by
\begin{equation}

\Theta(s):=\sup_{0\le v\le1}\sqrt{v\bigl(1-R_s(v)\bigr)}.
\end{equation}
Finally, the function $\Theta$ is non-increasing in $s$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:37:25.898945+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d4933799d8a2958b57b66a7d25a85a2c30083d277ccea86a8a82e64c8d19556d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 (Cyclotomic expansion for symmetric-power-colored $SU(n)$ invariants) of arXiv:2608.29585, '2608.29585'] For every zero-framed knot $K$ and every fixed $n\ge2$, there is a unique
sequence of Laurent polynomials
\[
 \bigl(H_k^{(n)}(K;q)\bigr)_{k\ge0},
 \qquad H_k^{(n)}(K;q)\in\Z[q^{\pm1}],
\]
such that, for all $r\ge0$,
\begin{equation}
 J_r^{SU(n)}(K;q)=\sum_{k=0}^{r}
 \left(\prod_{i=0}^{k-1}\{r-i\}\{r+n+i\}\right)H_k^{(n)}(K;q).
\end{equation}
Moreover, $H_0^{(n)}(K;q)=1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.290714+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d49a77524cfedc5443d091a340e2b784290873e64b93bfa11c974e3e254145d3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 27 of arXiv:2606.23099, 'Path-dependent Affine Processes'] Assume that there exist constants $L > 0$ and $C_{LG} > 0$ such that the following conditions hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(i)] (uniform Lipschitz condition) For all $(t,x) \in \ccC_T^d$ and $(t,y) \in\ccC_T^d$, we have
              \begin{equation}
                  \|b(t,x) - b(t,y)\|^2 + \|\sigma(t,x) - \sigma(t,y)\|^2 \leq L \sup_{0 \leq s \leq t}\|x(s)-y(s)\|^2.
              \end{equation}
        \item[(ii)] (linear growth condition) For all $(t,x) \in\ccC_T^d$, it holds that
              \begin{equation}
                  \|b(t,x)\|^2 + \|\sigma(t,x)\|^2 \leq C_{LG}\left(1 + \sup_{s \in [0,t]}\|x(s)\|^2 \right).
              \end{equation}
    \end{enumerate}
    Then the path-dependent SDE \eqref{path_dependent_sde_existence} admits a unique strong solution $X = (X(t))_{t \in [0,T]}$.
    Moreover, this solution satisfies the integrability condition $E\left[ \int_0^T \|X(t)\|^2 dt \right] < \infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

