{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:42:08.464581+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d4e1c50007ab52eb8c847a0e778a7024c1729c18fb4edc65f8168ed7a23aaee3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma notation:LT of arXiv:2511.15893, 'On a Class of Dynamical Poisson-Voronoi Tessellations'] For any $\gamma\geq 0$, the Laplace transform of $H_{\scriptscriptstyle V}^2$, under the Palm probability distribution $\mathbb{P}^0_{{\mathcal A}_{\scriptscriptstyle V}}$ of ${\mathcal A}_{\scriptscriptstyle V}$, is given by 

\begin{equation}
    {\mathcal L }^0_{H_{\scriptscriptstyle V}^2}(\gamma)= \left(1+\frac{\gamma}{\lambda\pi}\right)^{-1/2}, 
\end{equation}

that is, $H_{\scriptscriptstyle V}$  follows a Nakagami distribution with parameter $\left({\frac{1}{2}}, \frac{1}{2\lambda\pi}\right)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.385969+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d4ead1d68abc39cb01f002b5b2fa7b462aa73e4c5cc5a132a07f91ee21ed2e12","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem_hops of arXiv:2411.02046, 'Speed of Convergence and Moderate Deviations of FPP on Random Geometric Graphs'] Let~$\gamma = (x_0,\ldots, x_n)$ be a path in~$\mathcal G^t$. Assume that~$x_0$ and~$x_n$ belong to~$\mathcal H$ (the infinite cluster of~$\mathcal G$), and~$x_1,\ldots,x_{n-1}$ do not belong to~$\mathcal H$ (so that each of them is either in~$t\Z^d$ or in some finite cluster of~$\mathcal G$). Let
	\[b:= \max\left\{\|y-x\|_\infty:\; \begin{array}{l} x, y \in V,\; x \text{ and } y \text{ are in the same finite} \\ \text{cluster of }\mathcal G, \text{ and this cluster is visited by } \gamma \end{array}\right\},\]
		that is,~$b$ is the maximum~$\ell_\infty$-diameter of all finite clusters of~$\mathcal G$ intersected by~$\gamma$. Then,
	\begin{equation*}
		\sum_{e \in \gamma} \tau^t_e \ge \frac{\K t}{2t+b}\cdot \|x_n -x_0\|_\infty.
	\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:52:23.576276+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d4f3f29795b0b009716154c708e3a5a1ae55a729f483d4a47eb50c0d799dd7ea","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem T:PDE of arXiv:2602.05317, 'Strong local nondeterminism for stochastic time-fractional\\ slow and fast diffusion equat'] For $\alpha\in (0,\infty)$, $\beta\in (0,2]$, and $\gamma\ge 0$, the solution
  to
  \begin{align*} 
    \begin{cases}
      \left(\partial_t^\beta + \dfrac{\nu}{2} (-\Delta)^{\alpha/2}\right) u(t,x) = I_t^\gamma\left[f(t,x)\right], & \qquad t>0,\: x\in\mathbb{R}^d,                         \\[1em]
      \left.\dfrac{\partial^k}{\partial t^k} u(t,x)\right|_{t = 0} = u_k(x),                                      & \qquad 0\le k\le \left\lceil \beta \right\rceil-1, \:\: x\in\mathbb{R}^d,
    \end{cases}
  \end{align*}
  with $f$ and $u_k$ being smooth functions with compact support (i.e., $f\in
    C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R}_+\times\mathbb{R}^d)$ and $u_k\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb{R}^d)$) is
  \begin{align*} 
    u(t,x) = J_0(t,x) + \int_0^t \ensuremath{\mathrm{d} } s \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \ensuremath{\mathrm{d} } y\: f(s,y) \: \DRL{0+}{\left\lceil \beta \right\rceil-\beta-\gamma} Z(t-s,x-y),
  \end{align*}
  where $\DRL{0+}{\left\lceil \beta \right\rceil-\beta-\gamma}$ denotes the
  \textit{Riemann-Liouville derivative}\index{Riemann-Liouville integral} $D_{0+}^{\left\lceil \beta \right\rceil-\beta-\gamma}$
  acting on the time variable,
  \begin{align*} 
    J_0(t,x)\coloneqq \sum_{k = 0}^{\left\lceil \beta \right\rceil-1}\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} u_{k}(y) \partial_t^{\left\lceil \beta \right\rceil-1-k} Z(t,x-y) \ensuremath{\mathrm{d} } y
  \end{align*}
  is the solution to the homogeneous equation and $Z(t,x) \coloneqq
    Z_{\alpha,\beta,d}(t,x)$ is the corresponding fundamental solution. Denote
  \begin{gather*}
    Y(t,x) \coloneqq Y_{\alpha,\beta,\gamma,d}(t,x) = \DRL{0+}{\left\lceil \beta \right\rceil-\beta-\gamma}
    Z_{\alpha,\beta,d}(t,x), \\
    Z^* (t,x) \coloneqq Z^{*}_{\alpha,\beta,d}(t,x) = \frac{\partial}{\partial t} Z_{\alpha,\beta,d}(t,x),
    \quad \text{if $\beta\in(1,2]$.}
  \end{gather*}
  Then, we have the following Fourier transforms:
  \begin{align}
    \mathcal{F} Z_{\alpha,\beta,d}(t,\cdot)(\xi)        & = t^{\left\lceil \beta \right\rceil-1} E_{\beta,\left\lceil \beta \right\rceil}(-\tfrac12 \nu t^\beta |\xi|^\alpha),              \\
    \mathcal{F} Y_{\alpha,\beta,\gamma,d}(t,\cdot)(\xi) & = t^{\beta+\gamma-1} E_{\beta,\beta+\gamma}(-\tfrac12 \nu t^\beta |\xi|^\alpha),  \\
    \mathcal{F} Z^*_{\alpha,\beta,d}(t,\cdot)(\xi)      & = t^{k} E_{\beta,k+1}(-\tfrac12\nu t^\beta |\xi|^\alpha), \quad \text{if $\beta\in (1,2]$}.

  \end{align}
  Moreover, when $\beta\in (0,2)$, we have the following explicit expressions:
  \begin{align}
    Z(t,x) = \pi^{-\frac d2} t^{\left\lceil \beta \right\rceil-1} |x|^{-d}
    \FoxH{2,1}{2,3}{\frac{|x|^\alpha}{2^{\alpha-1}\nu t^\beta}}{(1,1),\:(\left\lceil \beta \right\rceil,\beta)}
    {(d/2,\alpha/2),\:(1,1),\:(1,\alpha/2)},
  \end{align}
  \begin{align}
    \begin{aligned}
      Y(t,x) = \pi^{-\frac d2} |x|^{-d}t^{\beta+\gamma-1}
      \FoxH{2,1}{2,3}{\frac{|x|^\alpha}{2^{\alpha-1}\nu t^\beta}}
      {(1,1),\:(\beta+\gamma,\beta)}{(d/2,\alpha/2),\:(1,1),\:(1,\alpha/2)},
    \end{aligned}
  \end{align}
  and, if $\beta\in (1,2)$,
  \begin{align}
    Z^{*}(t,x)=
    \pi^{-\frac d2} |x|^{-d}
    \FoxH{2,1}{2,3}{\frac{|x|^\alpha}{2^{\alpha-1}\nu t^\beta}}{(1,1),\:(1,\beta)}
    {(d/2,\alpha/2),\:(1,1),\:(1,\alpha/2)},
  \end{align}
  where $H_{2,3}^{2,1}$ 
  refers to the Fox $H$-function defined as in~(equation E:Fox-H of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:48:02.410569+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d50170312f65b1b153a5be19b1e5198f39f2592deb22588a379a6d10ad4cff0b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:modelcriterion of arXiv:2608.26564, 'Real ideal points, Conway spheres, and left-orderable Dehn fillings'] Under the above hypotheses, the four-longitude decomposition is in positive parabolic normal form along one, and hence both, real half-branches if and only if $c_1c_2\alpha_1\alpha_2 < 0$. Moreover, for each real half-branch $B$, there is a sign $\sigma_B \in \{\pm 1\}$ such that its limiting height vector is 
\begin{equation*}
	(h_1, h_2, h_3, h_4) = \sigma_B(\eta_1, \mathrm{sgn}(c_1c_2)\eta_2, \eta_3, \mathrm{sgn}(c_1c_2)\eta_4)
\end{equation*}
Consequently, height-cancellation holds along one, and hence both, real half-branches if and only if $\eta_1+\eta_3 + \mathrm{sgn}(c_1c_2)(\eta_2 + \eta_4) = 0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:23:05.302296+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d50995405b1f1040683c1912d9f66829d2e56c0b7541763932740c8654859be2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 5 of arXiv:2608.21787, 'Stability of extremal hyperplane projections of balls in $\ell_p^n(\R)$'] Let $Y_1$, $Y_2$ be i.i.d. square integrable random variables whose distribution is not a Dirac delta distribution. Let $a_1$, $a_2$, $b_1$, $b_2$, $s$ also be real numbers such that $s \ge 0$, $a_1^2 + a_2^2 = b_1^2 + b_2^2 = \sigma^2 > 0$ and $a_1^2 \ge b_1^2 \ge b_2^2 \ge a_2^2$. Then there exist positive constants $c$, $\eta$, depending only on the distribution of $Y_1$, such that
        \begin{align*}
            \E\sqrt{b_1^2Y_1^2 + b_2^2Y_2^2 + s} - \E\sqrt{a_1^2Y_1^2 + a_2^2Y_2^2 + s} \ge c\left(s + \eta\sigma^2\right)^{-\frac 3 2}\left(a_1^4 + a_2^4 - b_1^4 - b_2^4\right).
        \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.090618+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d50c4627629a66377aee3aa83edd934a36260a1ae151d391c688be98e20ad27c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi 27 of arXiv:2608.28148, 'Flows and homotopies of Banach Lie algebroids'] Let $(E,\pi,M)$ be a Banach bundle and $U$ be an open set of $M$. A \emph{linear local}  vector field on $E$ is a pair $(\mathcal{X},X)$ where $\mathcal{X}$ is a vector field on $E_{U}$ and $X$ a vector field on $U$ such that
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
E_U \arrow[r,"\mathcal{X}"] \arrow[d,"\pi"]& TE_U \arrow[d,"T\pi"] \\ U \arrow[r,"X"] & TU
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
is a bundle morphism. When $U=M$ the pair $(\mathcal{X},X)$ is simply called a \emph{linear vector field} on $E$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.376438+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d51060e7d37e6ed3b7dbb15137eac2e5e003d9cda09db376d3aaec27b7af7bf2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:e2 of arXiv:2412.01995, 'Exciting games and Monge-Amp\`{e}re equations'] For any $N \in \mathbb N$, we have that
