{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.162583+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5632b0db3e0eb0d45ffbf39f08fce1dc4719dd645c252975053261b738002af","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 13 (Theorem 2.2 of \cite{zhang2016central}) of arXiv:2604.17302, 'Elephant random walk with attributed steps and extractions of random sizes'] Suppose $\{Z_{n}\}$ converges to the constant $\theta^{*}$ almost surely, and that Assumption~\eqref{gen_assump_dist_2} is satisfied. Suppose $0<\rho<1/2$, that
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\E\left[\Delta M_{i+1}\left(\Delta M_{i+1}\right)^{t}\Big|\mathcal{F}_{i}\right]=O(n) \text{ almost surely},
\end{equation}
and that there exists some $0<\epsilon<1-\rho$ such that
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\delta_{i}=o\left(n^{1-\rho-\epsilon}\right) \text{ almost surely}.
\end{equation}
Then there exist complex random variables $W_{1},\ldots,W_{s}$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\frac{n^{\rho}}{(\log n)^{\nu-1}}(Z_{n}-\theta^{*})-\sum_{j\in[s]:\text{Re}(-\lambda_{j})=\rho,\nu_{j}=\nu}\exp\left\{i\text{Im}(\lambda_{j})\log n\right\}W_{j}\left(T^{-1}\right)^{t}e_{j}\convas 0,
\end{equation}
where $i=\sqrt{-1}$, the notation $\text{Im}(\lambda)$ indicates the imaginary part of $\lambda$, the matrix $T$ is as defined via \eqref{similarity_eq_general}, and $e_{j}$ is the $d$-dimensional column vector such that, if its first $\nu_{1}$ coordinates make up its first block, the next $\nu_{2}$ coordinates make up its second block, and so on, then the $\nu_{j}$-th coordinate of its $j$-th block equals $1$, and all other coordinates equal $0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:30:36.777591+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d563ee8bb48ff93829f9231f7e682cb5d008a7980c83fa570ced5753d66b4c3c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 6 of arXiv:2607.10486, 'Weak martingale solutions to stochastic Navier-Stokes-Cahn-Hilliard system with transport '] If $\phi \in \zero{H}{2}(\domO)$ and 
