{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.218374+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d69d27f77e1bfa6cf22e29b58ad25539f17fc12054d1eb3fa9309e9ddcf1452e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:LIL of arXiv:2608.20537, 'Persistent anisotropy in multi-dimensional\\ branching Brownian motion'] Let $\beta \in \mathcal{C}^2\big([0, \infty); \mathbb{R}\big)$ satisfy $t \dot{\beta}_t \to 0$ as $t \to \infty$.
  If 
  \begin{equation}

    \int_1^\infty \frac{\mathrm{d} r}{r \exp \beta_r} = \infty,
  \end{equation}
  then almost surely, for every nonempty open $U \subset S^{d-1}$, there exist sequences $t_n \nearrow \infty$ and $p_n \in \mathcal{P}_{t_n}$ such that for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$,
  \begin{equation}

    R_{t_n}(p_n) = 2t_n + \frac{d-2}{2} \log t_n + \beta_{t_n} \quad \text{and} \quad \Theta_{t_n}(p_n) \in U.
  \end{equation}
  If (equation eq:integral-condition of the paper) does not hold, then almost surely there exists $\tau > 0$ such that for all $t > \tau,$
  \begin{equation*}
    \max_{p \in \mathcal{P}_t} R_t(p) < 2t + \frac{d-2}{2} \log t + \beta_t.
  \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:31.956261+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d69d5ea816b2757391fe8b2600393965a70c29c691e71bdb9e164763e0e1810a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 12 of arXiv:2512.01483, 'Scaling Limits of Line Models in Degenerate Environment'] For $T>0$, let $\hat{X}^{(T)}(t) := (\hat{X}^{(T)}_1(t), \hat{X}^{(T)}_2(t))$, $t \geq 0$, where 
\[ \hat{X}^{(T)}_1(t) := \frac{1}{\sqrt{T}} \hat{X}^T_1(Tt), \quad \hat{X}^{(T)}_2(t) := T^{-\frac{1}{2\delta}} \hat{X}^T_2(Tt), \qquad t \geq 0. \]
Then, for $\mathbb{P}$-a.e. $\omega$, as $T \to \infty$, the process $(\hat{X}^{(T)}(t))_{t \geq 0}$ converges in distribution, in $D \times D$, to  
$\mathcal{X}(t) = (\mathcal{X}_1(t), \mathcal{X}_2(t))_{t \geq 0}$, where 
\begin{equation}

\mathcal{X}_1(t) = B_1(t), \quad \mathcal{X}_2(t) = B_2( \Delta^{-1}(t)), \qquad t \geq 0,
\end{equation}
where $B_1$ and $B_2$ are two independent Brownian motions with diffusion coefficient $\sqrt{2}$,  $\Delta(t) = \int_{\mathbb{R}} L^{B_2}_t(x) \, \mathcal{H}(dx)$, $t \geq 0$, and $(\Delta^{-1}(t))_{t \geq 0}$ denotes the inverse of $(\Delta(t))_{t \geq 0}$ (note that, by Lemma \ref{lem:delta_increasing}, the paths of $(\Delta(t))_{t \geq 0}$ are strictly increasing). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.302713+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d6a8e1ffb4076e87f7ffd2971e5083121dadb7f49bb6c47f150956a7f2ffe1b8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Th:simulation_stable of arXiv:2412.06374, 'Stable and tempered stable distributions and processes: an overview toward trajectory simu'] Let $\alpha \in (0,2)$ and  $U,V$ be two independent random variables such that $U\sim \mathcal{U}\left([-\frac\pi2, \frac\pi2] \right)$ and $V\sim \mathcal{E}(1).$
