{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.899250+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d6ba976ca336e952699c9b988ba941201fa47ef3968cef7cd3c49cae946c2e0c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 21 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Given an equilibrium measure $\boldsymbol{\mu}$, we will use the following terminology for each $h \in [H]$.
\begin{itemize}
\item[$\bullet$] A \emph{void} is a maximal (for the inclusion) and closed interval in $[\hat{a}'_h,\hat{b}'_h]$ where
$\mu_h(x) =0$.
\item[$\bullet$] A \emph{saturation} is a maximal closed interval in
$[\hat{a}'_h,\hat{b}'_h]$ where $\mu_h(x) = \frac{1}{\theta_{h,h}}$.
\item[$\bullet$] A \emph{band} is a maximal open interval in $[\hat a'_h,\hat b'_h]$ where $0<\mu_h(x)<\frac{1}{\theta_{h,h}}$.
\end{itemize}
We denote by $\amsmathbb{V}_{h}$ (respectively $\amsmathbb{S}_{h}$, $\amsmathbb{B}_{h}$) the union of voids (respectively saturations, bands) in $[\hat{a}'_{h},\hat{b}'_{h}]$. If we speak of a \emph{saturated segment} (respectively, a \emph{void segment}), we mean a segment included in a saturation (respectively, in a void) but not necessarily maximal.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.122575+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d6c7bc9892c8bf2fe0880dfc98f76c064772e44ccf09699f66955182e1ba2c43","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:irreducibilegraphs of arXiv:2510.00748, '\bf On irreducible central limit theorems'] {\rm Let $G_n = (V_n, \mathcal{E}_n)$, $n\geq 1$, be a sequence of graphs such that $|V_n|, |{\ensuremath{\mathcal E}} _n|\to \infty$, and write $A_n$ for the adjacency matrix of $G_n$. Consider the sequence $\{Z_n\}$ defined as in (equation eq:bilformsubsec of the paper) by identifying $A_n$ with the indicator $\mathds{1}_{E_n}$ of a symmetric and non-diagonal set $E_n \subset V_n\times V_n$. We refer to $\{Z_n\}$ as the {\em sequence of homogeneous sums generated by} $\{G_n\}$. We say that $\{G_n\}$ {\em generates an irreducible CLT}, if $\{Z_n\}$ verifies an irreducible CLT in the sense of Definition (def:irreducibility of the paper) --- that is, if $\{Z_n\}$ is irreducible and 
 \begin{equation}
\tilde{Z}_n:= \frac{Z_n}{\sqrt{2|E_n|}} \xlongrightarrow{d} \, N\sim  {\ensuremath{\mathcal N}}  \big(0,1).
\end{equation}
By extension, if the sequence $\{Z_n\}$ is reducible, we will say that $\{G_n\}$ {\em is reducible} or, more precisely, that $\{G_n\}$ {\em generates a reducible CLT}. Similar notions for hypergraphs and associated sequences of homogeneous sums will be introduced in Definition (def:irreducibileHG of the paper).
 }","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.131138+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d6d9c9003e5e336b0aa47e165f58bd9f4b78f1a1246ea3e0098fe423c8a65f99","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn HomProj of arXiv:2506.18207, 'Cartan's Path Development, the Logarithmic Signature and a Conjecture of Lyons-Sidorova'] For each $m\geqslant1$, we define $D_{m}:T((\mathbb{R}^{2}))\rightarrow T((\mathbb{R}^{2}))$
to be linear operator induced by 
\[
D_{m}({\rm e}_{j_{1}}\otimes\cdots\otimes{\rm e}_{j_{n}})\triangleq\begin{cases}
{\rm e}_{j_{1}}\otimes\cdots\otimes{\rm e}_{j_{n}}, & \#\{l:j_{l}=2\}=m;\\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]
In other words, $D_{m}$ projects a tensor series onto the sub-series
whose components have precisely $m$ of ${\rm e}_{2}$'s.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:36:49.894610+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d6dcfb9f8be1395909aa784197b5f9055d7e05db11897c1e80d36962891dd743","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Haar of arXiv:2609.00336, 'Critical points of point charge potentials along lines'] Let $p>0$, $\{(a_j,|b_j|)\}_{j=1,\ldots,N} \subset \mathbb{R}\times[0,+\infty)$ pairwise distinct, and set
		\[
		R_j(x)=(x-a_j)^2+b_j^2.
		\]
		Then, any nonzero linear combination of the $2N$ functions
		\begin{equation}
			R_j(x)^{-(p+2)/2},\qquad
			(x-a_j)R_j(x)^{-(p+2)/2},\qquad j=1,\ldots,N,
		\end{equation}
		has at most $2N-1$ distinct zeroes in $D=\mathbb{R}\backslash\{a_j \ | \ b_j=0\}$. In particular, when all $b_j>0$, the functions in (equation eq:Haar-family of the paper) form a Haar system on $\mathbb{R}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:21:56.724562+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d6e311028410843dea4150abdb6677b164c4326f7e74463afc07520b58ad8671","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:interior_intersection_right_first of arXiv:2507.15589, 'Tightness of approximations to metrics on non-simple conformal loop ensemble gaskets'] Fix $a_1 >0$, $p \in (0,1)$. Suppose that we have the setup of Section~(se:bubble_setup of the paper) with $(D,x_0,y_0) = (\delta\mathbf{D},-i\delta,i\delta)$. Let $z_1,z_2,w_1 \in \partial B(0,\delta/2)$ (where $z_1=z_2$ is allowed). Let $\eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2}$ (resp.\ $\eta_{z_2}^{\theta_2}$) be the angle $\theta_2$ flow lines starting from $z_1$ (resp.\ $z_2$), stopped upon exiting $B(0,3\delta/4)$. Let $\eta_{w_1}^R$ be the angle $-\pi/2$ flow line starting from $w_1$, stopped upon exiting $B(0,3\delta/4)$. Let $\eta_{w_1}^L$ be the angle $+\pi/2$ flow line starting from $w_1$, stopped upon hitting $\eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2}$ or exiting $B(0,3\delta/4)$ (whichever occurs first). In case $\eta_{w_1}^L$ intersects $\eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2}$ in $B(0,\delta/4)$, let $\overline{w}$ denote the intersection point, and let $\overline{\eta}^R$ be the angle $-\pi/2$ flow line starting from $\overline{w}$, stopped upon exiting $B(0,\delta/4)$. In case $\overline{\eta}^R$ and $\eta_{z_2}^{\theta_2}$ intersect, sample the internal metric in the regions bounded between them. 
Let $E^p$ be the event that the following hold.
\begin{itemize}
\item $\eta_{w_1}^L$ intersects the left side of $\eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2}$ with an angle difference of $-3\pi/2 - \theta_2$, and they do not intersect in any other way.
\item The right side of $\overline{\eta}^R$ and the left side of $\eta_{z_2}^{\theta_2}$ intersect with an angle difference of $-\pi/2 - \theta_2$, and they do not intersect in any other way.
\item There exist $x',x,y,y' \in \eta_{z_2}^{\theta_2} \cap \overline{\eta}^R$ such that $(x,y) \in \intptsapproxbubble{U_{x',y'}}{\epsilon}$ as in~(equation eq:intpts_bubble of the paper) and $U_{x',y'} \subseteq B(0,\delta^{1+a_1})$ and $\metapproxacres{\epsilon}{U_{x',y'}}{x}{y}{\Gamma} \geq \median{\epsilon}+\epsilon^{-a_0}\mathbf{a}_{\epsilon}$.
\item The following event has conditional probability at least $p$ given $\eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2}$, $\eta_{z_2}^{\theta_2}$, $\eta_{w_1}^R$, $\eta_{w_1}^L$, $\overline{\eta}^R$, $X_\delta^{\theta_2}$, $X_\delta^{-\pi/2}$, $X_\delta^{+\pi/2}$, and the values of $h$ on these sets:
\begin{itemize}
\item The flow line $\eta_2$ merges into $\eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2}$ before entering $B(0,\delta/4)$, and then (in case $\eta_{z_1}^{\theta_2}, \eta_{z_2}^{\theta_2}$ do not merge) into $\eta_{z_2}^{\theta_2}$ before entering $B(0,\delta/4)$ the next time.
\item The extension of $\overline{\eta}^R$ merges into the right side of the extension of $\eta_{w_1}^R$ after both exit $B(0,3\delta/4)$.
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
Then $\mathbf{P}[E^p] = O(\delta^{{\alpha_{\mathrm{apr}}}})$ as $\delta \to 0$.","status":"unverified"}

