{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:01:39.421206+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d77f8f9bd714785f55869cfc1f9b44cd839fc09435ecd3ba16761ee54a555a2a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition Graph of colored components of arXiv:2607.22156, 'A random matrix approach to lamplighter groups'] \begin{enumerate}
            \item Let $T$ be a *-test graph in the variables $\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{x_{1}}\cup\cdots\cup\mathbf{x_{p}}$, where the $\mathbf{x_{j}}$’s are families of pairwise disjoint variables (a variable appears at most in one family). A colored component of $T$ with respect to $\mathbf{x_{1}},\cdots,\mathbf{x_{p}}$ is a maximal connected sub-graph of $T$, with at least one edge, whose edges are labeled by variables in only one family among $\mathbf{x_{1}},\cdots,\mathbf{x_{p}}$. We denote by $\mathcal{CC}(T)$ the set of colored components of T with respect to $\mathbf{x_{1}},\cdots,\mathbf{x_{p}}$.
            \item The \emph{graph of colored components} of $T$ with respect to $\mathbf{x_{1}},\cdots,\mathbf{x_{p}}$, denoted $\mathcal{GCC}(T)$, is the following bipartite undirected graph.
            \begin{itemize}
                \item The first kind of vertices are the colored components $T_{1},\cdots, T_{K}$ of $\mathcal{CC}(T)$.
                \item The second kind of vertices are the vertices $v_1,\cdots,v_{L}$ of $T$ that belong to at least two graphs among $T_{1},\cdots, T_{K}$.
                \item There is an edge between $T_{i}$ and $v_j$ if $v_j$ is a vertex of $T_i$, $i=1,\cdots,K,\,j=1,\cdots, L$.
            \end{itemize}
        \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.395228+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d7837fd756c0c88a2ddf3b6ebfa18cbe2220bd01a0a667d15f2dede7bf41070e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem graph matrix of arXiv:2412.07936, 'Simple Norm Bounds for Polynomial Random Matrices via Decoupling'] For any shape $\tau$ and $2 \leq t \leq \infty$,
