{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.901282+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8d785e5e18761e97d0c7e492d0f73599aff1f60b06530e893f0c020adc687c1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma normbound:hadamard of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] Let $\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_d$ be a sequence of positive numbers.
	Set $\boldsymbol{\lambda} := (\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_d)$ and $\lambda_{\max} := \max_{i \in [d]} \lambda_i$.
	Let $q \in [-\infty, \infty]$ and $\widetilde{\mathbf{H}}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{(q)} \in \R^{d \times d}$ 
	be the matrix defined by
	\begin{equation*}
		(\widetilde{\mathbf{H}}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{(q)})_{ij} 
		:= 
		\mathcal{M}_q (\lambda_i, \lambda_j)
		\quad
		\text{ for }
		i,j \in [d],
	\end{equation*}
	where $\mathcal{M}_q$ is the power mean as in Definition (def:powermean of the paper). Recall $c_q$ from (eq:c_q:def of the paper), i.e., for $q \in [-\infty, \infty]$,
	\begin{equation*}
	c_q =
	\begin{cases}
	1, & \text{ if } q \in [-\infty,1], \\
	2^{2-1/q}-1, & \text{ if } q \in (1,\infty), \\
	3, & \text{ if } q = \infty.
	\end{cases}
	\end{equation*}
	Then for any unitarily invariant norm ${\left\vert\kern-0.25ex\left\vert\kern-0.25ex\left\vert \cdot 
    \right\vert\kern-0.25ex\right\vert\kern-0.25ex\right\vert}$ on $\R^{d \times d}$ and any matrix $\mathbf{B} \in \R^{d \times d}$, it holds that
	\begin{equation*}
	\vertiii{ \widetilde{\mathbf{H}}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{(q)} \circ \mathbf{B} }
	\le c_q \lambda_{\max}\, \vertiii{ \mathbf{B} }
	\leq
	\begin{cases}
	\lambda_{\max}\, \vertiii{ \mathbf{B} }, & \text{ if } q \in [-\infty,1], \\
	3 \lambda_{\max}\, \vertiii{ \mathbf{B} }, & \text{ if } q \in (1, \infty].
	\end{cases}
	\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

