{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.049511+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d902c70152cc26e3cc47479f55bb7325b44ba8a7418379d0a20a29737f58c3bb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:metastability of arXiv:2608.25279, 'Provable Non-Acceleration of Standard Strang\\ Splittings of Kinetic Langevin Dynamics'] Suppose Assumptions~(ass:cycle of the paper) and~(ass:monodromy of the paper)
hold, and let $R\ge1$, $j\in\mathbb Z$, $n\ge1$,
$\varepsilon_0\ge0$, $\delta'>0$, the sequences
$(Y_k)_{k\ge-1}$ and $(\zeta_i)_{i\ge1}$ satisfying the
recursion (equation eq:abstract-recursion of the paper), and the event
$\mathcal G$ in (equation eq:good-general of the paper) be as above. Assume
\begin{equation}
 C\varepsilon_0+G_0\delta'\le\frac{a}{2},
\end{equation}
where $C$ is the constant fixed after
Assumption~(ass:monodromy of the paper), and $G_0$ and $a$ are defined in
(equation eq:impulsegain-general of the paper) and (equation eq:tuberadius-general of the paper).
Then the following conclusions hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\rm(i)] On $\mathcal G$, $\abs{Y_k-Rx_{j+k}^\circ}\le aR/2$ for $0\le k\le n$.
\item[\rm(ii)] Let $(Y_k^{(j)})$ and $(Y_k^{(j+1)})$ be sequences as above with consecutive indices $j$ and $j+1$. Then
\begin{equation*}
 \norm{\operatorname{Law}(Y_n^{(j)})-\operatorname{Law}(Y_n^{(j+1)})}_{\mathrm{TV}}
 \ge1-\mathbb P(\mathcal G_j^c)-\mathbb P(\mathcal G_{j+1}^c).
\end{equation*}
The same lower bound holds for the laws of random elements $Z_n^{(j)}$ and $Z_n^{(j+1)}$ if there is a common measurable map $f$ such that $Y_n^{(r)}=f(Z_n^{(r)})$ for $r\in\{j,j+1\}$.
\item[\rm(iii)] Suppose $E_0=0$ and
\begin{equation*}
 \sup_{i\ge1}\mathbb P\bigl(\lvert \zeta_i\rvert>\delta'R\bigr)\le Ae^{-bR^2},
\end{equation*}
where $A,b>0$ are independent of $R$, $n$, and $i$. Then $\mathbb P(\mathcal G^c)\le Ane^{-bR^2}$. If the sequences with consecutive indices $j$ and $j+1$ both satisfy these assumptions, then
\begin{equation*}
 \norm{\operatorname{Law}(Y_n^{(j)})-\operatorname{Law}(Y_n^{(j+1)})}_{\mathrm{TV}}\ge1-2Ane^{-bR^2}.
\end{equation*}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.304295+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d90709fb6e670e47706c1075b77a5c11e71ffdc1deb6cd75aaf0ecdf73434dda","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 52 (Backwards-time propagation of test functions in $d=2$) of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] Let $d=2$. Assume $\x_N=(x^1_N,x^2_N)$ is any sequence such that $N^{-1/2}x_N^1 \to x_1$ and $x^1_N-x^2_N$ remains bounded, and consider the same setting as Assumption \ref{ass:SRI}. 
      Consider smooth bounded functions $\phi_1, ..., \phi_m : \mathbb R^d\to \mathbb R$ with globally bounded and continuous first and second partial derivatives, and consider $f^1,..., f^m : I\to \mathbb R$ of exponential decay at infinity. Then \begin{equation} 
    \lim_{N\to\infty} \Ebbnn \bigg[ \frac1{(\log N)^m}\sum_{0\leq r_1 < \ldots < r_m \leq Nt} \prod_{\ell =1}^m f^{\ell}(   R^1_{r_{\ell}}-   R^2_{r_{\ell}})\phi_{\ell}(N^{-1/2} (R^1_{r_\ell}-   s_N r_\ell))\bigg] = \prod_{\ell = 1}^m \bigg( \phi_\ell (x_1) \int_I f^\ell \; \dr \pi^{\mathrm{inv}} \bigg)\end{equation}
    The claim remains true if we replace $(f^1,...,f^m)$ on the left side by some sequence $(f^1_N,...,f^m_N)$ converging uniformly on compact sets as $N\to\infty$ to some $(f^1,...,f^m)$, as long as $\sup_{N} |f^j_N|$ decays exponentially fast at infinity for $j=1,...,m$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.729421+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d911276b8f03a732127e5bcadb19c89ab5eec2f087bff8a8099b17a78bc2c94a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma holderlemmas of arXiv:2608.01279, 'Stochastic heat equation with nondegenerate Hölder diffusion coefficient: uniqueness below'] Let $G$ satisfy the assumptions in Theorem  (thm:vector-valued of the paper). Then we can find a constant \(L_G<\infty\) depending only on $G$ such that,
