{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:31.974638+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9666e1c06e26f660168f83096f79ed497dcbd89f9784729a64704199f21c40b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2606.11142, 'Strong invariance principles for diffusions, Markov chains and their perturbations.'] For transition densities $p^h, p^h_\epsilon$ of Markov chains there exists a constant $C_1(q) > 0$ such that for any $t_i < t_j$ and $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$:
\begin{equation} 
\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} |p^h(t_i, t_j, x, y) - p^h_\epsilon(t_i, t_j, x, y)| dy \le C_1 \Delta_{\epsilon,\gamma,q}.
\end{equation}
For transition densities $p, p_\epsilon$ of diffusions there also exists a constant $C_2(q) > 0$ such that:
