{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.268796+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9aa8adc22690d3ecdb6a5a9ebe04064d8c83e94937b714f59db81a15f5e6e5f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def: solution of arXiv:2411.05924, 'Non-negative Martingale Solutions to the Stochastic Porous Medium Equation with Sticky Beh'] A tuple $((\Omega, \mathcal F, \mathbb F, \mathbb P), u, W)$, where $(\Omega, \mathcal F, \mathbb F, \mathbb P)$ is a stochastic basis supporting a $U$-valued $\mathbb F$-adapted $Q$--Wiener process $W$ and an $\mathbb F$-adapted process $u$, is called a \emph{non-negative martingale solution to the stochastic porous medium equation} (equation eq: intro1 of the paper)--(equation eq: intro3 of the paper) if
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item $u \in \bigcap_{\delta\in(0,1)} \Xi_{\mathfrak u}^\delta$;
        \item $u(t,x) \geq 0$ $\mathbb P \otimes \mathrm d t \otimes \mathrm d x$-a.s.;
        \item for all $\varphi \in C^3_c([0,1],\mathbb R)$, $\mathbb P \otimes \mathrm d t$-a.s.~we have that 
        \begin{align}
            \nonumber
            &\langle \varphi, u(t) \rangle = \left\langle \varphi, u_0 \right\rangle + \int_0^t \left\langle \partial_x^2 \varphi, u^\alpha(r) \right\rangle \mathrm d r \\

            &\qquad+ \int_0^t \langle \varphi, \1_{u(r)>0} B(u(r)) \mathrm d W(r) \rangle + \int_0^t \langle \varphi, \1_{u(r)=0} R(u(r)) \rangle \mathrm d r.
        \end{align}
    \end{enumerate}
    Furthermore, let $\mathcal S: L^2(\mathcal Q_T) \rightarrow \mathbb R$ be a functional given by
    \begin{equation}

        \mathcal S(u) := \iint_{\mathcal Q_T} \1_{u(t,x)=0} \mathrm d t \mathrm d x.
    \end{equation}
    We say that the system (equation eq: intro1 of the paper)--(equation eq: intro3 of the paper) exhibits \textit{sticky behavior} if
    \begin{equation}

        \mathbb P\left( \mathcal S( u) > 0 \right) > 0.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:37:14.890199+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9b9246299e68a57ecae46155f35e225cee97a8336f21be797919f238914daa1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2607.11608, '2607.11608'] Let  $n\in \N$ and $M>0.$ Let $h_n$ as in \eqref{EIGEN} and $f_M$ as in \eqref{fM}. Then, for any $M\gg 1$ the following hold:
