{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.230968+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc6a048c1c0b868d1386490d5a3983767a52e90cb78a7dc7f968805f5faf4390","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 23 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] For a down-right path $\lambda=(z_i)_{i\in \Z} \in \Lambda$ and a function $h_0 \colon \lambda \rightarrow \R \cup \{-\infty\}$ we define the \textbf{last passage percolation height function} $\hL$ with initial condition $h_0$ on $\lambda$ by
\begin{equation}
    \hL_{\lambda}(h_0;x,y) := \sup_{u \in \lambda} \big( h_{0}(u) + T_{u,(x,y)}\big) 
\end{equation} for all $x,y \in \Z$ with $(x,y) \succeq u $ for some $u\in \lambda$. . The \textbf{periodic height function} $\hLp_{\lambda}$ is defined as in \eqref{eq:LPPRecursion}, but with respect to the periodic last passage times $\Tper$ from Definition~\ref{def:LastPassageTimes} and a down-right path $\lambda=\lambda^{\per}_N \in \Lambda_{N,k}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:00:46.843077+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc6a1aeab3247a3ff79e42527181d60b1e10074609ea7942642a67a8aec55118","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 of arXiv:2608.13631, 'The classical discrete laws near the mode:\\ complete local expansions for the negative bi'] Let $t_c$ be the vertex of the quadratic part of $A_1$ of \eqref{eq:hyp-Am}.  Then
	\begin{equation}
	\begin{gathered}
		t_c=-\frac{(N-2n)(N-2K)}{2N^{2}},\\
		\mu+t_c+\tfrac12-\frac{(n+1)(K+1)}{N+2}
		=-\frac{(2K-N)(2n-N)}{N^{2}\,(N+2)} .
	\end{gathered}
	\end{equation}
	and the discrepancy satisfies
	\[
		\Bigl|\mu+t_c+\tfrac12-\frac{(n+1)(K+1)}{N+2}\Bigr|<\frac1{N+2} .
	\]
	Consequently, whenever $(n+1)(K+1)/(N+2)\notin\Z$, the unit window
	$\bigl(\mu+t_c-\tfrac12,\,\mu+t_c+\tfrac12\bigr]$ contains exactly one integer and it is
	the mode \eqref{eq:hyp-mode-classical}; when $(n+1)(K+1)/(N+2)\in\Z$ the window boundary
	falls within $1/(N+2)$ of the tied pair, and the integer it selects is one of the two
	modes.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.344989+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc6db74f7b935a655e643c85ff8be10c81c1ac312f17f2496ff993420338dc63","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 29 of arXiv:2607.14948, 'Graph alignment in sparse inhomogeneous models via self-overlap'] Let $x \geq 0$. Assume that, for any $\eta > 0$ and $\pi_0 \in \Sym_n$, there exist $\gamma > 0$, a random subset $\Neg \subseteq \Sym_n$, and a function $F: B(\pi_0, x - \eta) \setminus \Neg \to \mc{P}(\Sym_n)$ verifying the following conditions.

    \begin{enumerate}
        \item if $\pi \in \Dom F$, $|F(\pi)| \geq e^{\gamma n \log n}$;
        \item if $\pi \in \Dom F$ and $\pi' \in F(\pi)$, $\Proba(\pi'| G, H, (p_e)) \geq e^{-o_{\Proba}(n \log n)} \Proba(\pi| G, H, (p_e))$;
        \item if $\pi' \in \Sym_n$, $|F^{-1}(\pi')| \leq e^{o_\Proba(n \log n)}$ (uniformly over $\pi'$);
        \item $\Proba(\pi^* \in \Neg) = o(1)$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, if $\hat{\pi}$ is an estimator of $\pi^*$, there exist at least $x(n - o_\Proba(n))$ vertices $v \in [n]$ such that $\hat{\pi}(v) \neq \pi^*(v)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.680336+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc6e3a7d6acaac9df849bad858ec359b6dd0756484c29a8d51c2457dee7f14e9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l.moments-cumulants of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] For a fixed  collection of integers $k_1,\ldots,k_p$, $p\ge2$,
 assume that  for all $J\subset[p]$,
 \begin{equation}

 \sup_{(x_j)_{j \in J} \in\mathbb{R}^{d|J|}} 
 m^{(k_j:j \in J) }( x_j : j \in J)<\infty.
 \end{equation}

 The following statements are equivalent:
 \begin{enumerate}[wide,label=(\roman*),  labelindent=0pt]
\item There exist fast decreasing functions $\tilde\phi=\tilde\phi_{k_1,\ldots,k_p}$
and constants
	$\tilde C=C(k_1,\ldots,k_p)$, such that
  for any subset  of integers  $J\subset [p]$, $|J|\ge2$, a nonempty, proper subset
  $I\subsetneq J$, and all configurations $\{x_j:  j \in J \}, x_j \in\mathbb{R}^d$, of distinct points
	we have

\begin{equation}
	\Bigl|m^{(k_j : j\in J)}(x_j : j\in J)-
	m^{(k_j: j\in I)}(x_j:j\in I)\, m^{(k_j: j\in J\setminus I)}(x_j:j\in
	 J\setminus I)\Bigr|\le
	\tilde C\tilde\phi(s)\,,
	\end{equation}

	where  $s:=d\bigl(\{x_j:j\in I\}, \{x_j:j\in  J\setminus I\}\bigr)$.
      \item  	 There exist fast decreasing functions $\tilde\phi_\top=\tilde\phi_\top^{k_1,\ldots,k_p}$ and constants
	$\tilde C^\top=\tilde C^\top(k_1,\ldots,k_p)$, 
        such that for any subset  of integers $ J\subset [p]$, $|J|\ge2$ and all configurations  $x_j\in\mathbb{R}^d$, $j\in J$ of distinct points
	we have

	\begin{equation}
	|m_{\top}^{(k_j:j\in J)}(x_j : j\in J)|\le
	\tilde
	C^\top\tilde\phi_\top\bigl(\mathrm{diam}(x_j : j\in J)\bigr)
	\,,
	\end{equation}

where $\mathrm{diam}(x_j : j\in J):=\max_{i,j\in J}(|x_i-x_j|)$.
 \end{enumerate}
 Moreover, the bound~(equation e.Usell-clustering-bound of the paper)  for $J=[p]$ implies
 	\begin{equation}
\sup_{x_1\in \mathbb{R}^d}\int_{
(\mathbb{R}^d)^{(p-1)}}|m_\top^{\bk k1p}(\bk x1p)|\,\mathrm{d} x_2\cdots \mathrm{d} x_p< \infty.
	\end{equation}

Further, $\tilde\phi_\top$ and $\tilde C^\top$ in (ii) depend only on $\tilde \phi$, $\tilde C$, the dimension $d$ and the bound in (equation e.moments-uniformly-bounded of the paper) and similarly the $\tilde\phi$ and $\tilde C$ in (i) depend only on $\tilde \phi_\top$, $\tilde C^\top$ and the bound in (equation e.moments-uniformly-bounded of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.279095+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc717c26529da11c9b91c5dade6a8d64deadca72b7366ad89b69431fbacb9211","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition pro: Lp norm of q_infty of arXiv:2603.14163, '\LARGE \bf Tail Bounds for Queues with Abandonment: Constant, Moderate, Large Deviations, '] \textnormal{($L^p$ norm of queue length)} For SSQ, under assumption (ass:heavy_overload of the paper), the $L^p$ norm of the centered queue length is bounded in the below interval
    \begin{align}
        \| \hat{q} \|_{L^p} &\leq \min\{(C_{1,\lambda,\mu}\sqrt{\frac{1}{\gamma}} \sqrt{p} + C_{2,\lambda,\mu} p),  (C_{3,\lambda,\mu} \frac{p}{\log(1+\gamma p/\lambda)})\} \\
        \| \hat{q} \|_{L^p}&\geq C_{4,\lambda,\mu} \exp\left( -C_{5,\lambda,\mu} \frac{1}{ \gamma p} \right) \frac{p}{\log(1+\gamma p/\lambda)} 
