where~$a_{\ell+1}=a_1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:18:19.444284+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dc9f4a5abe72d98da8181a609c63855e0f8d706741a85060d9f2a3a6cc3a67b3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2508.17838, '\bf Edge Universality for Inhomogeneous Random Matrices'] Given any fixed integers  $s\ge 1$ and  $t_i\ge 1$ for $1\le i\le s$, $1\le j\le t_i$, if  $\|A\|\le \sqrt{\alpha}+O(N^{-\frac{1}{3}})$ with $ \epsilon\le \alpha\le 1$ for some fixed $\epsilon>0$, and
    \begin{equation}
        \theta t_N\ll n_{i,j}\le  \tau N^{\frac{1}{3}}
    \end{equation}
    for any fixed constant $\tau$  such that   $t_1n_1+\cdots+t_kn_k$ is even, then   
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}\Big[\prod_{i=1}^{s}\mathrm{Tr} \prod_{j=1}^{t_i} (\frac{1}{n_{i,j}}Q_{n_{i,j}}(\frac{X}{2}))\Big]=\mathbb{E}\Big[\prod_{i=1}^{s}\mathrm{Tr} \prod_{j=1}^{t_i}(\frac{1}{n_{i,j}}Q_{n_{i,j}}(\frac{\widetilde{X}}{2}))\Big]+o(1).
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:25.272750+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dca60109d34adf93e149ea5a4dff4ecb978438dca055121c865d63df4299c49f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 14 of arXiv:2606.21111, 'On Elephant Random Walk with Delayed Amnesia'] Let $0\le p<(4a-1)/4a$. Then,
{\small\begin{equation*}
\lim_{n\to \infty}\frac{1}{\log n}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\frac{S_k^2}{k^2}=\frac{1}{(2a(1-p)-1)^2}\Big(\frac{(a(2p-1))^2}{4a(1-p)-1}+\frac{(1-2p)(a-1)}{1-p}+(a-1)^2\Big)\ \ \text{a.s.}
\end{equation*}}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:02.087910+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dca72a92585b1581dee451aee14ebe4aae59fc8cb9e9b08156eb3086f4bae891","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 of arXiv:2604.25769, 'The conformal dimension of the Brownian tree is one'] Let $x \in \SCT$ and $n \in \BN$. Then we shall write 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\varrho(x, n) \defeq \eta + 2\sup\{\sigma(y, n) : y \in \SCT \text{ with } d_\SCT(x, y) \le 26\alpha^n\}$ (cf.~\Cref{lem:core});
        \item $\varsigma(x, n) \defeq \eta + 64\alpha \cdot \mathbf 1_{\widetilde E(x, n)}$; 
        \item $\varpi(x, n) \defeq \prod_{j = 1}^n \varsigma(x, j)$. 
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.620442+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcb0195426e52b415ff9e873c49d2eed69b05e85738354f28ea0013a2c600f66","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop prop_free_boundary_equations of arXiv:2606.23648, '\textsc{Optimal Stopping for a Diffusion with Unobserved Bernoulli Drift}'] Recall the notation of (equation def_phi_2d of the paper), and let \(x\in\mathcal I\). If \(a(x)>-x\), then the free-boundaries $a(\cdot), b(\cdot)$ satisfy
    \begin{equation}
        \Phi(x,a(x))=\Phi(x,b(x)),
        \qquad
        \int_{a(x)}^{b(x)}
            \big(\Phi(x,z)-\Phi(x,a(x))\big)\,dG(z)=0.
    \end{equation}
    If \(a(x)=-x\), then the free-boundary $b(\cdot)$ satisfies
    \begin{equation}
        \int_{-x}^{b(x)}
            \big(\Phi(x,z)-\Phi(x,b(x))\big)\,dG(z)=0.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.073225+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcb2995fce134e0d99f51d816d4759bdb3399f562df4a9cadbe22b39db6c52dd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:shol=3term of arXiv:2509.08928, 'The near critical random bond Ising model via embedding deformation'] Let $\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{S}_\mathcal{X}$ be a proper $s$-embedding and $F$ an $s$-holomorphic on $\diamondsuit(G)$. Given $z\in\diamondsuit(G)$, a corner \mbox{$c\in\Upsilon^\times(G)$} belonging to the quad $z$, one can define the spinor $X$ at $c \in \Upsilon^\times $ by the formula 
\begin{align}
X(c)\ &:=\ |\mathcal{S}(v^\bullet(c))-\mathcal{S}(v^\circ(c))|^{\frac{1}{2}}\cdot\operatorname{Re}[\overline{\eta}_c F(z)] \notag\\
 &=\ \operatorname{Re}[\overline{\varsigma}\mathcal{X}(c)\cdot F(z)]\ =\ \overline{\varsigma}\mathcal{X}(c)\cdot \mathrm{Proj}[F(z);\eta_c\mathbb{R}].

\end{align}
The map $c\mapsto X(c) $ satisfies all three terms identities  (equation eq:3-terms of the paper) around the quad $z$. Conversely given $X:\Upsilon^\times(G)\to\mathbb{R}$ a real valued solution to (equation eq:3-terms of the paper), there exists a unique s-holomorphic function $F$ on $\diamondsuit(G)$ such that the identity (equation eq:X-from-F of the paper) holds. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.797235+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcb4fcab3afa066d22799410c43c81cbb2aea4bd72cc7ed3531d694b97d64602","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 54 (Euler--Lagrange equation for the unconstrained model) of arXiv:2604.00635, 'Large deviations of the periodic Toda chain'] Let $\nu_\ell$ be the minimiser of the rate function $I$ \eqref{ratefunction}  in the space $\mathcal{M}_1(\mathbb{R})$. Then $\nu_\ell$ is absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure and there is a constant $C_\ell \in \mathbb{R}$ such that its density $\frac{\mathrm{d\nu_\ell}}{\mathrm{d}x}$ satisfies
    \begin{align}
       &V(x) - \ln\Big( 1 + \frac{2}{\ell} \Int{\mathbb{R}}{}\ln|x-y| \, \mathrm{d}\nu_\ell(y) \Big)  -\Int{\mathbb{R}}{} \frac{\frac{2}{\ell} \ln|x-y|}{1 + \frac{2}{\ell} \Int{\mathbb{R}}{}\ln|y-z| \, \mathrm{d}\nu_\ell(z)}  \, \mathrm{d}\nu_\ell(y) + \ln \frac{\mathrm{d\nu_\ell}}{\mathrm{d}x} = C_\ell \, , \nonumber \\
    &\text{for } \mathrm{Leb}-\text{a.e. } x \in \mathbb{R}   \, . 
    \end{align}
where $1 + \frac{2}{\ell} \Int{\mathbb{R}}{}\ln|x-y| \, \mathrm{d}\nu_\ell(y) > 0$ Lebesgue almost everywhere. Furthermore, viewed as an equation in $\nu_\ell$, the solution is unique. More precisely, if there exists a constant $C_\ell$ and a measure $\nu$ such that \eqref{eulerlagrange} is satisfied (upon replacing $\nu_\ell$ by $\nu$) then in fact $\nu = \nu_\ell$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.254709+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcbe6333e025c42c3b170786b67dd8d0bcc4931299d76081f173e2899d71704e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 23 of arXiv:2605.23308, 'Convergence rate estimates for semigroups and heat kernels associated with resistance form'] For an isometric embedding between two compact metric spaces $f:X\to Z$, define $A_f\subseteq \calM(Z)_{\mathrm{fin}}$ by setting
\begin{align*}
  A_f:=\{\mu\circ f^{-1}:\mu\in \calM(X)_{\mathrm{fin}}\}.
\end{align*}
Let $f:X\to Z$ and $f_n:X_n\to Z,\;n\in\N,$ be isometric embeddings between compact metric spaces $(X,d^X),\;(X_n,d^{X_n})$ and $(Z,d^Z)$.
Suppose that $\lim_{n\to\infty}d^{Z}_{\mathrm{H}}(f(X),f_n(X_n))=0$. It is then the case that
\begin{align*}
  \lim_{n\to\infty}(d^{Z}_{\BL^{\kappa}})_{\mathrm{H}}(A_f,A_{f_n})= 0.
\end{align*}
Here, we denote the Hausdorff metric on $\calM(Z)_{\mathrm{fin}}$ with respect to $d^{Z}_{\BL^{\kappa}}$
by $(d^{Z}_{\BL^{\kappa}})_{\mathrm{H}}$.","status":"unverified"}

