{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.627864+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcd3b747b357783ad3490ae5816f407a9ded699022901f2a82044c2df465ffa2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Th th_original_threshold of arXiv:2606.23648, '\textsc{Optimal Stopping for a Diffusion with Unobserved Bernoulli Drift}'] Assume that the hypotheses of Theorem~(th_2d_structure of the paper) hold. Then
    \begin{equation}
        \mathcal C^0
        =
        \{x\in\mathcal I:\kappa(x)>0\}.
    \end{equation}
    In particular, for every \(x\notin\mathcal C^0\), immediate stopping is optimal and \(V(x)=k(x)\). For every \(x\in\mathcal C^0\), we have
    \begin{equation}
        V(x)=Q(x)G(0)+R(x),
    \end{equation}
    with the functions $Q$ and $R$ defined in (equation def_q_R_coefficients of the paper), and an optimal stopping time is
    \begin{equation}
        \tau_x^*
        =
        \inf\{t\ge0:X(t)\notin(x+a(x),\,x+b(x))\}.
    \end{equation}

    If, in addition, (ass_3 of the paper) holds, then the function $\kappa(\cdot)$ of (equation def_kappa_threshold of the paper) is non-decreasing on \(\mathcal I\), and \(\mathcal C^0\) is either empty or a right interval. More precisely, with
    \begin{equation}
        x^*
        \coloneqq
        \inf\{x\in\mathcal I:\kappa(x)>0\}
    \end{equation}
    and the convention \(\inf\emptyset=\infty\), immediate stopping is optimal for every \(0\le x<x^*\), while \(x\in\mathcal C^0\) holds for every \(x>x^*\).

    Finally, assume that \(J\subset\mathcal C^0\) is an open interval on which the relevant two-dimensional boundaries are of class \(C^1\). Then \(V\) is \(C^1\) on \(J\), with
    \begin{equation}
        V'(x)=Q'(x)G(0)+R'(x),
        \qquad x\in J.
    \end{equation}
    If \(x^*<\infty\), \(V(x^*)=k(x^*)\), and \((x^*,0)\) is a regular horizontal crossing point, then \(V\) satisfies smooth fit at the original threshold:
    \begin{equation}
        V'(x^*+)=k'(x^*).
    \end{equation}
    If \(x^*>0\), then \(V'(x^*-)=k'(x^*)\) holds as well, so \(V\) is continuously differentiable at
    \(x^*\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.541664+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcda29ae4a87540ce13b29db2a4fd6b15e51b10192ac0708326be04619f12696","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:limit_correlation_function of arXiv:2603.27906, 'Marked GUE-corners process in doubly periodic dimer models'] Let~$K_{\operatorname{Int}}$ be the correlation kernel given in Theorem~(thm:correlation_kernel of the paper), then,  with the notation introduced above,  for $\mu_1\not=\mu_2$, 
