{"assumptions":["S is a separable metric space","\mu_n \Rightarrow \mu weakly"],"created_at":"2026-10-07T19:28:52+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dcee3d2ef103c9bd3264b089aa143def","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"Let S be a separable metric space, and let \mu_n, \mu be Borel probability measures on S such that \mu_n \Rightarrow \mu weakly. Then there exists a single probability space (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}) and S-valued random variables X_n, X defined on \Omega such that the law of X_n is \mu_n for all n, the law of X is \mu, and X_n(\omega) \to X(\omega) as n \to \infty for \mathbb{P}-almost every \omega \in \Omega.

