{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:26:52.667395+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dd96b3855c93b98e605bc1d2bd17fa7b2a4050c924a35df9bb0231e646919b65","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 26 (sharp half-disk Dirichlet-to-Neumann profile) of arXiv:2609.03736, '2609.03736'] There is a continuous function $q$ on the unit circle such that, uniformly
in $\theta$,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\mathcal B(re^{\iu\theta})}{\log r}
 \longrightarrow q(e^{\iu\theta})
 \qquad(r\uparrow1).

\end{equation}
Because $\log r\sim r-1$, the function $q$ is the outward normal derivative
of $\mathcal B$ along the circular boundary.
On the open right semicircle, put
\begin{equation}
 a=\tan\!\left(\frac\pi4-\frac\theta2\right).
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
 q(e^{\iu\theta})=D(a),
 \qquad
 D(a)=\frac12-\frac{2a\log a}{\pi(a^2-1)},

\end{equation}
where $D$ is extended continuously at $a=1$.  The function $D$ has its
unique minimum at $a=1$, with $D(1)=\delta_\pi$.  At the two corners
$q(\pm\iu)=1/2$, and $q(-z)=q(z)$.  Consequently,
\begin{equation}
 \min_{|z|=1}q(z)=\delta_\pi,

\end{equation}
with equality exactly at $z=\pm1$.","status":"unverified"}

