{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:26:52.681243+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dd98ca73d07289bde5cc26e16847ff7981faa204072f3200e66aebecedc100e8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 5 of arXiv:2609.03777, '2609.03777'] The following statements are equivalent.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The map $\bar\delta$ preserves $\ker(G)$ and therefore induces a well-defined derivation on $\mes$, again denoted by $\delta$.
\item The evaluation map
\begin{align}
\operatorname{ev}_{G_2}:\mesadm[T]&\longrightarrow\mes,\\
T&\longmapsto G_2
\end{align}
is injective.
\item The Eisenstein series $G_2$ is transcendental over $\mesadm$.
\end{enumerate}
If these statements hold, then
\begin{align}
\mes=\mesadm[G_2]
\end{align}
freely and
\begin{align}
\delta=-\frac12\frac{\partial}{\partial G_2}\,.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.251691+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dd9ca3fdf19d67223f2c7a0a8d03a11ff1b0f1429669ab832d4425f82eeecc82","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 66 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Given $(\md,q,\omega,L)$ as above, we let $w_0$ be a point in $\mL_q$ satisfying the following conditions: 
  \begin{itemize}
  \item When $\bd=\md$, let $w_0 \in \mathfrak{L}_q$ be any point (which is not necessarily unique) satisfying 
	\begin{flalign}

		\mathfrak{d}_{w_0} =  \bd; \qquad L^{-1} \be \le \mathfrak{e}_{w_0;q} \le L \be
	\end{flalign} 
	so that, by \eqref{ede}, we have
        \begin{flalign}

        L^{-1} N^{2B\delta} \omega (qN)^{-1} \le \mathfrak{d}_{w_0} \mathfrak{e}_{w_0;q} \le L N^{2B\delta} \omega (qN)^{-1}.  
        \end{flalign}
      \item  When $\bd>\md$, let $w_0 \in \mathfrak{L}_q$ denote the point with
        $\md_{w_0}=\bd$, such that the orthogonal projection of $w_0$ to $\ell_{w_0}$ is the tangency point $\mathfrak{A}_q \cap \ell_{w_0}$. 

  \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:01:39.422889+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dda1ca6b86c48d3a2e245f836c02ecdd08686c37bd1e23ee5cebcf1d213b683b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:cumulant-convergence of arXiv:2607.22156, 'A random matrix approach to lamplighter groups'] Let $a_1,\ldots,a_n\in\mathcal{A}$ be cactus field-type monomials and let $a_{i,N}$ be their evaluations at $(V_N,V_N^*,\mathbf D_N)$. Then, we have
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{N\to\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\frac1N\mathrm{Tr}\left(\kappa_n^{\mathcal{B}_N}(a_{1,N},\ldots,a_{n,N})\right)\right]=\tau_G\left(\kappa_n^{\mathcal{B}_0}(\Theta(a_1),\ldots,\Theta(a_n))\right).
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:22:08.498882+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dda3befb260bda7aa81c586e17faf2272cb27accabf6e0bbb4920bd78a783f2e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:RBJ-H5 of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] The dressed observable satisfies the mixed diamond
equation
