{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.228028+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dda4061ba50806eeba60172833f6d1e066d6af6108d8b669a30f27242f52bdc1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma C-for-critical-def of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] For $f$ of the form (equation Form of f of the paper), 
\begin{equation}
\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}\int_0^\infty u_\phi(t, x+\sqrt{2}t)xe^{\sqrt{2} x} dx \to \mathbf{C}(f)
\end{equation}
as $t\to \infty$, where $\mathbf{C}(f)$ is a positive constant. Furthermore, for any fixed $x \in \mathbb{R}$, 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{C}(f(x+ \cdot)) = e^{\sqrt{2}x}\mathbf{C}(f).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.121796+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddb1fa3cc59551d1c7cfbcb066c99032f09a65b5e6d4d6ee0de3c6aca3b82093","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Conj ER of arXiv:2601.20502, 'Optimal matching under size priority'] Let $G_n$ be Erdös-Reny\'i graphs with parameters $(n, \frac{c}{n})$.
    Define $\mathcal{M}_{\max}(G_n)$ to be the set of maximum size matchings of $G_n$. Writing $\underline{\gamma}$ to be the smallest solution to $x=e^{-ce^{-cx}}$ and $\overline{\gamma}=e^{-c\underline{\gamma}}$, we then have the following:
        \begin{equation}
            \begin{aligned}
                \lim_{n \rightarrow +\infty} \mathbb{E}\left[ \frac{1}{|E(G_n)|} \sum_{e \in E(G_n)} \mathbbm{1}_{\forall \mathbb{M} \in \mathcal{M}_{\max}(G_n), e \in \mathbb{M}}\right] &= \underline{\gamma}^2, \\
                \lim_{n \rightarrow +\infty} \mathbb{E}\left[ \frac{1}{|E(G_n)|} \sum_{e \in E(G_n)} \mathbbm{1}_{\forall \mathbb{M} \in \mathcal{M}_{\max}(G_n), e \notin \mathbb{M}}\right] &= (1-\overline{\gamma})^2.
            \end{aligned}
        \end{equation} (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.317335+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddb834ed4c58ca3ad67154a2404e77f3226007d877d5a36380743c87e126324e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 of arXiv:2506.20470, 'Pivot probabilities and norm effects in Gaussian elimination for $\beta$-ensembles'] Let $A$ be a $2 \times 2$ GUE matrix and GEPP pivot comparisons use the $L^1$ norm (as in LAPACK). Then the pivot probability is
    \begin{equation}
    \P(\|A_{21}\|_1 > |A_{11}|) = \P(|Z_1| + |Z_2| > \sqrt 2 |Z_3|) = \frac23
\end{equation}
for $Z_i \sim N(0,1)$ iid. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.550471+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddba2adb5bb8fa063ea24c51576fb7e4b87bd35913299e5a170d4d7378912f71","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2604.01978, 'Homogenized Transformers'] Fix $d, n\ge 2$ and $\upbeta\in[0,1]$, and assume that $\langle x_i(0),x_j(0)\rangle\ge \gamma$ for $i\neq j$ and $\gamma\in(0, 1)$. Define the empirical mean
\begin{equation*} 
m(t)\coloneqq\frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n \langle x_i(t),x_j(t)\rangle.
\end{equation*}
Let $u$ be the unique solution to the  Cauchy problem for the logistic equation
\begin{equation} 
\begin{cases}
\dot{u}(t)=u(t)(1-u(t)) \\
u(0)=m(0).
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Then there exists a universal constant $C>0$ such that for every every $T>0$ and every $\delta\in(0,1)$, with probability at least $1-\delta$, the solution to \eqref{eq:Diffusive_gaussian_case} satisfies
\begin{equation}
\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\left|m(t)-u(t)\right|
\le Ce^{CT}\max\left(\frac{1}{d},\,d^{2}\sigma_{\bA}^{4}\upbeta^{2}\right)
+ C\sqrt{\frac{T}{d}\log\left(\frac{4}{\delta}\right)}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:03:59.238713+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddbc0389bc38ca1750b78b987bfcdd08e60f2a72653fcc7ff074f7a07acb8c35","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem_translate of arXiv:2507.06522, 'Balanced multi-species spin glasses'] Given $p,d\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geqslant1}$, let $N = p d$ and consider the multi-species model (equation MSKHamiltonian of the paper) with $\mathscr{S} = \{1,\dots,p\}$, $\# \mathcal{I}_{s,N} = d$ for each $s\in\mathscr{S}$, and 
\eeq{ 
\Delta^2_{s_1,\dots,s_q} = 0 \quad \text{whenever $q\ne p$}, \qquad
\Delta^2_{s_1,\dots,s_p} = \begin{cases}
\frac{p^{p+1}}{p!} &\text{if $s_1,\dots,s_p$ are distinct} \\
0 &\text{else.}
\end{cases}
}
Then the covariance function is
\eeq{ 
\xi_N(x_1,\dots,x_p) = px_1\cdots x_p, 
}
and we have the following distributional equality:
\eeq{ 
\frac{\|T\|_\mathrm{inj}}{\sqrt{d}} \stackrel{\mathrm{law}}{=} \max_{\sigma\in\Sigma_{N,\mathsf{spherical}}}\frac{H_{N}(\sigma)}{N}.
}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.075880+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ddc1544fa56ef26e22ac20b2f4c6140622d9580569d11520d5469a5890fd0e84","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 7. cor: GW tree satisfies the sub-aging assumption of arXiv:2412.08236, 'Aging and sub-aging for Bouchaud trap models on resistance metric spaces'] Define a probability measure $\tilde{p} = (\tilde{p}_{k})_{k \geq 1}$ on $\mathbb{N}$ 
