{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.678809+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dde1016d59346aeaab559f4d5a91b7612b55cf25870f009060b7e326a13d6c27","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def.Stab.Radius of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] Given  a marking  function $\xi :
W_n \times\mathbb{K}\times\hat{\mathcal{N}}_{W_n \times \mathbb{K} }\longrightarrow \mathbb{K}'$, 
 $n\in\mathbb{N} \cup \{\infty\}$ (as usual for $n=\infty$, we take $W_\infty:=\mathbb{R}^d$) and $(x,u)\in\tilde{\mu} \in\mathcal{N}_{W_n \times \mathbb{K}}$  we 
define the radius of stabilization $R_{W_n}^\xi((x,u);\tilde{\mu})$ 
to be the smallest $r \in \mathbb{N}$    such that
\begin{equation}
\xi((x,u), \tilde{\mu} \cap B_r(x)) = \xi((x,u), (\tilde{\mu} \cap B_r(x)) \cup ( \tilde \nu \cap B^c_r(x))) \,
\end{equation}
for  all $\tilde\nu\in \mathcal{N}_{W_n \times \mathbb{K}}$. The definition is naturally extended to $\tilde{\mu} \in\mathcal{N}_{\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{K}}$ by setting $R_{W_n}^\xi((x,u);\tilde{\mu}) := R_{W_n}^\xi((x,u);\tilde{\mu} \cap W_n)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.038983+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dde3ac7e05c235b4f0ae1ae9f34338df9484d527011d3515d9fb5bb54c9ff052","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 16 (\cite{MR2778606} Theorem 5.1.3) of arXiv:2606.26496, 'Homeomorphism of the Revuz correspondence under Dynkin class assumptions'] The quotient space of $\mathbf{A}_c^+$ under the equivalence relation and the family ${\sf S}$ are in one-to-one correspondence under the following relation: For $\mu \in {\sf S}$ and ${\sf A}\in\mathbf{A}_c^+$,
		 \begin{equation}
		 	\lim_{t \downarrow 0} \frac{1}{t}\int_E \mathbb{E}_x\left[  \int_{0}^{t} f(X_s)\ {\rm d}{\sf A}_s  \right]h(x)\mathfrak{m}({\rm d}x)=\int_E f(x) h(x)\mu({\rm d}x)
		 \end{equation}
		 where $h$ is any $\gamma$-excessive function and $f \in \mathscr{B}_+(E)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.331848+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_dde40e165ce82c55816ccfbf06bd9531d425265fe944b6f5032b7b248b8b2231","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2506.22040, '\vspace{-3em} Khinchin inequalities for uniforms on spheres with a deficit'] Let $p \geq 2$. Suppose that  $a_1 \geq b_1 \geq b_2 \geq a_2 > 0$ with $a_1^2 + a_2^2 = b_1^2 + b_2^2 = \sigma^2$ for some $\sigma > 0$. For every vector $v$ in $\R^d$, $d \geq 2$, we have
\begin{align}\notag
&\E\left|b_1\xi_1 + b_2\xi_2 + v\right|^p - \E\left|a_1\xi_1 + a_2\xi_2 + v\right|^p \\
&\qquad\qquad\geq \frac{1}{d}(a_1^4+a_2^4 - b_1^4-b_2^4)\cdot \begin{cases}\beta_{p,d}(|v|^2+\sigma^2)^{\frac{p-4}{2}}, & 2 \leq p \leq 4, \\ \frac{p(p-2)}{8}\E|v+\sigma\xi|^{p-4}, & p > 4, \end{cases}
\end{align}
with $\beta_{p,d}$ defined in \eqref{eq:beta-def}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.239992+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de02cf2180dceb2f89d6b854deda3874685365d128d0fbe5fc7e1fd630fa2b46","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 56 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] Suppose that $(\eta_t)_{t \geq 0}$ is an ASEP on $\Z$, and that $(\bar{\eta}^{\per}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ is an unwrapped periodic ASEP. Fix some $\rho \in [0,1]$ and $\theta \in \R$, which are allowed to depend on $N$. Set
\begin{equation}
    E_t := \left\{ \{ x, x+1 \} \colon x \in \mathcal{I}^{\rho}_{N}(\theta,\theta+1) \right\}  
\end{equation} for all $t\geq 0$. We define the $\boldsymbol{(\rho,\theta,1,N)}$\textbf{-reference frame basic coupling} as follows. 
For all edges, we place independent rate $(1+q)$ Poisson clocks. 
Whenever the clock of an edge $e=\{ x,x+1\}$ rings, and the exclusion processes are in states $\eta$ and $\eta^{\prime}$, respectively, we sample an independent Bernoulli-$(1+q)^{-1}$-distributed random variable $B$. If
\begin{itemize}
\item $B=1$ and $e\in E_t$. Then, if $\eta(x)=1-\eta(x+1)=1$ holds, we move the particle at site $x$ to site $x+1$ in configuration $\eta$. Similarly, if $\bar{\eta}^{\per}(x)=1-\bar{\eta}^{\per}(x+1)=1$ holds, we move for all $j\in \Z$ the particle at site $x+jN$ to site $x+jN+1$ in $\bar{\eta}^{\per}$. 
\item $B=0$ and $e\in E_t$. Then, if $\eta(x)=1-\eta(x+1)=0$ holds, we move the particle at site $x+1$ to site $x$ in configuration $\eta$. Similarly, if $\bar{\eta}^{\per}(x)=1-\bar{\eta}^{\per}(x+1)=0$ holds, we move for all $j\in \Z$ the particle at site $x+jN+1$ to site $x+jN$ in  $\bar{\eta}^{\per}$. 
\item $B=1$ and $e \notin E_t$. Then, if $\eta(x)=1-\eta(x+1)=1$ holds, we move the particle at site $x$ to site $x+1$ in configuration $\eta$.
\item $B=0$ and $e\notin E_t$. Then, if $\eta(x)=1-\eta(x+1)=0$ holds, we move the particle at site $x+1$ to site $x$ in configuration $\eta$.
\end{itemize}
In all other cases, the configuration remains unchanged.","status":"unverified"}

