{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.304091+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df1e1a64d777b01b590eedfda61f4bce15612bbad76cdb67099fb1715dd635ad","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 49 of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] Let $d \geq 1$ and consider the setting of Assumption \ref{ass:SRI}.
For any $t\ge 0$, and any $\phi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^d)$, we have that 
\begin{multline}\lim_{N\to\infty} N^{\frac{d-2}2} \sum_{r=0}^{Nt} \Ebbnn \big[\phi(N^{-1/2} (   R^1_r -    s_N r)\big)f_N(   R^1_r-   R^2_r) ] \\ =\bigg( \int_0^t \int_{\mathbb R^d} G(2s,x_1-x_2) G(2s,y) \phi(\tfrac12(y+x_1+x_2)) \dr y\dr s\bigg) c_d \int_I f \;\dr\pi^{\mathrm{inv}}
\end{multline}
whenever $N^{-1/2}\mathbf x_N \to \boldsymbol x= (   x_1,   x_2)\in \mathbb R^{2d},$ where $   x_{1}\ne    x_{2}$. Here $G$ appearing on the right side is the standard heat kernel on $\mathbb R^d$, that is $G(t,y) = (2\pi t)^{-d/2} e^{-|y|^2/(2t)}.$ Furthermore, $c_d$ and $\pi^{\mathrm{inv}}$ are as in Definition \ref{def:unit}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.478202+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df25b55ffafa1c2ca05017fb558de76a2d160178ff490772c5a16ea706b5e0f8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:spec-Borel of arXiv:2606.21391, 'Extreme points and faces in the moment problem'] Let $\mu$ be a Borel measure on a topological space $X$. Then $\mu$ is a 
\emph{Radon measure}

\footnote{Radon measures are also known as \emph{tight} measures [topsoe70].}

[schwartz73] if $\mu$ is inner regular on compact sets, that is to say, the 
measure $\mu(A)$ of any $A\in\mathcal{B}(X)$ is the supremum of $\mu(B)$ over all compact 
subsets $B\subset A$. The measure $\mu$ is \emph{$\tau$-smooth}

\footnote{It is well known that every Radon measure is $\tau$-smooth
[bogachev07-vol2]. Also, for every $\tau$-smooth 
measure $\mu$ there exists a smallest closed set $A$ of full measure 
$\mu(A)=\mu(X)$, called the \emph{support} of $\mu$.}

if $\mu(\bigcup_{i\in I}A_i)=\sup_{i\in I}\mu(A_i)$ for every increasing net 
$(A_i)_{i\in I}$ of open sets [topsoe70,winkler85]. Let $R$ and $P\!_\tau$
be the sets of Radon and $\tau$-smooth probability measures on $X$, respectively.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.730399+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_df2b04e33422791d2fd96b7c46d4a7e1ba7fac4d615eea472d163ae6e32d4572","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Poisson of arXiv:2606.08325, 'Digit Mixing under Polynomial Maps'] For every fixed \(K\ge1\), the following convergence in distribution holds:
