{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:23:25.633520+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0cd8b48b5f9fc0465700bfcd437702b5848159c9fd0b7b9fa1cda063f474251","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2609.01350, 'New classes of trace-form permutation polynomials and their compositional inverses'] {(Kloosterman Sum)}
Let $\chi$ be a non-trivial additive character of $\mathbb F_q$ and let $a,b \in \mathbb F_q$. Then the sum 
$$
K(\chi;a,b)=\sum_{x \in \mathbb F_q^*}\chi (ax+bx^{-1})
$$
is called a Kloosterman sum.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:07:48.566939+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0ce9688069fd5b0d466d8d445205dd3a749d6bc8b92a5527a88cda4ce25a4df","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 32 of arXiv:2609.01460, 'On cost-induced Santal\'o-type inequalities \\ in Polish measure spaces'] Fix $m\in \N$ and a measurable, non-increasing function $\Omega:\R_+\to\R_+$. Let $f_1,\dots,f_{m+1}:\R^n\to\R_+$ be measurable, even functions that satisfy
    $$\left(\prod_{i=1}^{m}f_i(a_i)\right)\cdot f_{m+1}(v) \leq \Omega^{m+1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^m\langle a_i,v\rangle_+\right) \quad \text{for all } a_1,\dots,a_m,v\in \R^n.$$ 
    Then, we have
    \begin{equation}
  \begin{split}
    &\prod_{i=1}^m\left(\int_{\R^n}f_i(x)dx\right)\Bigg(\int_{\R^n} f_{m+1}(v)^\frac{1}{m}dv\Bigg)^m
    \\
    &\leq \left(\int_{\R^n}\cdots\int_{\R^n}\Omega\left(\left(\max_{|\xi| \leq 1} \sum_{i=1}^m \langle a_i, \xi \rangle_+\right)^{m+1}\right)da_1\cdots da_m\right)\left(\int_{\R^n}\Omega\left(|v|^\frac{m+1}{m}\right)dv\right)^{m}.
\end{split}
    \end{equation}
    When $\Omega$ is log-concave and integrable over $\R_+$, there is equality when there exists $C>0$ such that $$\prod_{i=1}^mf_i(a_i) = C\cdot \Omega\left(\left(\max_{|\xi| \leq 1} \sum_{i=1}^m \langle a_i, \xi \rangle_+\right)^{m+1}\right) \text{ and }f_{m+1} = \frac{1}{C} \cdot \Omega\left( |\cdot|^{\frac{m+1}{m}}\right)^m.$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.058483+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0cf8993ff759e5721dd7ac0d397c90271a602b02914d6cce90a9a9bbbb827cf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:adaptive-proper-upper of arXiv:2608.20480, 'When Clean Data Hurts: Learning with Monotone \\ Corruptions Beyond Binary Classification'] For every increasing sequence $(L_k)$, the class
$\mathcal{H}(L_1,L_2,\ldots)$ is properly learnable under arbitrary adaptive
additions. More precisely, for every $\varepsilon,\delta\in(0,1)$, let
$K=\lceil C/(\varepsilon\delta)\rceil$ for a universal constant $C$. Then
\[
    \mathsf{N}_{\mathrm{ad}}^{\mathrm{prop}}
    (\varepsilon,\delta;\mathcal{H}(L_1,L_2,\ldots))
    \leq \frac{C'}{\varepsilon}
    \left(L_K+K+\log\frac1\delta\right).
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:03:59.236724+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0d6e743350ac228844f8e2ff30f261501b10b769cc6aad3ef50068a4db5e7f7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop_key of arXiv:2507.06522, 'Balanced multi-species spin glasses'] Assume that both $(\vc \Delta,\vc\lambda_N)$ and $(\widetilde{\vc\Delta},\vc\lambda_N)$ satisfy (equation delta_decay_finite of the paper).
For either ${\scaleobj{1.1}{\diamond}}\in\{\mathsf{Ising},\mathsf{spherical}\}$, we have
\begin{subequations} 
\begin{align}

F_{N,{\scaleobj{1.1}{\diamond}}}(\widetilde{\vc\Delta},\vc\lambda_N)- F_{N,{\scaleobj{1.1}{\diamond}}}(\vc\Delta,\vc\lambda_N) 
&\leqslant \frac{1}{2}\Big[\xi_{N,\widetilde{\vc\Delta}}({\boldsymbol 1})-\xi_{N,\vc\Delta}({\boldsymbol 1}) \nonumber \\
&\phantom{\leqslant\frac12\Big[}+ \sup_{{\boldsymbol x}\in[-1,1]^\mathscr{S}}\big(\xi_{N,\vc\Delta}({\boldsymbol x})-\xi_{N,\widetilde{\vc\Delta}}({\boldsymbol x})\big)\Big], \\

\text{and}\quad |F_{N,{\scaleobj{1.1}{\diamond}}}(\vc\Delta,\vc\lambda_N) - F_{N,{\scaleobj{1.1}{\diamond}}}(\widetilde{\vc\Delta},\vc\lambda_N)| &\leqslant \sup_{{\boldsymbol x}\in[-1,1]^\mathscr{S}} |\xi_{N,\vc\Delta}({\boldsymbol x})-\xi_{N,\widetilde{\vc\Delta}}({\boldsymbol x})|.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
In the large-$N$ limit: if (equation converging_ratios of the paper)  holds, and both $(\vc\Delta,\vc\lambda)$ and $(\widetilde{\vc\Delta},\vc\lambda)$ satisfy (equation delta_decay of the paper), then
\begin{subequations} 
\begin{align}

\limsup_{N\to\infty}\big(F_{N,{\scaleobj{1.1}{\diamond}}}(\widetilde{\vc\Delta},\vc\lambda_N)-F_{N,{\scaleobj{1.1}{\diamond}}}(\vc\Delta,\vc\lambda_N)\big)
&\leqslant\frac{1}{2}\Big[\xi_{\infty,\widetilde{\vc\Delta}}({\boldsymbol 1})-\xi_{\infty,\vc\Delta}({\boldsymbol 1}) \nonumber \\
&\phantom{\leqslant\frac12\Big[}+\sup_{{\boldsymbol x}\in[0,1]^\mathscr{S}}\big(\xi_{\infty,\vc\Delta}({\boldsymbol x})-\xi_{\infty,\widetilde{\vc\Delta}}({\boldsymbol x})\big)\Big], \\

\text{and}\quad\limsup_{N\to\infty}|F_{N,{\scaleobj{1.1}{\diamond}}}(\widetilde{\vc\Delta},\vc\lambda_N)-F_{N,{\scaleobj{1.1}{\diamond}}}(\vc\Delta,\vc\lambda_N)|
&\leqslant\sup_{{\boldsymbol x}\in[0,1]^\mathscr{S}}|\xi_{\infty,\vc\Delta}({\boldsymbol x})-\xi_{\infty,\widetilde{\vc\Delta}}({\boldsymbol x})|.
\end{align}
\end{subequations} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:17.375729+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0da4e6a9d7f9d97ab006ff3358f997f4005f557dbd451edf1015812bed779b7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 2 of arXiv:2606.04950, 'The Tricomi equation in the hyperbolic half plane under additive space-time Gaussian White'] For any $t>0$ the stochastic process $\{U(t,x)\}_{x\in\mathbb{R}}$ is second order stationary with expectation
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[U(t,x)]=0\quad\mbox{ for all }x\in\mathbb{R}
\end{align*}
and covariance
\begin{align}
\mathtt{cov}(U(t,x),U(t,y))=\frac{1}{2\pi^2}\int_0^{+\infty}\cos(\rho |x-y|)\int_0^t|C(t,s,\rho)|^2dsd\rho\quad\mbox{ for all }x,y\in\mathbb{R}.
\end{align}
Moreover, the correlation function
\begin{align*}
r_t(h):=\frac{\mathtt{cov}(U(t,x+h),U(t,x))}{\mathbb{V}[U(t,x)]},\quad t>0,x,h\in\mathbb{R},
\end{align*}
verifies
\begin{align*}
r_t(h)=1-\alpha_t|h|+o(|h|)\mbox{ as $|h|$ tends to zero},
\end{align*}
with
\begin{align*}
\alpha_t:=\frac{\pi}{2\int_0^{+\infty}\int_0^{t}\left|C(t,s,\rho)\right|^2ds d\rho}.
\end{align*}
In particular, the stochastic process $\{U(t,x)\}_{x\in\mathbb{R}}$ admits a continuous modification. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.793387+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e0dbae2bd866cb8d06062c6f6f9dc604d5756f4c57957927bddaec8b9ea2b260","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemme 25 of arXiv:2604.00635, 'Large deviations of the periodic Toda chain'] Let $\kappa, \delta  >0$ and let $\mu\in \mathcal{G}_{\kappa}$, which in particular means $\mu$ has bounded density and is compactly supported. Assume also that $\int x \, \dd\mu(x)=0$. Then,
