as $\N \rightarrow \infty$. Here $\mu$ is the equilibrium measure, $-\I$ is the energy functional that it minimizes, $\mathbbm{Rest}_0^{\textnormal{ref}}$ depends on $\N$ and are twice-continuously differentiable with respect to $(\hat{n},\hat{A}_1,\hat{A}_2,\hat{B}_1,\hat{B}_2)$ for $(\theta, \hat{n},\hat{A}_1,\hat{A}_2,\hat{B}_1,\hat{B}_2)\in\mathcal D$ with derivatives of orders $0$, $1$, $2$, uniformly bounded in $\N$. Finally, $o(\frac{1}{\N^{2}})$ is a term which after multiplication by $\N^{2}$ tends to $0$ as $\N\rightarrow\infty$ uniformly over the parameters varying in compact subsets of $\mathcal D$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:15.349593+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e274507f6be58c5ee28aabb8f68c3d03c13ca5412dc9efc48c8d12da1f3ce9b1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 2 of arXiv:2506.15341, '\bf Superpositions for General Conditional Mckean-Vlasov Stochastic Differential Equations'] An eight-tuple $(\O, \cF, \hP,\hF,  B^1, B^2, X, Y)$ is called a weak solution to the CMVSDE (\ref{SDE1}) if 

{\rm (i)}  $(\O,\cF,\hP;\hF)$ is a filtered probability space satisfying the usual hypothese;

\ss
{\rm (ii)} $(B^1,B^2)$ is an $(\hP, \hF)$-Brownian motion;

\ss
{\rm (iii)} SDE (\ref{SDE1}) holds with $\m_t=\hP^{X|Y}_t$, for all  $t\in[0,T]$, $\hP$-a.s.
\qed","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.667730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e275576fc121d2b7453d03e1da17d3002f2eb9b8d3dc3db5c4a3a61966d0bbf8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 8 of arXiv:2604.24189, 'Malliavin calculus and densities for chaos-driven stochastic differential equations'] Let $q \in \N$ and $f \in \mathfrak{H}^{\odot  q}$ be fixed, and set $F=I_q(f)$. There exist two events $\Omega_F^\prime \subseteq \Omega_F$ with $\mathbb{P}(\Omega_F^\prime)=\mathbb{P}(\Omega_F)=1$, together with a random variable $\widetilde{F} \in \widetilde{\mathbb{D}}^{\infty}$, such that
\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
\item  $\Omega_F + j(\mathfrak{H}) \subseteq \Omega_F$;
\item for all $\ell \in \N$, $\widetilde{D}^\ell \widetilde{F}=D^\ell F$ on $\Omega_F^\prime$;
\item for all $\omega \in \Omega_F$, $h \in \mathfrak{H}$ and $0 \leq \ell \leq q$,
\begin{align}
\widetilde{D}^\ell \widetilde{F}(\omega + j(h)) = \sum_{k=0}^{q-\ell} \frac{1}{k!} \langle \widetilde{D}^{k+\ell} \widetilde{F}(\omega),h^{\otimes k} \rangle_{\mathfrak{H}^{\otimes k}}.
\end{align}
\item for all $\ell >q$, $\widetilde{D}^\ell \widetilde{F}=0$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.596909+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e276de8141efff49d93ac4cec2bfff8f81e08d91b77d49561904530e038a0628","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 of arXiv:2607.07283, 'Multi-time Markov renewal chains and stratified renewal theorems'] The Markov renewal potential associated with $q$ is the kernel sequence $\psi$ defined by

\begin{equation*}
\psi_k(x,A)=\sum_{n\geq0}q^{(n)}_k(x,A).
\end{equation*}

When $q$ is strict, this sum is finite for each fixed $k$. In general it is interpreted as a non-negative kernel, possibly sigma-finite.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.448719+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e27849b90a64ca6bf04159f65660cee1ec78b8b15e0f13fbf66a1c1a2b660ce6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 (Covering-to-measure principle, \cite{dadush2022matrix} Lemma 3.3) of arXiv:2606.16005, '\bfseries An Algebraic Matrix Spencer Theorem'] Let $K\subset\R^s$ be symmetric and convex.  The following are equivalent,
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[\textup{(1)}] $N\!\left(K^\circ,\frac1sB_\infty^s\right)\le 2^{O(s)}$,
    \item[\textup{(2)}] $N\left(K^\circ,\frac{1}{\sqrt s}B_2^s\right)\le 2^{O(s)}$,
    \item[\textup{(3)}] $\gamma_s(K)\ge 2^{-O(s)}$.
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, we will use the following parameterized form of the implication \(\textup{(2)}\Rightarrow\textup{(3)}\). If \(K\subseteq\mathbb R^s\) is a symmetric convex body, and for some $r\ge s$, $N\left(K^\circ,\frac{1}{\sqrt s}B_2^s\right)\le 2^{Cr}$, then $\gamma_s(cK)\ge 2^{-C'r}$, where $c,C'>0$ depend only on $C$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.912823+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e279d9aeb2cfa1741c7cd608ac09b641552db9b1e10f04db92a3e7b57882820e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop propbasicproperty of arXiv:2608.29914, 'Fixed points in abstract provability structures'] Let $S = \langle L, \leq, \top, \bot, \Box, \boxtimes\rangle$ be an APS.
Then, the following statements hold: 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\top = \boxtimes \bot = \Box \top$.

        \item For every $x \in L$, we have $\boxtimes \top \leq \boxtimes x \leq \Box^k \boxtimes x \leq \Box^{k+1} \boxtimes x$ for all $k \geq 0$. 
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:17:17.095982+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2801c11085a0d8a7f0b92a285ff7c6d4e0a32c87266898c6dfbd1b01b1479e4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 27 of arXiv:2608.19976, 'Random valuations'] A monotone $\sigma$-continuous ID valuation $\Phi$ has
  symmetric-independent increments if and only if the outer measure of
  its L\'evy measure $\Lambda$ is supported by valuations $\psi$ each
  of which is of the form
  \begin{displaymath}
    \psi(K)=g^\vee(K)
  \end{displaymath}
  for an upper semicontinuous quasi-concave function $g:\Rd\to\R_+$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.971220+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2830b68ebe566d6948af61841ddc6bc210eb97cccdcc1c0d7b6ceea4ec1b138","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2607.22438, 'A deterministic scalar reduction for the phase transition in two interacting urns'] For \(u_{\alpha,p}\) defined by \eqref{eq:u-alpha-p}, one has
\begin{equation*}
 \Phi_p'\!\left(\frac12\right)
 =2\lambda_+(u_{\alpha,p},1-u_{\alpha,p}).
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.760652+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e283772e4d67d38e1b4b16e5ad395136cff36a21f22bdae40a49642d06d9f06b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop propotts of arXiv:2609.02682, 'LDP for tensor forms'] For any $W_\infty\in \mathcal{W}_v$,  $\boldsymbol{\alpha}=(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_c)\in \mathbb{R}^c$, and $\boldsymbol{h}=(h_1,\ldots,h_c)\in \mathbb{R}^c$, consider the optimization problem in the RHS of (equation eq:potts_unconstrained_opt of the paper). Then the following conclusions hold:
\begin{itemize}
    \item [(i)](Characterization of optimizers)
If ${\boldsymbol f}=(f_1,\ldots,f_c)$ is a maximizer, it satisfies
\begin{equation} 
f_r(x)\stackrel{\lambda-a.e.}{=}\frac{\exp(\alpha_r v \mathcal{V}_{r}[W_\infty, \boldsymbol{f}](x)+h_r)}{\sum_{s=1}^c \exp(\alpha_s v \mathcal{V}_{s}[W_\infty, \boldsymbol{f}](x)+h_s)},\ r\in [c].
\end{equation}

\item [(ii)](Replica-symmetry breaking) Suppose there exist $r,s\in[c]$ such that $\alpha_r e^{(v-1)h_r} \neq \alpha_s e^{(v-1)h_s}$. Moreover, assume $\mathcal{V}[W_\infty](\cdot)$ (as in~(equation deft1 of the paper)) is not constant $\lambda$-a.e. Then none of the maximizers are constant $\lambda$-a.e.

\item [(iii)](Replica symmetry) If $\mathcal{V}[W_\infty](\cdot)=1$   $\lambda$-a.e., $W_\infty>0$ $\lambda$-a.e., and $\alpha_r \ge 0$ for $r\in[c]$, then all of the maximizers are constant $\lambda$-a.e.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.167939+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2849e1f18801c523003a25383c0e9322b9ec9fd27e25ea23766f20bf47f39d0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2604.17973, 'Schauder estimates and classical solutions of the Dirichlet problem for stochastic parabol'] There exist $r_0>0$ and $C>0$, depending only on
\[
n,\ \alpha,\ \gamma,\ \kappa,\ K,\ L,\ L_\alpha,\ T,
\ \text{and the }C^{2+\alpha}\text{-character of }G,
\]
such that for every $z\in\partial G$ and every quasi-classical solution $u$ of \eqref{eq:main}--\eqref{eq:boundary-initial},
\begin{align}
|u|_{(2+\alpha,\alpha/2);(B_{r_0}(z)\cap G)\times(0,T]}^{L_\omega^\gamma}
\le C\Bigl(
|u|_{0;(B_{2r_0}(z)\cap G)\times(0,T]}^{L_\omega^\gamma}
+|f|_{\alpha;(B_{2r_0}(z)\cap G)\times(0,T]}^{L_\omega^\gamma}
\notag\\
\qquad
+|f|_{(\alpha,\alpha/2);\Gamma_T\cap(B_{2r_0}(z)\times[0,T])}^{L_\omega^\gamma}
+|g|_{1+\alpha;(B_{2r_0}(z)\cap G)\times(0,T]}^{L_\omega^\gamma(\elltwo)}
\Bigr).

\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.769612+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2868175a18d3efa819bbe2b1d2e5fa9304be49ddcf78899b4dd4669cbced3c2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 11 (Positive exponential moments) of arXiv:2605.14511, 'Clumsy and Careless:\\ Stationary-Entry Flux in Non-monotone Coupon Collectors'] Assume the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:reset-rare-success}.  If, for a fixed $a\in(0,1)$,
\begin{equation}
        \frac{\phi_n(q_ne^{a\rho_ns_n})}{s_n}\longrightarrow1,
