{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.415447+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e28d5d9f2e847a3ca4ba3aee972226bbac62c4473dbb3e3f8248c45850fd4d42","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 94 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Take two variational data satisfying Assumptions~\ref{Assumption_A} and \ref{Assumption_B}, differing only by their segment filling fractions $\hat{n}_h^{\textnormal{I}}$ and $\hat{n}_{h}^{\textnormal{II}}$ for a single $h \in [H]$. Assume furthermore that $\boldsymbol{\mu}^{\textnormal{I}}$ satisfies Assumption~\ref{Assumption_C}. Then, there exists $M > 0$ depending only on the constants in the assumptions, such that for $|\hat{n}_{h}^{\textnormal{I}} - \hat{n}_{h}^{\textnormal{II}}| < \frac{1}{M}$ and for any measurable function $f$ with finite $|\!|f|\!|_{\frac{1}{2}}$ and $|\!|f|\!|_{\infty}$ norms we have
 \begin{equation}

 \bigg| \int_{\amsmathbb R} f(x)\dd(\mu^{\textnormal{I}}-\mu^{\textnormal{II}})(x)\bigg|\leq M \cdot \big(|\!|f|\!|_{\frac{1}{2}} + |\!|f|\!|_{\infty}\big)\cdot |\hat{n}_{h}^{\textnormal{I}} - \hat{n}_{h}^{\textnormal{II}}|. \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.076569+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e29229f97d91bc056535c24e759f00dc43601740ff75e65634c3d130cd9f1148","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 (Letwin's quadratic and moment-map estimates) of arXiv:2608.27657, '\bfseries Stein Kernels and Normal Approximation\\ for Log-Concave Bilinear Forms'] The cited \cite[Theorems~1.2 and~2.5]{Letwin} state the following estimates.
For
every isotropic log-concave \(Z\in\R^n\) and every deterministic symmetric
matrix \(A\),
\begin{equation}
  \Var(Z^{\mathsf T}AZ)\leq8\Tr(A^2).
  \tag{2.7}
\end{equation}
In the regular moment-map model above, for every deterministic symmetric
matrix \(A\),
\begin{equation}
  \E\Tr(AHAH)\leq2\Tr(A^2).
  \tag{2.8}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:20.884944+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e29e41bd42737a8316ee84c8651e84a99ac80bfe1d9e4dd21c86826acb5791cf","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2506.11918, 'Central limit theorems for the Euler characteristic in the Random Connection Model for hig'] Let $(W_n)_{n\in\N}$ be a sequence in $\K^d$ with $r(W_n)\rightarrow\infty$ for $n\rightarrow\infty$ and $f:\left(\R^d\right)^m\rightarrow [0,\infty)$, $m\in\N$, be a measurable and translation-invariant function such that
\begin{align*}
\nu_f \,:=\, \int_{\left(\R^d\right)^{m-1}} f(\mathbf{0},x_2,\dots,x_{m}) \; \d {(x_2,\dots,x_m)} \,<\, \infty.
\end{align*}
For $m=1$, we read this term as $f(\0)$.
Then it is true that
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{|W_n|} \int_{W_n^m} f(x_1,\dots,x_{m}) \; \d {(x_1,\dots,x_m)} \;\xrightarrow{\;\;}\; \nu_f \qquad \text{for } n\rightarrow\infty.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:28.951986+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e29f04077f3d9403c5a672a1acd5fa0ee5d571998ede5cdea359f7cbc83445a5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Qeq of arXiv:2608.16915, 'Exact mean--covariance dynamics of the Weber field in the stochastic Lagrangian representa'] The tensor $Q$ solves
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal D_uQ+G^\top Q+QG=2\nu\sum_{k=1}^3(\partial_kM)^\top(\partial_kM),\qquad Q(0)=0,
\end{equation}
and, with $S:=\tfrac12(G+G^\top)$ the symmetric strain,
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal D_u\tr Q+2\tr(SQ)=2\nu\left\lvert \nabla M\right\rvert_{\mathrm F}^2.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.136858+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e29fa5fa8389c0d03ee3dbfa61c24829039946c671f68b39ca50e6cef58e6375","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:introexamples of arXiv:2507.01236, 'Disintegration theorem for multifunctions, \\ with applications to empirical Wasserstein d'] For any $\alpha\in(0,\infty)$:
    \begin{enumerate}    
        \item[{\rm (i)}] The interval $[0,1]$ with its Euclidean metric and absolutely continuous measure with density bounded away from both~$0$ and~$\infty$ is asymptotically disintegrable with rate 
        \begin{equation} 
        \mbox{$\left(O\left(n^{-\alpha} \right),O\!\left(\sqrt{\frac{\ln n}{n}}\right)\right)$}. 
        \end{equation}
        \item[{\rm (ii)}] The circle in $\mathbb{R}^2$ with normalised geodesic metric and absolutely continuous measure with density bounded away from both~$0$ and~$\infty$ is asymptotically disintegrable with rate~(equation big-oh of the paper).
        \item[{\rm (iii)}] Any finite connected simple graph $(V,E)$ embedded in $\mathbb{R}^2$ with appropriate graph metric and absolutely continuous measure with with density bounded away from both~$0$ and~$\infty$ is asymptotically disintegrable with rate~(equation big-oh of the paper).
        \item[{\rm (iv)}] For any natural number $D\geq 2$, the unit cube $[0,1]^D$ with its $\ell^{\infty}$ metric and any absolutely continuous measure with with density bounded away from both~$0$ and~$\infty$ is absolutely disintegrable with rate $\left(O(n^{-\alpha}),O\!\left((\frac{\ln n}{n})^{1 / (2D)}\right)\right)$.
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:06.324496+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2a15f926cf1eb3b9e629ad211c03fc39e666d2fbb35a93f959808fbc0ec1d2d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 13 (Local contraction) of arXiv:2607.05219, 'Random free semigroups of affine groups'] Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on $\ASim(V)$ satisfying:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\rm(i)] \textit{(Moment condition)}
$\displaystyle\int\!\bigl(\log^+c(g)+\log^+\|b(g)\|\bigr)\,d\mu(g)<\infty$.
\item[\rm(ii)] \textit{(Affine non-degeneracy)}
There is no $v\in V$   fixed by all $g\in\supp\mu$.
\item[\rm(iii)] \textit{(Centered ratio)}
$\displaystyle\int\!\log c(g)\,d\mu(g)=0$.
\item[\rm(iv)] \textit{(Non-trivial ratio)}
$\mu\{g; c(g)=1\}<1$. 
\end{enumerate}
Let $L_n:=X_n\cdots X_1$ be the associated left random walk. Then for
every compact $U\subset V$ and every $x,y\in V$,
\[
\|L_nx-L_ny\|\,\mathbf{1}_{\{L_nx\in U\}}\longrightarrow 0
\qquad\text{almost surely}.
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.776133+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2a9f190e6b0077e2681c5181694daf0854a532cfc69a7166c81bbfe9f09435e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:APZ of arXiv:2608.05089, 'Correlation decay in area-tilted line ensembles'] A line ensemble $\mathbf{X}$ is asymptotically pinned to zero if for any $ \varepsilon, T > 0$ there is some
$k \in \mathbb{N}$ such that
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{P} \mleft[ \sup_{t \in [-T,T]} X^k(t) \leqslant \varepsilon \mright] \geqslant 1 - \varepsilon.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:46:25.364735+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2b0b183e95b0e812140e0d4e4fbe69bfd9cd0c44d5b423d2d57f5c4f182c1ca","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor no_individual_bubble_long of arXiv:2604.14122, 'The scaling limit of random walk and\\ the intrinsic metric on planar critical percolation'] There exist $\beta>0$, $\varepsilon>0$ such that the following holds for all $R$ sufficiently large and $p$ sufficiently small depending on $R$. For all $\alpha>\frac{3}{4}-\varepsilon$ for which~(equation eq:assumptionsubpoly of the paper) holds, we also have that for all $k$ and $n>R^k$,

\begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
        \mathbf{P}\!\left(\exists i\colon X_{x_{i},x_{i+1}}\geq \mathbf{q}_n\right)\leq C(R,\alpha)\cdot R^{-(\alpha+\beta)k},
    \end{split}
\end{equation} for some constant $C(R,\alpha)>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.433438+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2b5d02c4b93c61fdc48a86d2046ee812f4a2afe3884a7f8595c79f2452ae585","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Define of arXiv:2601.09990, 'Remarks on the convex integration technique applied to singular stochastic partial differe'] \rm{(\hspace{1sp}[H14])} Let $\alpha < 0$ and $r = - \lceil \alpha \rceil > 0$. We say $\xi \in \mathcal{S}'$ belongs to $\mathscr{C}_{\mathfrak{s}}^{\alpha}$ if it belongs to the dual of $C_{c}^{r}(\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{T}^{d})$ and for every compact set $K \subset \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{T}^{d}$ there exists a constant $C$ such that 
\begin{align*}
\langle \xi, \mathscr{S}_{\mathfrak{s},\bar{z}}^{\delta} \eta \rangle  \leq C \delta^{\alpha} \hspace{3mm} \forall \, \eta \in \{ \psi \in C^{r}: \lVert \psi \rVert_{C^{r}} \leq 1, \supp \psi \subset B_{\mathfrak{s}}(0,1)\},  
\end{align*}
for all $\delta \in (0, 1]$ and all $\bar{z} \in K$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.749557+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2bcdc6a65df74918dddc58950a71b473d820554f3bb275348ef6c4348ba75c9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 (\cite{VanDenBergPerimeter,VanDenBergDavies}) of arXiv:2608.18834, 'The Parabolic Anderson Model's Total Mass at Small Times: Geometry, Fluctuations, and Reno'] If $\partial D$ is sufficiently regular
(such as the $R$-smooth condition in \cite[Definition 6.1]{VanDenBergDavies}), then
the lower bound \eqref{Equation: Heat Content Lower Limit} is optimal; in fact,
\begin{align}

|D|-\int_D u_0(t,x)\d x\sim\frac{\sqrt{2}|\partial D|}{\sqrt{\pi}}t^{1/2},
\end{align}
where $|\partial D|$ is the Hausdorff measure of $D$'s boundary. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.663437+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2bddb412368aca1570dda5bc54cf1c3d81dde6d12c1e265173272bc64e2442b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:two-slab of arXiv:2607.06249, 'Extreme least singular values of Gaussian row submatrices and a phase retrieval stability '] For each $\mathbb{F}\in\{\mathbb{R},\mathbb{C}\}$ there is a constant $C_\mathbb{F}$ such that, for $0<t\le1$,
