In particular,
$
\#\{u\in\mathcal T^n: G_{k_n}(\mathcal T^n_u)=1\} \rightarrow \infty$ in
probability.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.606866+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2f01a73c10a5c1f9e7e69878d3382e8ed65296a8edf86c245b046bfd7cad038","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 20 of arXiv:2607.08129, '2607.08129'] Let $X_1,\ldots,X_N$ be independent centered random variables satisfying $|X_i|\le b$ almost surely, and put $V:=\sum_{i=1}^N\Var(X_i)$.  Then, for every $x>0$,
\begin{equation}
