since $H(t) > n-1 \geq 1 > 1/\alpha$). Then, almost surely,
for every $t \in \mathbb{R}$:
\begin{equation}

\alpha_{X^{(n)}_H}(t) = H(t) - \frac{1}{\alpha}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:04:07.032740+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2ffd3be05076284d6bb40884f3f88efa6848cc711859d0352b1ee654b996671","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:ZIMsurvival of arXiv:2607.29409, 'The Zombie Infection Model'] The following statements are equivalent:
		\begin{enumerate}
