{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.773517+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e3142bff079ca86602c5f2c35e4030544a58bf7bfae21be6abed0495f4feee0f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop propThreeAnnulus of arXiv:2609.01464, 'Removability of non-isolated singularities for Einstein metrics and RCD spaces'] For every $\beta>0$, every integer $n\geqslant 2$, and $\mathcal{K}>0$, $\rho_0>0$, $0\leqslant c<n-2$ there are $0<r_0<1$, $0<\delta<1$, and $C>0$ such that the following holds. 

    Let $(X,\mathrm{d},\mathcal{H}^n)$ be an $\mathrm{RCD}(-\eta,n)$ space, with $\eta\geqslant 0$ such that:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\Sigma \subset X$ satisfies (def:Codimension of the paper) with $k=2$ and the choices of $\mathcal{K},\rho_0,c$ above: in particular, $\Sigma$ has codimension greater than $2$. Moreover, $X\setminus\Sigma$ is (locally isometric to) a smooth Riemannian manifold;
        \item $|\mathrm{Ric}| \leqslant \eta$ on $X\setminus \Sigma$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Let $x\in X$, and assume $\{f_i\}_{i=1}^n$ are harmonic functions on $B_4(x)$. Denote
    \[ 
    D:=\sum_{i,j} \fint_{B_4(x)} |\nabla f_i \cdot \nabla f_j - \delta_{ij}|\mathrm{d}\mathcal{H}^n.
    \]
    Assume $D+\eta\leqslant \delta$. Then there is a symmetric matrix $A_{ij}$ with $\|A_{ij}\| < C(D+\eta^{1/2})$ such that $\widetilde{f}_i := f_i + \sum A_{ij}f_j$ satisfy
    \[ \sum_{i,j} \fint_{B_{r_0}(x)} |\nabla \widetilde{f}_i\cdot \nabla \widetilde{f}_j - \delta_{ij}|\ensuremath{\mathrm d}\mathcal{H}^n \leqslant r_0^{1-\beta} (D+\eta^{1/2}). \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.661174+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e314ac94ae08b06defee692c2d593d356c1daf36f344047d47126b71dced5016","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2605.09372, 'Sharp weighted norm estimates for martingale square functions'] Let $1\leq p<\infty$ and let $W$ be a matrix weight. There exists a sequence of matrix-valued functions $(\widetilde W_n)_{n\geq 1}$ such that for every $n$,  the entries of $\widetilde W_n$ are adapted to $ \mathcal F_n$ and
