{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.098490+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e351e239875e16aa4f3a3ea04dd5309c855dd04192e6244f3cfd51683fdd0ca3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 (Upper Bound on Cumulative Expected Error over a Finite Horizon) of arXiv:2608.28198, 'Performative Privacy: When Differential Privacy Maximizes Utility'] For a finite operational timeline $T$ and an initial population $N_0$, let $C_t(\gamma) > 1$ denote the strictly positive expected per-round demographic growth factor for all $t$. Under mean field-approximation of $N_t$, the cumulative expected Mahalanobis error aggregated across all $T$ rounds, defined as $\mathcal{E}_{\text{total}}(\gamma) \triangleq \sum_{t=1}^T \mathcal{E}_t(\gamma)$ is upper bounded by $\mathcal{E}_{\text{total}}(\gamma) \leq \hat{\mathcal{E}}_{\text{total}}(\gamma)$, where:
