{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:35:04.212986+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e38c79dee049eafad806e9b8220723734cd704699ee6a5389de1b55cf6b60e7f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 4 of arXiv:2608.03956, 'From Triangular Array Progression\\ to Near-Log-Concave State Transitions'] A sequence $[\eta_1,\ldots,\eta_{n+1}]$ for $n\geq 2$ is a \emph{near}-log-concave sequence of degree $r\in \Z_+$ for finite $0 \leq r \leq n-2$, if the following holds:
\begin{align}
\eta_{i-1}\eta_{i+1} \leq \eta^2_{i} \hspace{0.15in} \text{$\forall i\in\mathcal{I}_{n}^{(r)}$}, \notag
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{I}_{n}^{(r)} \subseteq \mathcal{I}_{n} = \{2,\ldots,n\}$ such that 
\begin{align}
|\mathcal{I}_{n}^{(r)}| = n - r - 1.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:25:06.201045+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e38eca02684bc8691906bfc53b0d05f5a2e89c5f883fd26578cc5e53394a89f4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 22 (Error recursion bounds) of arXiv:2608.22636, 'Q-Learning with Stable Infinite-Dimensional \\Linear Function Approximation'] For $\alpha>1$ and integers $n_0,m_0>0$ such that
$m_0+n_0\ge16\alpha$, define
$$\alpha_k=\frac{\alpha}{k+n_0},\quad     \tau(j,m)=\sum_{k=j}^{m-1}\alpha_{k+1},\quad\text{and}\quad m_{r+1}:=\max\cW(m_r,T),\; r\ge 0$$ 
where $\cW(\cdot,T)$ is the window defined in
Lemma~\ref{lemma:step size_window}. We will consider $T=1/4$ or $T = 1/5$. 

Let $g > 1/\alpha$ be a fixed constant and set $\lambda:=\alpha g$. In addition, when $T=1/4$, assume $g\le1$; when $T=1/5$, assume $g\le4/5$. 

Suppose that for every $r\ge0$ and every $m\in\cW(m_r,T)$ the nonnegative sequence
$\set{\Delta_n:n\ge m_0}$ satisfies, 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
        \Delta_m
        &\le
        e^{-g\tau(m_r,m)}\Delta_{m_r}
        +
        K_1\sqrt{\frac{H_{m_r}}{m_r+n_0}}
        +
        \frac{K_2}{m_r+n_0}
\end{aligned}

\end{equation}
for some non-negative and non-decreasing sequence $\set{H_n:n\ge m_0}$ and $K_1,K_2\ge0$. 
Then, for every $n\ge m_0$,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
        \Delta_n
        \le e^{1/16}\Delta_{m_0}
        \crbk{\frac{m_0+n_0}{n+n_0}}^\lambda +
        \frac{14\alpha  K_1}{\lambda-1/2}
        \sqrt{\frac{H_n}{n+n_0}}+
       \frac{17\alpha  K_2}{\lambda-1}
        \frac{1}{n+n_0}.
\end{aligned}

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.922119+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e38f5a883986688cd8420dfef13cb332affa85e0046718e706ce593bdecee0bf","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2608.02927, 'Thinning Operation via the Poisson-F\"ollmer Process'] Let $X$ be a random variable with density $f$ with respect to $\pi_{T},$ and let $a \in [0,1].$ Assume that $f$ is bounded away from zero and infinity. Then $(\lambda_{t})_{0 \le t \le aT}$ defined in \eqref{eq:minimizer}, is the minimizing process for $T_{a}(X)$ with respect to the Poisson measure $\pi_{aT}.$ Therefore,
