{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:48:17.116101+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e697e6349af0e5dd913cd7da1a06be9d0ac3f34d02adc8b515a5feb84d322170","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 135 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Let $\md,\me\lesssim1$ be positive numbers. There exists a constant
    $C>1$ such that the following holds. 
	Fix $(x', y') = (x', \gamma_{x'})$ on the arctic boundary $\mathfrak A_{q;a,b,c}$ with $x' \in [1/4, 5/4]$, and denote $f = f_{x'} (\gamma_{x'})$; $\gamma = \gamma_{x'}$; $\gamma' = \gamma_{x'}'$; and $\gamma'' = \gamma_{x'}''$. 
