{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.066656+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6a73f27894a75ca067fc9db484387ee0a5966d84b17ba4506a62a039af83948","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2606.27520, 'Critical curve of loop percolation on the $d$-regular tree'] Let $\alpha>0$ and $\kappa\geq\kappa_c$. Let $u,v,w$ be vertices such that $v$ lies on the geodesic between $u$ and $w$. Then 
\begin{equation}
\P(\exists \ell\in\mathcal L_{\alpha,\kappa}\,:\, u,v \in \ell\text{ and }w\notin\ell) = 1 - \big(1 - \tau_\kappa^{2d(u,v)}\big)^\alpha\big(1 - \tau_\kappa^{2d(u,w)}\big)^{-\alpha}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.487428+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6a7ad4287a6332ef11a24a69f779257606ea907d2bbf97320ec0258ab09328e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Conj 92 of arXiv:2509.01797, 'Relation between Wick powers and excursion clusters of the 2D GFF'] Fix $M>0$ and $\eta>0$.
The following asymptotic expansion holds in
$L^{2}(d\PP,\sigma((B_{t})_{0\leq t< \zeta_M}),H^{-\eta}(\C))$.
For every $N\geq 1$,
\begin{align*}
&\ind_{S_{\varepsilon}^{\rm CR}(\zeta_M)}
\\&
=
\sum_{n=1}^{N} \dfrac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n!}
\hat{\mu}^{\rm loop}_{\text{SLE}_{8/3}}
\Big(
\Big(\frac{1}{\pi}\vert \log (\varepsilon d(0,\Gamma)/\CR(0,\inter{\Gamma}))\vert + \cst (M)\Big)^{-n}
\Big)
:\Theta_{\zeta_M}^{n}:
\, + o(\vert \log\varepsilon\vert^{-N})
\\&
=
\sum_{n=1}^{N}  \dfrac{(-1)^{n-1}}{n!}
\dfrac{1}{\big(\frac{1}{\pi} \vert \log \varepsilon\vert\big)^{n}}
\sum_{k=1}^{n} 
\dfrac{n!(n-1)!}{(n-k)! k! (k-1)!} C^{\text{SLE}_{8/3}}_{n-k,M}\,
:\Theta_{\zeta_M}^{k}:
\, + o(\vert \log\varepsilon\vert^{-N}).
\end{align*} (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:42:55.313946+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6abb5d80e693f72773193c2d4c335b7c2426df2f9fa2e96a91ae0a48374dc87","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 11 of arXiv:2609.02398, '\textbf{Convex Order Comparisons for Sub-Gamma Random Variables}'] For all $\sigma, \alpha > 0$,
\begin{align*}
    \sigma \le c^{\SE}(\sigma, \alpha) \le \sigma \vee 2\alpha,
\end{align*}
and the constant $\sigma \vee 2\alpha$ is itself admissible: every $X \in \SE(\sigma^2,\alpha)$ satisfies $X \lecx (\sigma \vee 2\alpha)\,\Lap$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.826240+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6aeeb1523d6fdfcb30cd2c14be50ccfc725adc6b2beb629b8dc49726050c49e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:popularity of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Fix $h\geq2$.  There are explicit positive rational constants
$\kappa_2,\ldots,\kappa_h$ such that, as $q\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}
 F_{h,M_h-M_{h-r}}(q)=\kappa_rq^3+O_h(q^2)
 \qquad(2\leq r\leq h),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 F_{h,M_h}(q)=\binom q4-
 \left(\sum_{r=2}^h\kappa_r\right)q^3+O_h(q^2).
\end{equation}
For every
\[
 n\in\mathcal R(h,4)\setminus
 \left(\{M_h\}\cup\{M_h-M_{h-r}:2\leq r\leq h\}\right)
\]
there is an explicit positive rational constant $\lambda_{h,n}$ such that
\begin{equation}
 F_{h,n}(q)=\lambda_{h,n}q^2+O_h(q).
\end{equation}
Thus exactly $h$ cardinalities are popular: the maximum has frequency of
order $q^4$, the other $h-1$ displayed values have frequency of order
$q^3$, and every remaining attainable value has frequency of order $q^2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.690276+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6b8436949c63964fdc3a8aef9327d6ccefa1268afd420882def7f8324053c39","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:firstboundongradsth2 of arXiv:2411.10235, '\textbf{Smooth transport map via diffusion process}'] There exists  $0<C_\star<C_2$ such that if $t\in [0,1)$  and $x\in \mathbb{R}^d$ satisfy \begin{equation}
1-t^2\leq C_\star^{-1}\log(\epsilon^{-1})^{-C_\star} \text{ and } \|tx\|^2\leq 1-C_2^{-1}\log(\epsilon^{-1}+1)^{-K},
\end{equation}
    then for all $f\in L^2(\mathbb{R}^d)$, $i\in \{1,2\}$ and $\xi\in \mathbb{R}^d$, we have
    \begin{align*}
    \|\int f(y) \nabla_x^i p^{t,x}(y)d\lambda^d(y)\| 
    &\leq \frac{Ct^i}{(1-t^2)^{i/2}}\left(\int \|f(y)-\xi\|^2dp^{t,x}(y)\right)^{1/2}.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.653034+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6b996a13cd9872a316d03ce8e449f23117bec9f023088cb04148a86778655b9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 (Hold-and-jump product-form representation) of arXiv:2607.24533, 'Colored Markov-Modulated Brownian Motion for Preemptive Workload Stacks'] {Suppose that Assumptions~\ref{assump:intensity}, \ref{assump:stability}, and~\ref{assump:first-moment} hold. Then the hold-and-jump model admits a stationary probability law.} On the face $\Theta_{c_1,\dots,c_n}$, the stationary density row vector
of the hold-and-jump model is
\begin{equation}

\vect{\pi}^{\mathrm H}(\bm{x})
=
\vect{p}^{\mathrm H}_0
\Lop{0}{c_1}(x_{c_1})
\prod_{r=1}^{n-1}
\Lop{c_r}{c_{r+1}}(x_{c_{r+1}}).
