{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:41:28.173419+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6bab3d680eefadd134cff3f80c6dd3c1cf7d640ce98c94f504cd17a5c4bfae3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D_Kr_tree of arXiv:2510.26724, 'Sharp Fuss-Catalan thresholds in graph bootstrap percolation'] We call a graph $T$ a {\it $K_r$-tree}
(or a {\it clique tree})
of {\it order} $\vartheta$ if it is 
the union of $\vartheta$
many copies $H_1,\ldots,H_\vartheta$ of $K_r$, 
and that, 
for every $1<i\leqslant \vartheta$, $H_i$ shares exactly 
one edge with $H_1 \cup\cdots \cup H_{i-1}$.
We call an edge $e\in E(G)$ an {\it internal edge} 
of $T$ if it is contained in at least two of the copies of $K_r$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.577764+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6c28f1ef61145861068066cd48f8d3e80ac31ec538534f9a936dd8886862515","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def 40 of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] A \textit{current} $\n$ on $G_\fg$ is a function defined on the edge set $E_\fg$ and taking its values in $\mathbb N=\lbrace 0,1,\ldots\rbrace$. We let $\Omega_{G_\fg}$ be the set of currents on $G_\fg$. The set of \emph{sources} of $\n$, denoted by $\sn$, is defined as
\begin{equation}
\sn:=\Big\{ x \in V_\fg \: : \: \sum_{y\in V_\fg:\: xy\in E_\fg}\n_{xy}\textup{ is odd}\Big\}.
\end{equation}
We also define, for every $\beta\geq 0$ and $\mathsf{h}\in (\mathbb R^+)^V$, 
\begin{equation}
w_{\beta,\mathsf{h}}^G(\n):=\prod_{\substack{xy\in E}}\dfrac{\beta^{\n_{xy}}}{\n_{xy}!}\prod_{x\in V}\frac{\mathsf{h}_x^{\n_{x\fg}}}{\n_{x\fg}!}.
\end{equation}
If $\mathsf{h}=0$, we simply write $w_{\beta}^G(\n)=w_{\beta,0}^G(\n)$.  Similarly, we define $\Omega_G$, the set of currents on $G$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.673000+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6c3e588a9cbff1f5706ba501791aa713ddf5d892fc745becfa78c645e45afe7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:gamma:summing of arXiv:2605.28106, 'Gaussian Processes with Sample Paths in Reproducing Kernel Banach Spaces'] The space \( \gamma_\infty(H,X) \) is a Banach space with respect to the norm
    \begin{equation} 
        \| A \|_{\gamma_\infty(H,X)} =  \sup_{(h_k)_{k=1}^n \in \mathcal{F}(H)} \left( \int_\Omega \left\| \sum_{k=1}^n \xi_k(\omega) A h_k \right\|^2 \mu(d\omega) \right)^\frac{1}{2}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:47:40.728180+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6c430ecc4a74ae64bb80ac1081b0b6a32b0fe50e0bcd2d64237e291ee27f7c8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:mixing-time-langevin of arXiv:2604.20008, 'Mixing times of Langevin dynamics for \\ spiked matrix models'] Fix $\alpha \in (0,\infty)$, $\beta = \frac{\alpha}{\theta}$,  and $\theta>\theta_0(\alpha)$ large. Then with $\mathbb{P}_M$-probability $1-o(1)$, for large $N$, the following hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item  If $\alpha<1$, then  $t_{\mathsf{mix}} = \Theta(\log N)$.
          \item If $\alpha>1$, then $t_{\mathsf{mix}}^{\mathsf{sym}} = \Theta(\log N)$ even though $t_{\mathsf{mix}} = \exp( \Theta(N))$. 
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.245684+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6c80137abc249b680d0242c63c3a6588bd26ad1a9a32accbae69a85da31f433","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 16 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Given $H_+,H_-:\mX_{a,b,c}\mapsto \mathbb R$, with ${H_-}\le {H_+}$ pointwise,
  let $\mathcal P^+[H_-,H_+]$ denote the collection of  probability distributions $\mu$ on $\Omega_{H_\pm}$ that are increasing with respect to  $\pi_{H_\pm}$: 
  \begin{eqnarray}

   \eta\ge \eta'\Rightarrow {\mu(\eta)}\ge \mu(\eta').
  \end{eqnarray}
  When $H_+$ and $H_-$ are the height functions of the maximal and minimal configurations in $\Omega^N_{a,b,c}$, respectively, we write $\mathcal P^+$ instead of $\mathcal P^+[H_-,H_+]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.313315+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6cacd5b53b2f418c651617470422421f3f42aaa056bb0341f37cf796972eab9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem WP_H^2 of arXiv:2606.00325, 'Ergodicity for a class of stochastic Hasegawa-Mima equations with dissipation'] Assume that $A$ satisfies (ass2 of the paper), $v\in C^\infty_c(\mathcal{D})$ and the noise coefficients $(f_k)_{k=1}^M \subset L^2(\mathcal{D})$. Then for every $\phi_0 \in H^2 \cap V$ let $\phi_t$ be the unique solution of (eq:main of the paper) in the sense of (def-sol-H1 of the paper). Then the process $\xi = \phi -\Delta \phi - v$ belongs to $C(0, T; L^2) \cap L^2(0, T; Y) $ almost surely and it verifies, for every $\psi \in Y$
    \begin{align}
        \left\langle\xi_t, \psi\right\rangle - \left\langle\xi_0, \psi\right\rangle = -\int_0^t \brak{B^{1/2 }\xi_s, B^{1/2} \psi}ds + \int_0^t\left\langle \xi_s, \nabla^\perp \phi \cdot \nabla \psi\right\rangleds + \int_0^t \left\langle\xi_s , dW_t\right\rangle.
    \end{align}
    Moreover it satisfies the following energy balance
    \begin{align}
    \mathbb{E}[|\xi(t)|^2] - \mathbb{E}[|\xi_0|^2]
     +2\int_0^t\mathbb{E}\left[\|\xi\|_{Y}^2\right]dt = 2 \int_0^t\mathbb{E}\left[\brak{B^{1/2} \xi, B^{1/2} v} \right]dt+ \sigma t,
\end{align}
    where $\sigma = \sum_{k=1}^M \sigma_k^2|f_j|^2$. Moreover, $\xi$ is a Markov process in $L^2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.414344+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6d73bb1ce7f15392fa7b9eb4412f5c31dff1545d43cd275a77d69d0a9503bde","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 79 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Suppose Assumptions~\ref{Assumptions_Theta}, \ref{Assumptions_basic} and \ref{Assumptions_offcrit} hold. For any $\zeta_2 \in \big(0,\frac{\mathfrak{b} - \mathfrak{a}}{2}\big)$, there exist $c>0$ depending only on the constants in the assumptions, and $\zeta_1 \in (0,\zeta_2)$ (which may depend on all the data) such that
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{x\in [\mathfrak{a}+\zeta_2, \mathfrak{b}]} \big(V^{\textnormal{eff}}_h(x) -
V^{\textnormal{eff}}_h(\mathfrak{a}+\zeta_1)\big) \leq c
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 V^{\textnormal{eff}}_h(\mathfrak{a}+\zeta_1)=\sup_{x\in [\mathfrak{a}+\zeta_1,\mathfrak{b}]}
V^{\textnormal{eff}}_h(x).
\end{equation}
Moreover, there exist $\zeta_1^\pm$ depending only on $\zeta_2$ and the constants in the
assumptions such that the choice of $\zeta_1$ can be made to obey $0<\zeta_1^-<\zeta_1<\zeta_1^+<\zeta_2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:07:56.653589+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6da7bd1d85199639a863f94a2f44d72cb52900ebbfb8e1bad32bc87bddbac00","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition MAL of arXiv:2411.04590, 'Singular stochastic delay equations driven by fractional Brownian motion: Dynamics, longti'] Let $\Phi: [s,t]\rightarrow \mathbb{R}^d$ be a stochastic process such that $\phi\in\mathcal{H}^d$ and
			\begin{align*}
				\mathbb{E} (\Vert \Phi\Vert_{\vert\mathcal{H}^d\vert}^2)+
				\sum_{1\leq i\leq d}\int_{s}^{t}\mathbb{E}(\Vert D^{B}\Phi^{i}(r)\Vert_{\vert\mathcal{H}^d\vert}^2) \mathrm{d}r<\infty .
			\end{align*}
			Assume that \( \text{Tr}_{[s,t]} D^B \Phi =\sum_{{1\leq i\leq d}} \text{Tr}_{[s,t]}D^{B^i}\Phi^{i}\) exists, where
			\begin{align}
				\text{Tr}_{[s,t]} D^{B^j} \Phi^i := \lim_{\epsilon \rightarrow 0} \int_{s}^{t} \frac{\langle D^{B^j} \Phi^i(x), \chi_{[x-\epsilon, x+\epsilon] \cap [s,t]} \rangle_{\mathcal{H}}}{2\epsilon} \, \mathrm{d}x,
			\end{align}
			where \( e_k \) denotes the usual unit vector in \(\mathbb{R}^d\).
			Then the symmetric integral is well-defined and
			\begin{align*}
				\int_{s}^{t}\Phi(x)\mathrm{d}^{\circ}B_{x}=\delta^{B}(\Phi)+Tr_{[s,t]} D^{B}\Phi .
			\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:40.044680+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6e0d2c6252cb6bb0362cf1ad7fdd86e2aab70bc191c9b9500d108f081251846","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 24 of arXiv:2510.24244, 'Statistical properties of Markov shifts: part II-LLT'] For every $0<\delta<T$ and $L\in\bbN$ there exists $0<\delta(L)<1/2$ such that if
\begin{equation}

\varepsilon\leq \delta(L) 
\end{equation}
then  for every $j$ and $\ell\leq L+1$ we have the following.
If  for some nonzero real $t$ such that $\del \leq |t|\leq T$ there exists $h$ with 
$\|h\|_{\om,*}\leq 1$ and
\begin{equation}

\inf_{x}|\cL_{j,t}^{\ell}h(x)|\geq 1-\varepsilon 
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
f_{j+\ell-1}=g_{j,t,\ell}+H_{j+\ell-1}-H_{j+\ell}\circ T_{j+\ell-1}+(2\pi/t) Z_{t,j,\ell}
\end{equation}
where $Z_{t,j,\ell}$ is integer valued, and for every $0<r<1$ we have 
