{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:45:27.885978+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7cf3c42ea78ea55fed4abbe8ebfb7c165da0d23c78530bd74c3b3525fd4ab63","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:maximum principle trunc of arXiv:2607.02367, 'Flux solutions for stochastic chemical systems with sources and sinks'] Assume that the sequence of functions  $\psi^N (t)= (\psi_N(t, n ))_{n \in \mathbb N^M }  $ that are such that for every $n \in \mathbb N^M $ the function  $ t \mapsto \psi_N(t, n )$ is continuously differentiable and satisfies the following equation 
        \begin{align} 
 \partial_s  \psi^N(s, n)   -  
   \mathcal L^N_r[ \psi^N (s) ](n) - \bar{ \mathcal L}_s^N [ \psi^N (s) ](n) \leq 0 , \quad s \geq 0  \quad n \in \mathbb N^M     
\end{align}
    with initial datum $ \psi (0) = \psi_0 \in c_{00} (\mathbb N^M)$.
    Then 
    \begin{align*}
       \sup_{ t \in [0, \infty) } \max_{ n \in \mathbb N^M } \psi^N (t, n ) \leq    \sup_{ n \in \mathbb N^M } \psi_0 ( n ). 
    \end{align*}
    Moreover if $\psi^N $ attains a maximum at time $ t= \overline t  $, then $ \psi^N $ is constant. 

    Assume that the sequence of functions  $\psi^N (t)= (\psi_N(t, n ))_{n \in \mathbb N^M }  $ that are such that for every $n \in \mathbb N^M $ the function  $ t \mapsto \psi_N(t, n )$ is continuously differentiable and satisfies
        \begin{align} 
 \partial_s  \psi^N(s, n)   -  
   \mathcal L_r[ \psi^N (s) ](n) -  \mathcal L_s [ \psi^N (s) ](n) \geq 0 , \quad s\geq 0, \quad n \in \mathbb N^M     . 
\end{align}
    with initial datum $ \psi (0) = \psi_0 \in c_{00} (\mathbb N^M)$.
    Then 
    \begin{align*}
  \inf_{ t \in [0, \infty)   } \min_{ n \in \mathbb N^M } \psi^N (t, n ) \geq   \sup_{ n \in \mathbb N^M } \inf_0 ( n ) . 
    \end{align*}
    Moreover if $\psi^N $ attains a minimum at time $ t= \overline t  $, then $ \psi^N $ is constant.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:52:47.246499+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e7cfa7365ce4d6a65045acc26cdcbf0b5d626482938204688371327aeafaf296","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2609.01098, 'Gaussian upper bounds for averaged heat semigroups on graphs'] The {\em relative Faber-Krahn inequality} $FK_a(n,r_1,r_2)$ holds in $B$, if for all $x\in B$, $r\in[r_1,r_2]$ and $U\subset B_x(r) $, we have
