{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:12:13.497911+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e964b25d39b234419e622679b7e294b1aeabe1f57c65a182ddf66ce88f9fb89d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.28410, 'Mean curvatures and symmetry\\of convex hypersurfaces'] Let $M\subset\R^{n+1}$ be a $\C^2$ hypersurface bounding a convex body $K$, and $e\in\S^n$ be a direction. Suppose that, for some $1\leq m\leq n$,
\begin{equation}
\sigma_m(p)\leq\sigma_m(q)
\end{equation}
whenever $p-q=\lambda e$, for some $\lambda\geq 0$, and the open
segment joining $p$ and $q$ lies in the interior of $K$. Then $M$ is symmetric with respect to a hyperplane orthogonal to $e$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:30.015302+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e96ed9d3d67361a0a85e33d142ee3d7f7e8f0d10edecaba53033f0b710fcb1b6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 of arXiv:2605.05430, 'Dirichlet problems and exit distributions for the telegraph process and its planar extensi'] For $x\in[a,b]$, it holds that
\begin{equation}h_0(x)=\frac{\lambda}{c^2}\,(b-x)(x-a)+\frac{b-x}{c}\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}h_1(x)=\frac{\lambda}{c^2}\,(b-x)(x-a)+\frac{x-a}{c}\end{equation}
Consequently, \begin{equation}h(x)=\frac{\lambda}{c^2}\,(b-x)(x-a)+\frac{b-a}{2c}.\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:41:19.330832+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e9767ce995efad0bb77d5d6026fe750d4300bc54c0dec106cde3eb7e47dfc1d2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Theorem~2 in \cite{HebischSikora1990} of arXiv:2609.04045, 'Heat content and spectrum for subordinated sub-Laplacians'] Let $\mathbb G$ be any homogeneous Carnot group equipped with the  dilations $(\delta_\lambda)_{\lambda>0}$. Then there exists $\varepsilon_0>0$ such that for every $0<\kappa<\varepsilon_0$, 
    \begin{align*}
        \rho(x):=\inf\{t>0: |\delta_{1/t} x|<\kappa \}
    \end{align*}
    defines a symmetric homogeneous norm on $\mathbb G$. In particular, the unit metric ball $\{x\in\mathbb G: \rho(x)<1\}$ coincides with the Euclidean ball $B_{\mathbb{R}^n}(0,\kappa)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

