{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.572947+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea495541c5f289b1bd4d949d6e93f2d4da80d1c6bcbc0eaac53681d75f00d709","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 21 (Domain Markov property) of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $d\geq 2$ and $\beta\geq 0$. Let $G=(V,E),H=(V',E')$ be subgraphs of $\mathbb Z^d$ with $G\subset H$. Let $\xi\in \{0,1\}^{E'\setminus E}$. Then, for every event $\mathcal A\subset \{0,1\}^E$, one has
 \begin{equation}
 	\phi_{H,\beta}^0[\mathcal A \: |\: \omega_{|E'\setminus E}=\xi]=\phi_{G,\beta}^\xi[\mathcal A].
