{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:59:53.385951+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea4a87c8565655625edf776bba47275ee97891974cd25b43fdb28036ddb04342","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition appendix:zeros:integer_beta of arXiv:2608.18320, 'Asymptotics for Beta-Splitting Trees via Homogeneous Fragmentations and Meromorphic Potent'] [Zeros and poles for positive integer $\beta$]

        Let \(\beta>0\)  be an integer. Then 
        \begin{enumeratei} 
            \item the function \(f_\beta\) has exactly \(\beta+1\) simple zeros and \(\beta+1\) simple poles, the latter being exactly \(\{-\beta,-\beta+1,\dots, 0 \}\);

            \item if \(\beta\) is even, then \(f_\beta\) has a unique real zero at \(w=\beta+2\), and \(\beta\) nonreal zeros, which appear in conjugate pairs;

            \item if \(\beta\) is odd, then \(w=\beta+2\) and \(w=-2(\beta+1)\) are the real zeroes of \(f_\beta\), and its remaining \(\beta-1\) zeros are nonreal and appear in conjugate pairs;

            \item  every zero \(w_0\) of \(f_\beta\) satisfies 
             \[
                \operatorname{Re}(w_0)\in\left[-2(\beta+1),\beta+2\right],
                \qquad\text{and}\qquad
                \left|\operatorname{Im}(w_0)\right|<\left(3\beta+4\right)/{2}.
        \]    
        \end{enumeratei}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:28:34.963585+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea5a237c6776530ead41f02cc6f42071aced0c926419d501bcacba7cf63979da","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Teo_Krylov of arXiv:2412.05950, 'Quantitative particle approximation of nonlinear stochastic Fokker-Planck equations with s'] Assume $(\mathbf{A}^c)$ and one of the two regimes:
				\begin{enumerate}
					\item $(\mathbf{A}^F)$ and $(\mathbf{A}^K)$.
					\item $(\mathbf{A}^I)$ and $(\mathbf{A}^K)$.
									\end{enumerate}
				Let $0 \le \rho_0 \in L^1 \cap H^{1-\frac{2}{q}}_q\left(\mathbb{T}^d\right)$, with $\Arrowvert \rho_0 \Arrowvert_{1}=1$. For each $\kappa>0$, there exists a $T>0$ depending on $\kappa,\lambda, q, C_K, L, M$ and $d$ such that if $\Arrowvert \rho_0 \Arrowvert_{q} = \kappa$,  the SPDE ((SPDE_Ito of the paper)) admits a unique nonnegative solution in
				\begin{align*}
					\mathbb{M} \doteq L_{\mathcal{F}^B}^q\left([0, T]; H_q^{1}\left(\mathbb{T}^d\right)\right) \cap S_{\mathcal{F}^B}^{\infty}\left([0, T] ; L^1 \cap L^q\left(\mathbb{T}^d\right)\right).
				\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.257881+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea5bbdd148e4e6b665a90902de18ffc18a29537d13de183fb487b970f9b847ab","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prp 70 of arXiv:2605.23308, 'Convergence rate estimates for semigroups and heat kernels associated with resistance form'] Let $f\in C_c^{+}(\R_{\ge 0})$ and set 
  \begin{align*}
    \omega(h):=\sup_{\substack{s,t\in [0,\sigma_f]\\|s-t|\le h}}|f(s)-f(t)|,\;h\ge 0,\qquad
  \omega^{-1}(r):=\sup\{h>0:\omega(h)<r\},\;r>0.
  \end{align*}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item [$\rm{(i)}$]For any $x\in T^f$ and $r>0$, we have
    \begin{align*}
      \omega^{-1}\left(\frac{r}{2}\right)\wedge\frac{\sigma_f}{2}\le m^f(B(x,r)).
    \end{align*}
    \item [$\rm{(ii)}$]For any $t\in [0,\sigma_f]$ and $r>0$, let $I(t,r)$ be the connected component of $\{s\in[0,\sigma_f]:f(s)>f(t)-r\}$ containing $t$.
    Suppose that there exists a function $V:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$ such that
    \begin{align*}
      \rm{Leb}(I(t,r)\cap \{|f-f(t)|<r\})\le V(r),\qquad\forall t\in [0,\sigma_f],\;r>0,
    \end{align*}
where $\rm{Leb}$ is the one dimensional Lebesgue measure.
    Then it holds that 
    \begin{align*}
      m^f(B(x,r))\le V(r),\qquad \forall x\in T^f,\;r>0.
    \end{align*}
  \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:51:44.767901+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea71953d3c4555670a8179dcdf4b0e82e0fcafb842ab6345562e31cc32b38014","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Ingredient 21 of arXiv:2608.26803, 'On bubbling of Fueter maps'] Let $V$ be a Hausdorff topological vector space, $W$ a Banach space and $j: V \to W$ a continuous linear map with dense image. Let $K$ be a compact smooth manifold of dimension $k$ and let $\cR \subset W$ be Fredholm $C^1$-rectifiable of codimension $n$. If $k<n$, then 
    \begin{equation*}
        \{ f \in C^\infty(K, V) \, | \, (j\circ f)(K) \cap \cR = \emptyset \}
    \end{equation*} 
    is comeager in $C^\infty(K, V)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:07:35.020373+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea75722dabf09ee4a0ba705e7f5110bcb5208e7dc3bb072bdf82cee6d3d0a0e0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 37 of arXiv:2609.02878, '2609.02878'] Assume the semiample limiting-class setting of
\cref{sec:fano-fibration-setup}, let \(q\in Y^{\rm prod}\), and let
\(\xi=(\mu_t)_{t<T}\) be a fixed limiting point based at \(q\).  The
time-\((-1)\) model of every tangent space at \(\xi\) splits globally,
biholomorphically, and isometrically as
\begin{equation}
                         Z\cong\C^m\times Z',
                         \qquad \dim_\C Z'=n-m.
\end{equation}
The factor \(Z'\) is complete, normal, and klt; its K\"ahler current has
bounded local potentials and a local ambient lower bound.  Write \(f_Z\)
for the normalized tangent shrinker potential.  At time \(-1\),
\begin{equation}
 f_Z(z,y)=\frac{|z|^2}{4}+\ell(z)+f'(y),
\end{equation}
where \(\ell\) is real affine-linear and \(f'\) is locally Lipschitz.
Moreover,
\begin{equation}
                 \int_{\Reg Z'}e^{-f'}\,dV_{g'}<\infty,
\end{equation}
and, writing \((\mathcal X_s,g_\infty(s))\) for the tangent flow, there
is a residual ancient flow \(g'(s)\) such that in the canonical gauge
\begin{equation}
 (\mathcal X_s,g_\infty(s))
 \cong(\C^m,g_{\rm E})\times(Z',g'(s)),\qquad s<0.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:30.074581+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea769e3395895506b0dc6b9645ecec909b296c7259d3bce752a035365163b2dc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2606.02036, 'Self-intersection local times for Volterra Gaussian processes in stochastic flows with int'] The random variable 

$
T^{u}_k \coloneq \lplim{\ve\to0} T^{u}_{\ve,k}
$

is said to be the $k$-multiple self-intersection local time for $u$  if the limit exists for some $p\geqslant 2.$ If the limit exists, then the random variable $T^{u}_k$ does not depend on the choice of the approximating family $\{f_{\ve} :  \ve >0 \}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.070070+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea798af1d08e824d75bfb658d2e2b9b78fa5d9961afcdf0e6951c6bee22b54fe","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem3 of arXiv:2606.28631, '\Large \bf On the maximal displacement of subcritical branching random walks with stretche'] Define $\psi(u):=\sum_{n=0}^\infty p_n(1-u)^n+ mu-1$ with the convension $0^0=1$. Then the function $u(x):= \mathbb{P}(M>x)$ satisfies
	\begin{align}
		u(x)=\frac{1-p_0}{m}\sum_{n=1}^\infty m^{n} \mathbb{P}(T_x^+=n)-\sum_{n=1}^\infty m^{n-1}\mathbb{E}\left( \psi\left(u(x-S_n)\right)1_{\{T_x^+>n\}}\right).
	\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.780345+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea7a801d555f84edc3ee31b9bebd1c541637122d85ceb764cd5fef85618ea4c4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Moment Central Limit Theorem of arXiv:2411.01633, 'On the Limit of the Tridiagonal Model \\for $\beta$-Dyson Brownian Motion'] Let $M(t)$ be the stationary GOE processes. Then for $k$ fixed times $t_1,\dots ,t_k$ and exponents $j_1, \dots ,j_k$ the vector 
\begin{equation*}
    \left(n^{-j_1/2}\left(\mathrm{Tr}(M(t_1)^{j_1})-\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Tr}(M(t_1)^{j_1})]\right),\dots,n^{-j_2/2}\left(\mathrm{Tr}(M(t_k)^{j_k})-\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Tr}(M(t_k)^{j_k})]\right)\right),
\end{equation*}
converges both weakly and in the sense of moments to a Gaussian vector, as $n\to\infty$. Moreover, as a process
\begin{equation*}
    \left(n^{-j_1/2}\left(\mathrm{Tr}(M(t)^{j_1})-\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Tr}(M(t)^{j_1})]\right),\dots,n^{-j_k/2}\left(\mathrm{Tr}(M(t)^{j_k})-\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Tr}(M(t)^{j_k})]\right)\right),
\end{equation*}
converges weakly in $C[0,\infty)$ to a Gaussian process. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.424096+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea7d15b9ba4a4ff4b1e9757a256dfa05af1ac27cc1eea844ceab0ca7093f7286","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 150 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Let $\boldsymbol{\chi}_{\N}^{(1)}$ and $\boldsymbol{\chi}_{\N}^{(2)}$ be two families (indexed by a large parameter $\N$) of discrete random $d$-dimensional real vectors. We say that the distribution of $\boldsymbol{\chi}_{\N}^{(1)}$ is asymptotically equal to the distribution of $\boldsymbol{\chi}_{\N}^{(2)}$ if
\begin{equation}

 \lim_{\N\rightarrow\infty} \sup_{f \in \textnormal{Lip}_1} \big|\E\big[f(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{\N}^{(1)})\big]-\E\big[f(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{\N}^{(2)})\big]\big| =0,
\end{equation}
where $\textnormal{Lip}_1$ is the set of $1$-bounded $1$-Lipschitz functions
\[
 \textnormal{Lip}_1=\big\{f:\amsmathbb{R}^d \rightarrow \amsmathbb{R} \quad \big|\quad \forall \boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{y} \in \amsmathbb{R}^d \quad |f(\x)-f(\y)| \leq |\!|\x-\y|\!|_{\infty}\,\,\textnormal{ and }\, |f(\x)|\leq 1\big\}.
\]
If \eqref{eq_asymptotic_equivalence} holds, we write $\chi_{\N}^{(1)} \stackrel{\textnormal{d}}{\sim} \chi_{\N}^{(2)}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.902170+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea81724dfe94153affde7bb4e39e9cf91671fb566710483d0656cc57bd1ec48e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma normbound:nullspace of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] Assume that the linear measurement operator $\mathcal{A}: \mathbb{R}^{d_1 \times d_2} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^m$ satisfies the NSP of order $1$ with constant $0 < \eta_1 < 1$. Then it holds that for any $\mathbf{N} \in \ker(\mathcal{A})$, 
\begin{equation} 
	\specnorm{\mathbf{N}} \leq \frac{\eta_1}{1+\eta_1}\nucnorm{\mathbf{N}}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.597347+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea87e260f9aaee6cb2978091fecc97b7499932f932353c88606ea527d5229717","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 14 of arXiv:2607.07283, 'Multi-time Markov renewal chains and stratified renewal theorems'] Under $\Pp_{\mathfrak a}$, the sequence

\begin{equation*}
\bigl(L_m,Y_m,R_m(g)\bigr)_{m\geq1}
\end{equation*}

is independent and identically distributed, for every non-negative reward $g$ and, by decomposition into positive and negative parts, for every integrable reward $g$. Under an arbitrary initial law, the cycles after the first entrance into $\mathfrak a$ are independent and identically distributed with the same law. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

