holds for any $s\in\left[s_0,\frac{e_0\tilde{\mu}_{n,N}}{\tilde{\sigma}_{n,N}}\right]$ when $N$ large enough.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:01:39.423202+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea914fe12a6d2aecb4c67828041d96a3961c2305c3d10b468d431ad557b417b9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:Phi1-Phi2-fg of arXiv:2607.22156, 'A random matrix approach to lamplighter groups'] Let $f,f'\in L$ and $g,g'\in\Gamma$. We have
    \begin{equation}
        \Phi^{(1)}(U_{f,g})=\tau_{G}(U_{f,g})=\1_{f=e_{L},\;g=e_{\Gamma}}.
