{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.583992+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eae7f8955153226ef852521784d4c8ff4ee5f3c2d5a4abd3149a3c4e803f5084","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:stable of arXiv:2411.09716, 'Stable Tables'] For preferences $\pi\in \Pi_n$:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(i)] If $\pi$ is regular, there is a unique stable matching.
        \item[(ii)] If $\pi$ is irregular and $n$ is even, a matching is stable if and only if no person is unmatched.
        \item[(iii)] If $\pi$ is irregular and $n$ is odd, there exists no stable matching.\footnote{Notice that since a cycle graph with an odd number of vertices is not bipartite, a stable matching is not guaranteed from Gale-Shapley.}
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

