{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.783681+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eaee64333788079387af770ac54d55291d2ac55891c8e701cc411c313c7a9c08","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 10 of arXiv:2609.03054, 'The classes of bivariate Schur and Herglotz matrix-valued rational functions: realizations'] Let $G\in \mathbb C^{n\times n} (s,p)$ be a rational  matrix  function. Then the following conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[\rm (i)]
\item $G$ has no singularities in $\mathbb{GH}$, the matrix $G(s,p)$ has nonnegative real part
for any $(s,p)\in \mathbb{GH}$,
and $\lim_{(s,p)\to\infty} G(s,p)$ exists;
\item $G$ admits a realization of the form
\begin{equation} 
G(s,p)=  D+  \frac12{C} \left(s I+ 2A -
    (s^2-4p) P(s I+ 2A)^{-1} P\right)^{-1}B.
\end{equation}
where the block matrix $\matr{A&B\\C&D} \in\Cmats{(k+n)}{(k+n)}$ is such that
\begin{equation*}
\matr{
A+A^* & C^*- B \\ C- B^* & D+D^*
} \geq 0
\end{equation*}
and $-I\le P \le I$.
\end{enumerate}
Additionally, if $G\in {\mathbb R}^{n\times n}(s,p)$, then the matrices $A,B,C,D$ and $P$ can be chosen as real.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:21:06.855045+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eaf6bc610b9afedb5f0d0390c0060ded30ed5680fce00d66686fc3f54e0b318c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2608.21014, 'The stochastic Cahn--Hilliard equation in critical spaces'] Let $1<q_0,q_1<\infty$, $1< p_0,p_1< \infty$ and $-4\leq s_0,s_1 \leq 4$.
