{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:41:19.327621+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec1d6190c05c9bd23383eda13ac7982390df4d5ea6ae37682d5c76e3c3c2f5b2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2609.04045, 'Heat content and spectrum for subordinated sub-Laplacians'] A continuous function $\|\cdot\|:\mathbb G\longrightarrow [0,\infty)$ is called a \emph{homogeneous quasinorm} on the Carnot group $\mathbb{G}$ if the following conditions hold:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item $\|\delta_\lambda x\|=\lambda \|x\|$ for all $\lambda>0$ and $x\in\mathbb G$,
    \item $\|x\|>0$ if and only if $x\neq 0$.
\end{enumerate}
The quasinorm is called \emph{symmetric} if $\|x\|=\|x^{-1}\|$ for all $x\in\mathbb G$. If $\|\cdot\|$ also subadditive, that is,
\begin{align*}
    \|xy\|\leqslant \|x\|+\|y\|, \quad x,y\in\mathbb G,
\end{align*}
we call it a \emph{homogeneous norm}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:04.991822+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec1e877f017f96fa832aeed8e234417794a087637837cd6c03de42837289d340","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2604.01257, '2604.01257'] Let conditions $[{{f}_{\nu }}]$, $[{{\mathcal{L}}_{\nu }}]$ and $[{{\omega }_{\nu }}]$ be satisfied.
    Then, the survival probability $q(t)$ has the following asymptotic representation:
