{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.754020+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec82dc786870e379b5b4fc9d4c90da5d33f4c82f4b6398c58a566f273e361d1b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem prop:chfuntheta2 of arXiv:2411.19192, 'Characteristic function and Esscher transform of a switching Levy model for the temperatur'] Let $(T_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be the temperature process given by equations ((eq:tempdyna of the paper)), ((eq:seass2 of the paper)) and ((eq:tchlevy2 of the paper)) starting at regime one. Then,  the Esscher measure $\mathcal{Q}^{\theta}$ is an EMM  if  for any $t>0$ the parameter $\theta$ verifies under the conditions $\lambda_{12}>max(\alpha+\theta,0)$ and  $\lambda_{12} < \lambda_{21}$ the equation:
 \begin{equation}
R(t,\theta)= \sigma^{-1} e^{\alpha t}(e^{rt} T_0-C_1(t, \alpha))
   \end{equation}
   where:
  \begin{eqnarray*}
 R(t,\theta)&=& -i  \left[ D_1(t,\theta) C_5(0, \theta)+ D_2(t,\theta) \right]\\

  \end{eqnarray*}
  The expressions $D_1(t,\theta)$ and $D_2(t,\theta)$  are given  by:
  \begin{eqnarray*}
  D_1(t,\theta) &=&  -i \alpha \left[\left(A_{12}(\theta, \alpha)+A_{21}(\theta, \alpha)\right)E_{\theta}[N_t]+
 A_{12}(\theta, \alpha) p^{\theta}_O(t) \right] \\ 
 && \\
 D_2(t,\theta) &:=&  \frac{dC_5(u \sigma^{1}e^{\alpha t}, \theta,k)}{du}|_{u=0} = \sum_{k=0}^{+\infty}   \frac{dJ^{\theta}_3(u \sigma^{-1}e^{\alpha t},k)}{du}|_{u=0}  p^{\theta}_{2k}(t)\\ \nonumber
  &+&  \sum_{k=0}^{+\infty}  \frac{dJ^{\theta}_4(u \sigma^{-1}e^{\alpha t},k)}{du}|_{u=0}p^{\theta}_{2k+1}(t) \\ 
 &&
  \end{eqnarray*}
 where $p^{\theta}_{O}(t)$ is the probability to observe an odd number of regime changes in the  interval $[0,t)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:43:04.728910+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec84239ab2a5d3dfe35c67a3cb8be27c7cdb4bcf9a98acec134e9fa7a2761a78","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 19 of arXiv:2605.17494, 'Generalized intersection exponents and local cut points for three-dimensional Brownian loo'] For all $\alpha \in I_0$, the following holds almost surely:
