{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:58:01.553090+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ed43e6dbab641d34f24da9fdb597048fbad7c07589be7e2458b478da5603d8a7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.27156, 'Traveling Waves in Equity Markets with\\Rank-Based Entry and Exit'] Assume $\lambda_b, \lambda_d, b \in C^1([0,1])$ and $\sigma^2 \in C^1([0,1])$ with $\min_{[0,1]} \sigma^2 > 0$, and let the initial positions be independent samples from a distribution on $(0,\infty)$ with continuous CDF $u_0$ and $\E|\log X^1_0| < \infty$. Set $m(t) := e^{f(1)t}$. Then for every $T > 0$,
