where $C_{quantile, \lambda, \mu}$ is constant independent of $\gamma$ and $p$, defined in (equation eq: Gaussian Wasserstein-$p$ Lower bound, P_0 of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.670880+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ed6350482d0c16d2da53b1127356098b94d027cabe9f796dafb4c638e9d5ef88","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:Driscoll of arXiv:2605.28106, 'Gaussian Processes with Sample Paths in Reproducing Kernel Banach Spaces'] Let \( (\Omega,\mathcal{A},\mu) \) be a complete probability space, let \( X \) be a separable reproducing kernel Banach space, and let \( \xi : T \times \Omega \to \mathbb{R} \) be a centered Gaussian process with covariance function \( K \) and associated Hilbert space \( H(K) \).
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item If \( H(K) \subseteq X \) and the embedding \( \iota : H(K) \to X \) is \( \gamma \)-radonifying, then there exists a centered Gaussian random element \( \eta : \Omega \to X \) such that \( \xi_t = \eta_t \) almost surely for all \( t \in T \).

        \item If \( H(K) \not\subseteq X \) or if the embedding \( \iota : H(K) \to X \) is not \( \gamma \)-radonifying, then
        \begin{equation*}
            \mu(\{ \omega \in \Omega : \xi(\cdot,\omega) \in X \}) = 0.
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:01:39.495341+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ed68407de703094ece0e79666dab3d0c6a09177de06304720400d9a13c6c0b60","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:error_est of arXiv:2607.22291, 'Rare subtree patterns in size-conditioned Bienaymé trees: Poisson approximation and declum'] Let $(m_{n})$ be a sequence of positive integers such that $m_{n}=o(n)$. Let $H : (\mathbb{Z}_{\geq -1})^{m_n} \to \{0,1\}$ be a function and let $(z_{n})$ be a sequence of positive real numbers such that $z_{n}=o(a_{n})$. Then there exists a constant~$C > 0$ (independent of $n$ and of $H$) such that for every $n \geq 1$ sufficiently large we have
