{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.753023+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee15090d5fcd33a31edda0822a1ae7d3536fc9fb0f7f9f187b04ff6666bacf27","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma tausdistrib of arXiv:2411.19192, 'Characteristic function and Esscher transform of a switching Levy model for the temperatur'] Let $\tau_l$ be the time of the l-th regime change, $f_{\tau_l}$ and $F_{\tau_l}$ its respective  probability density function (p.d.f.) and cumulative distribution function(c.d.f.). Then:
\begin{eqnarray*}
 F_{\tau_{2k}}(t)  &=& D(k,k,2k)  \sum_{l=0}^{+\infty}  C(k,2k,l)  \gamma(2k+l, \lambda_{21} t) \\
  F_{\tau_{2k+1}}(t)  &=&  D(k+1,k+1,2k+1) \sum_{l=0}^{+\infty} C(k+1,2k+1,l)  \gamma(2k+l+1, \lambda_{21} t)
\end{eqnarray*}
Moreover, let $N_t$  be  the number of transitions between regimes  in the interval $[0,t)$. Then, for $k \in \mathbb{N}$:
\begin{eqnarray} 
p_k(t):=P(N_t=k)  &=& F_{\tau_{k}}(t)-F_{\tau_{k+1}}(t)
\end{eqnarray}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:54:18.300311+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee173dd9257a40bde38db1688e9f6413e40d15764729911426bdfe4f987c1820","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop prop_ii of arXiv:2608.30382, 'Convergence rates for the RMSprop optimizer with full control of the hyperparameters'] Let $ \beta \in [ \nicefrac{ 1 }{ 2 }, 1 ) $, $ \varepsilon \in [0,\infty) $, $ n \in {\mathbb N} \backslash \{ 1 \} $, $ i \in \{1, \dots, d \} $,
let $ A_n \in \mathcal F_{ n - 1 } $, and let $ C_X^{(2)}, C_v^{(-1)} \in (0,\infty) $.
Assume that
\bas{
{\mathbb E}[(X^{(i)}(\theta_{n-1},U_n))^2|\mathcal F_{n-1}]\le C_X^{(2)}
\qquad\text{on }A_n
}
and
\bas{
{\mathbb E}[\1_{A_n}(v_{n-1}^{(i)})^{-1}]\le C_v^{(-1)}.
}
The negative-moment assumption implies that $v_{n-1}^{(i)}>0$ almost surely
on $A_n$. If $\varepsilon=0$, inverse factors used in the proof are evaluated on
$A_n$ and extended by zero to $A_n^c$.
Then one has
\bas{
{\mathbb E}\Bigl[\1_{A_n}(\sqrt{v_n^{(i)}}+\varepsilon)^{-2}
X^{(i)}(\theta_{n-1},U_n)^2\Bigr]
\le 4 C_X^{(2)} C_v^{(-1)}.
}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.801424+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee18cb574b334b833c48a1d6c3eeff0b3ac857372a736654767caab5b1085370","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:prn_integral of arXiv:2606.23547, 'The location of the largest exponential spacing and Euler’s generalized pentagonal numbers'] For $1\le r\le n$, the probability $p_{r,n}$ admits the integral representation
\begin{equation}
p_{r,n}
=\int_0^\infty r {\rm e}^{-r t}
\prod_{\substack{1\le k\le n\\k\neq r}}
\bigl(1-{\rm e}^{-k t}\bigr)\,{\rm d}t.
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
p_{r,n}
=\int_0^1 r\,u^{r-1}
\prod_{\substack{1\le k\le n\\k\neq r}}
(1-u^k)\,{\rm d}u.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:02:08.103043+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee1a0dab3088f71effc3d48f3fffa9794815c9873581177c2755ee92523437ec","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2608.27374, '\Large \bf Distribution-constrained optimal multiple stopping: the Root-type solution'] For every \(t\in[t_0,r_\xi^\beta)\) and \(x\in\mathbb R\),
\begin{equation}

    u^\beta(t,x)
    =
    \inf_{\rho\in\mathcal T_t^\Gamma}
    \E^x\!\left[
        v^\xi(t-\rho,Y_\rho)
        +
        w^\beta(t-\rho)\mathbf 1_{\{\rho<t\}}
    \right].
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.042794+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ee1c3b30e32ed97ba395b174f5793e54b271ccb24f669f659503d1c10ac0da15","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 63 of arXiv:2605.03236, 'Stochastic It\^o Equations and Parabolic Second-Order Equations with singular Drift'] For any $\kappa\in(0,1)$
there is a 
function $q(\xi)$, $\xi\in(0,1)$,
depending only on $\kappa,\delta,d $, 
and, naturally, on $\xi$,
such that for any $R\leq  \rho_{b}$, 
$y\in \bR^{d}$, $s\geq0$ on the set $A=\{ x_{s}\in
\bar B_{\kappa R}(y)\}$, for any
  closed $\Gamma\subset   C_{ R}(s,y)$ satisfying
$|\Gamma|\geq \xi|C_{ R}|$ we have
\begin{equation}

P _{\cF_{s}}(\tau_{\Gamma}  < s+\theta_{s}
\tau_{R}(y))\geq q(\xi),
\end{equation}
where $\tau_{\Gamma}  $ is the first time after $s$ the process 
$(t,  x_{t})$
hits $\Gamma$. Furthermore, 
$q(\xi)\to 1$ as $\xi\uparrow 1$.
Finally, for any closed $\Gamma'\subset B_{ R}$ satisfying
$|\Gamma'|\geq \xi|B_{ R}|$ on $A$ we have
