{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:12.197827+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ef72586f2c6a69aec737b411d8405abd03270c04df6297247bd33c91ad641774","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 44 of arXiv:2510.22783, 'Universality of Cutoff for Riffle Shuffling'] For any $K \geq (\widetilde C_{\mu}+\varepsilon) \log N$ and $i\in \{\iota(p),\ldots,\tau(p)-2\}$, and any $\xi>0$, there exists an event $F_i=F_i(\xi) \in \sigma(\mathbf a_i)$ and $\zeta=\zeta(\mu,\xi)$, such that the following holds.
        \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
            \item 

            We have
            \begin{equation} 
                \mathbb P(F_{i}) \geq 1- \exp(-N^{\zeta}).
            \end{equation}
            \item 

            There exists $C(\mu,\chi)>0$ such that if $s_i \in \mathsf S^\p _{\for}$, we have that for all $x \in \mathsf S^\p_{\for}$ and $x>_{\lex} s_i$, 
            \begin{equation}

                \mathbb E\left[\left|\cI(B_x) \right|\big\vert \mathbf a_i\right] \leq C(\mu,\chi)N(\lambda_{x})^{1-o_{\chi}(1)}\cdot N^{O(\chi)}.
