{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:19:42.477349+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f0a67e5dac78862baec6b1bdafd898ff56115f6a5d2cc37e12445334e8d99c24","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 (Single-edge expansion) of arXiv:2609.03466, 'Least-Favorable Location for Binomial Top-$t$ Selection'] The rate $I_1$ is invariant under $p\mapsto T(p)$ and has its unique minimum at $\pc$. At the center,
\begin{equation}
 \Jn:=I_1(\pc)=-\log(1-\delta^2),
 \qquad
 I_1''(\pc)=\frac{8\delta^2}{(1-\delta^2)^2}.

\end{equation}
On some closed interval $U\subset(0,1-\delta)$ centered at $\pc$, the single-edge probability has the strong expansion
\begin{equation}
 a_n(p)=n^{-1/2}e^{-nI_1(p)}
 \{A_1(p)+o_{C^2(U)}(1)\}.

\end{equation}
Here $A_1(p)$ is smooth, positive, and invariant under $p\mapsto T(p)$. At $p=\pc$,
\begin{align}
 a_n(\pc)
 &=A_{1,\delta}n^{-1/2}e^{-n\Jn}\{1+o(1)\},
 \notag\\
 a_n'(\pc)&=0,
 \\
 a_n''(\pc)
 &=-I_1''(\pc)A_{1,\delta}n^{1/2}e^{-n\Jn}\{1+o(1)\},

\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
 A_{1,\delta}:=A_1(\pc)=\frac1{2\delta\sqrt\pi}.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

