\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:56:36.592142+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f2c0f5310c14b6a8a6123fadaf00a419f647c2e9a297c74e438e86ee3c67ca98","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition DUFJ-INT-SJ-B of arXiv:2608.30582, 'The flexible part of intermittent Onsager theorem for the two-dimensional stochastic incom'] A random function $v\left( {x} \right)$ is said to be inertial-range intermittent, if for two positive exponents ${p_1} > {p_2}$, there exists an increasing function $\mathfrak{h}_2\left( r \right)$ such that
\begin{equation}
\frac{{{{\left\| {v\left( { \cdot  + l\mathbf{n}} \right) - v\left(  \cdot  \right)} \right\|}_{{L^{{p_1}}}}}}}{{{{\left\| {v\left( { \cdot  + l\mathbf{n}} \right) - v\left(  \cdot  \right)} \right\|}_{{L^{{p_2}}}}}}} \sim {\mathfrak{h}_2}\left( {r} \right) \to  \infty , \ \  \forall \, l \in \left[ {{l_D},{l_I}} \right] , \  \mbox{as}\ r \to  \infty , \ \mathbb{P}\mbox{-a.s.} 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:10.190585+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f2c2f41534160a4549e17ee410c277a716ee4639bb5da24d7d73872b510958a8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 179 of arXiv:2605.03236, 'Stochastic It\^o Equations and Parabolic Second-Order Equations with singular Drift'] Let $n\geq1$ and suppose that for 
