{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:25:49.851328+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f384586bb022d6e5670fe42ca4ca64ea1691a622d05b035b013d342b28963a74","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 9 of arXiv:2609.02017, 'Optimal girth-dependent bounds for the Bethe approximation of the permanent'] For $\bs{q}\in\Delta_d$, $\pi\in\on{Sym}(d)$, and $t\in[d]$, define
the {suffix mass}
\[
  s_t^\pi(\bs{q})
  :=\sum_{r=t}^d q_{\pi(r)}.
\]
Thus, $s_t^\pi(\bs{q})$ is the total $\bs{q}$-mass of the elements in
position $t$ or later in the ordering $\pi$. Define
\begin{align*}
  F(\bs{q})
  &:=\E_\pi\left[
       \sum_{t=1}^d q_{\pi(t)}
       \log\frac{s_t^\pi(\bs{q})}{q_{\pi(t)}}
     \right]
     -S(\bs{q})
  =\E_\pi\left[
       \sum_{t=1}^d q_{\pi(t)}
       \log s_t^\pi(\bs{q})
     \right]
     +H_{\mr c}(\bs{q}),
\end{align*}
where $\pi$ is uniformly distributed on $\on{Sym}(d)$.","status":"unverified"}

