{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:12:46.873506+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f3c1af10387cd5ac379576d4cc6f3456fa46c1e4333b94a915b6913ee2471ffd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:hill-paths of arXiv:2608.08375, 'Algebraic formulas for first-passage times of Markov processes in the linear framework: ge'] Under the conditions stated previously, if $k \in \overline{Z}$, then $u \leadsto_{\mathcal{H}_u[G]} k$ if, and only if, $u \leadsto_G k$.

\proof If $u = k$, the conclusion is trivial, so assume that $u \neq k$. If $u \leadsto_{\mathcal{H}_u[G]} k$, then there is a path, $u = j_1 \to \dotsb \to j_n = k$, of edges in $\mathcal{H}_u[G]$. We may assume that this path does not revisit $u$, so that $u \neq j_2, \dotsc, j_{n-1}$, for otherwise we can simply consider the portion of this path starting from the last visit to $u$. Since none of these edges now enter $u$, it follows from Definition~(def:hill of the paper).1 that they must have arisen from edges in $G$, so that $u \leadsto_G k$. Now suppose that $u \leadsto_G k$, so that there exists a directed path, $u = i_1 \to \dotsb \to i_m = k$, of edges in $G$. As before, we may assume that $u \neq i_2, \dotsc, i_m$, so that none of the edges enter $u$. Since $k$ is non-terminal, each vertex along the path must also be non-terminal. It then follows from Definition~(def:hill of the paper).1 that each edge must also exist in $\mathcal{H}_u[G]$, so that $u \leadsto_{\mathcal{H}_u[G]} k$.
\hfill \qed (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:34.577961+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f3c32f21620f9e14fb832cab6840dafce0e454f47848ba100b6a112263b0dcc1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2606.14876, 'High-Order Talagrand and Eldan--Gross Inequalities via Besov-Type Variance Functionals'] For all $k\in \Nbb_{\leq n}$, $f:\cube^n\to [-1,1]$, and $p\in [1,2]$,
\begin{equation}
    \left\|\left|\grad^k f\right|\right\|_{\Leb^p}^{p} \geq C^{\text{\rm\ref{thm:high-order-Poincare-type-Boolean}}}(p)^k\Wop^{\geq k}(f),
\end{equation}
with $C^{\text{\rm\ref{thm:high-order-Poincare-type-Boolean}}}(p)=2^{1-p}/\Beta(\frac{1}{2};\frac{p}{2},\frac{p}{2})$, where $\Beta(x;a,b) := \int_0^x t^{a-1} \left(1-t\right)^{b-1} \dif t$ is the incomplete Beta function. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

