{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.252103+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f3df1782bd6746be74e9f9c6f328afd318ba2af1de554fa05c63f42d43697ced","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: Wasserstein-$p$ distance JSQ of arXiv:2603.14163, '\LARGE \bf Tail Bounds for Queues with Abandonment: Constant, Moderate, Large Deviations, '] \textnormal{(Wasserstein-$p$ distance for JSQ)} In the heavily overloaded regime (ass:heavy_overload of the paper), with $\gamma\leq\gamma_1$ (equation eq: gamma assumption for JSQ W-p of the paper), the Wasserstein-$p$ distance between law $\mathcal{L}(\tilde{q}_{\Sigma})$ and standard normal distribution $\mathcal{L}(Z):=\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ is bounded in:







    \begin{subnumcases}{\mathcal{W}_p(\mathcal{L}(\tilde{q}_{\Sigma}), \mathcal{L}(Z)) \leq E'_{\lambda,\mu,n} \cdot}
     p\sqrt{\gamma}, & if $p \in (1, 1/\gamma^{\delta}]$ \\
     \sqrt{p} , & if $p \in [1/\gamma^{1/2-\alpha}, 1/\gamma)$ \\
     \frac{p\sqrt{\gamma}}{\log(1+\frac{n\gamma}{\lambda}p)}, & if $p \in [1/\gamma, \infty)$ 
