{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:30:36.800752+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4aea69bc387fadf0f574fe6967f657ad06c8a6132a1a5221903902f78310b19","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 48 of arXiv:2607.10486, 'Weak martingale solutions to stochastic Navier-Stokes-Cahn-Hilliard system with transport '] Assume that $z \in \nU$. If $0 \leq s \leq t \leq T$, we define the following maps
\begin{align}

I_{s,t}^{n,z} &: \bZ_T \ni \bX \mapsto \int_{s}^{t} e^{i \langle \bX(r), z \rangle} \varphi_n^{z}(r,\bX(r)) \, \d r \in \mathbb{C},
\\

I_{s,t}^{z} &: \bZ_T \ni \bX \mapsto \int_{s}^{t} e^{i \langle \bX(r), z \rangle} \varphi^{z}(r,\bX(r)) \, \d r \in \mathbb{C},
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}

\varphi_n^{z}(r,\bX(r))&= i \; \duality{\bF_n(\bX(r))}{z}{}{} 
- \frac{1}{2} \Vert \duality{\newG_{0,n}(r,\bX(r))}{z}{}{} \Vert_{\ell^2}^2,
\\

\varphi^{z}(r,\bX(r))&= i \; \duality{\bF(\bX(r))}{z}{}{} 
- \frac{1}{2} \Vert \duality{\newG_0(r,\bX(r))}{z}{}{} \Vert_{\ell^2}^2,
\end{align}
with
   \begin{align}
     \newG_0: [0,T] \times \mathbb{V} \ni (t,\bX=(\bu,\phi))\mapsto (\bSi_0(t)\bu + \bG_0(t,\bu), 0)_{k=1}^\infty\coloneqq (\newG_{0}^k(t,\bu))_{k=1}^\infty \in 
     \ell^2(\nHB).
  \end{align}
Then the maps $I_{s,t}^{n,z}$  and $I_{s,t}^{z}$ are measurable.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.275916+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4aeaa92b6ede92ddab0e696a9852a5413cbb3efebc5c000452d4cb70dad326f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 (Poisson-gamma representation) of arXiv:2606.22066, 'A Bessel-zero obstruction to hyperbolic complete monotonicity of noncentral chi-square den'] For \(a,b>0\) and \(\theta\ge0\), \(p_{a,b,\theta}\) is a probability density on \((0,\infty)\), and
\begin{equation}

p_{a,b,\theta}(x)
=
e^{-\theta}\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}
\frac{\theta^m}{m!}\,
\gamma_{a+m,b}(x),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\gamma_{\alpha,b}(x)
=
\frac{b^\alpha}{\Gamma(\alpha)}x^{\alpha-1}e^{-bx}.
\end{equation}
Equivalently, if \(N\sim\mathrm{Poisson}(\theta)\), then \(p_{a,b,\theta}\) is the density of a gamma law with random shape \(a+N\) and fixed rate \(b\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:42:39.076237+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4aeb14ccbafecd9aecbaa96a87f942da88d8f9a2232ce51911d9e2b3b562d3f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem convergence of conditioned ER graphs wrt resistance metric of arXiv:2412.08236, 'Aging and sub-aging for Bouchaud trap models on resistance metric spaces'] If a sequence $(m_{n})_{n \geq 1}$ of natural numbers satisfies $n^{-2/3}m_{n} \to \sigma \in (0,\infty)$,
  then it holds that
  \begin{equation}
    (V(G_{m_{n}}^{p_{n}}), n^{-1/3} R_{G_{m_{n}}^{p_{n}}}, \rho_{G_{m_{n}}^{p_{n}}}, n^{-2/3} \dot{\mu}^{\#}_{G_{m_{n}}^{p_{n}}})
    \xrightarrow{\mathrm{d}}
    (M^{(\sigma)}, R_{M^{(\sigma)}}, \rho_{M^{(\sigma)}}, \mu_{M^{(\sigma)}} \otimes \tilde{p})
  \end{equation}
  in the space $\mathfrak{M}_{c}(\markedfinMeasFunct{\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.702700+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4af397310edbd7e7768512f450aea328c64e5b0e474fb505580f74cb01ca765","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop edgeworth-prop of arXiv:2605.29095, 'Asymptotics of the number of components of random polynomial lemniscates'] For any sequence $C_n$ of non-negative numbers such that $\sqrt{n}C_n \to 0$, 
    \begin{align}
        \mathbb{P}&\bigl[ \log |P_n(X_0)| > nC_n, X_0 \in \mathbb{A}_n\bigr] \\& \qquad\quad= 2 \bigintsss_{ 1- \kappa\sqrt{\frac{\log n}{n}}}^1 (1- \Phi(C_r(n)))rdr + 2 \bigintsss_{ 1- \kappa\sqrt{\frac{\log n}{n}}}^1 \frac{Q_1(C_r(n))}{\sqrt{n}}rdr + O\left(\frac{1}{n} \right), \nonumber
    \end{align}
    where $\Phi$ is the distribution function of a standard normal random variable. In particular, by taking $C_n = 0$, 
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb{E}[Area(\mathbb{D}\setminus \Lambda_n)] = 2 \pi\bigintsss_{ 1- \kappa\sqrt{\frac{\log n}{n}}}^1 \biggl(1- \Phi\biggl(\frac{\sqrt{n}(1-r^2)}{2\sigma(r)}\biggr)\biggr)rdr + O\biggl(\frac{1}{n}\biggr).
\end{equation}
    Moreover, as $n\to \infty$, 
    \begin{equation}
      \sqrt{n} \bigintsss_{ 1- \kappa\sqrt{\frac{\log n}{n}}}^1 (1- \Phi(C_r(n)))rdr \to   \sqrt{\frac{1}{2\pi}Var(\log(|1-X_1|))}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:00:30.495993+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4bd35c4be90c0c568117056640f8a621ecc402af3eb162610fc3819fbbba17f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2607.04193, 'Onsager--Machlup Functionals for Generalized Newtonian Equations of Motion with Time-Varyi'] Let $f \in I_{a^+}^\alpha(L^p)$ and $g \in I_{b^-}^\alpha(L^p)$ with $p \ge 1$. The left-sided and right-sided Riemann--Liouville fractional derivatives of order $\alpha > 0$ are defined respectively by
    \begin{align*}
        (D_{a^+}^\alpha f)(x) &:= \left( \frac{d}{dx} \right)^{[\alpha] + 1} I_{a^+}^{1 + [\alpha] - \alpha} f(x), \\
        (D_{b^-}^\alpha g)(x) &:= \left( -\frac{d}{dx} \right)^{[\alpha] + 1} I_{b^-}^{1 + [\alpha] - \alpha} g(x),
    \end{align*}
    where $[\alpha]$ denotes the integer part of $\alpha$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.048255+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4c2340a9f5074cad0be902aa4bcafeb4d3d232af946c297dc9b6d61a48a16eb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Mytheo 1 of arXiv:2607.00495, 'Rank deficiency of Bernoulli random matrices for growing corank'] For every $p \in (0,1/2]$ and $\varepsilon > 0$, there are $n_{\ref{Theorem A}}$ and $c_{\ref{Theorem A}} >0$ depending only on $p$ and $\varepsilon$ with the following property. Let $n \ge n_{\ref{Theorem A}}$, $1 \le k \le c_{\ref{Theorem A}}\sqrt{\log{n}}$, and let $B_{n}(p)$ be $n \times n$ random matrix with independent entries $b_{ij}$ such that $\mathbb{P}(b_{ij}=0)=1-p$ and $\mathbb{P}(b_{ij}=1)=p$. Then we have 
   \begin{align*}
       \mathbb{P}\left(  \mathrm{rank}\left(B_{n}(p) \right) \le n-k   \right) \le \left( 1-p+\varepsilon \right)^{kn}.
   \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.409703+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f4c302f7655b1419bad23e45a316c9d2857502614ecd709bcec92c167574648e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2604.07207, 'Mixing times of step-reinforced random walks'] Under Assumption \ref{mainassum}, if $\mu$ is symmetric, then for any $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$,
       \begin{equation}

 t^{(\alpha)}_{\mathrm{mix}}(\varepsilon) \leq  \frac{C}{1-\alpha} \log \left(\frac{|G|}{\varepsilon}\right) \frac{1}{\gamma_*},
