{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:33:21.477491+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5d46b14b4d0c50f28d32e9b797acd1afd43799c529a73a867637f50d7ef2b82","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:simplex of arXiv:2606.21391, 'Extreme points and faces in the moment problem'] The convex set $K$ is a \emph{simplex}

\footnote{Simplices are called \emph{Choquet simplices} in [winkler85].}

if the convex cone 
\[
\{\lambda(x,1): \lambda\geq 0, x\in K\}\subset V\times\mathbb{R}
\] 
is a lattice in its own order [phelps01]. A \emph{$k$-simplex}, $k\in\mathbb{N}_0$,
is defined as the convex hull of any $k+1$ affinely independent points.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:54:15.658769+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5d7dbe991cd9de17df3e5758031098a29654b82d9a3ae881f0d264efc678b51","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 11 of arXiv:2608.09419, 'Left-tail expansions for Schr\"oder branching processes with explicit convergence rates'] \lb{T4} Suppose that the probability generating function $P(z)$ satisfies $P(0)=0$ and $0<p:=P'(0)<1$. Then, the unique solution of the Shr\"oder functional equation 
\[\lb{118}
 \Phi(P(z))=p\Phi(z),\ \ \ \Phi(0)=0,\ \ \ \Phi'(0)=1
\]
exists in some neighborhood of $z=0$. It can be extended analytically to the open connected component of the filled Julia set $\cJ_P(0)$. For its computation, one may use
\[\lb{118a}
\Phi(z)=\lim_{t\to+\iy}p^{-t}\underbrace{P\circ...\circ P}_{t}(z).
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.441717+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5e0fad04444ec23b8c3bf8914e4de1b4379b17ff9cde862f4c04bc068e55f18","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2604.07478, 'Mixing Times and Cutoff for the Rook's Walk'] Let $\varphi$ be defined as in Definition \ref{defphi}. Then,
   \[\varphi(i) = {d \choose i}(n-1)^i\]
   \begin{proof}
      Fix $i \in \{0,\dots,d\}$. Now, $\varphi(i)$ counts the number of elements in $\mathbb{Z}_n^d$ with Hamming distance of exactly $i$ from $\hat{1}$. That is, the number of elements with $i$ non-one elements. There are ${d \choose i}$ different choices for which dimensions should be non-one, and $(n-1)$ choices for each of these dimensions. 
   \[\therefore \quad \varphi(i) = {d \choose i}(n-1)^i,\quad \forall i \in \{0,\dots,d\}\]
   \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.776930+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5eaf049cc8b64d7cadbb1e8d334413ff48fb5c1b5dbf93d169292fac1695470","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 5 of arXiv:2603.25459, 'Cram\'{e}r-type moderate deviation for double index permutation statistics'] Assume $W$ is defined as (\ref{statistic-mann-whitney-wilcoxon}), we have
\begin{align}
     \left|\frac{\mathbb{P}\left(W>z\right)}{1-\Phi(z)}-1\right| = O(1)(1+z^3)/(1/n_{1}+1/n_{2})^{1/2},
\end{align}
for $0\leq z \leq \min(n_{1},n_{2})^{1/6}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:58:38.060071+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5ed2aec5e936c75d1b9b00aa94009821acb32458f06e6776d7d0e132c2aa8da","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:simplified-left-kernel of arXiv:2606.19507, 'The $t$-Split Two-Periodic Aztec Diamond Model'] For $m,\,m' < tN$, the correlation kernel of the $t$-split model can be written as
    \begin{multline} 
        \left[\mathbb{K}_N(4m',2\xi'+j;4m,2\xi+i)\right]_{i,j=0,1} = \frac{\1_{m\leq m'}}{2\pi \mathrm{i}} \oint_{\gamma_{0,1}}\frac{\mathrm{d}z}{z} z^{\xi'-\xi}\Phi_{\alpha}(z)^{m-m'} \\
        -\frac{1}{(2\pi\mathrm{i})^2}\oint_{\gamma_1}\mathrm{d}w\oint_{\gamma_{0,1}} \frac{\mathrm{d}z}{z(z-w)}\frac{w^{\xi'+N}(z-1)^N}{z^{\xi+N}(w-1)^N}r_{\alpha,2}(w)^{tN-m'}r_{\beta,2}(w)^{(1/2-t)N} \\
        \times c(w) F_{\alpha,2}(w) F_{\beta,2}(w) \Phi_{\beta}(z)^{(t-1/2)N} \Phi_{\alpha}(z)^{m-tN}.
    \end{multline} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:45:07.258131+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5fa4833af035abd9deede802cc6dcaeba56a2e95ffe432abc152637a6bb39d4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 12 of arXiv:2604.19308, '\textbf{On McKean--Vlasov SDEs with polynomial drifts for SIS epidemic models}'] Let~\eqref{co:6} and~\eqref{eq:integrability condition} hold for $\rho = 1$ and $N_{\infty} := \limsup_{t\uparrow\infty} N(t)$ be positive. Then each positive solution $I$ to~\eqref{eq:SIS epidemic model} satisfies the following two statements:
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item If there is a continuous function $f_{\infty}\colon ]0,N_{\infty}]\cap\R_{+}\rightarrow\R$ that admits at least one zero and satisfies $\liminf_{x\downarrow 0} f_{\infty}(x) > 0$ and
\begin{equation*}
\liminf_{s\uparrow\infty} \inf_{x\in ]0,N(s)]:\,x < x_{0}}h\big(s,x,N(s),\mathcal{L}(I_{s})\big) - f_{\infty}(x) \geq 0
\end{equation*}
for the smallest zero $x_{0}$ of $f_{\infty}$, then $I$ persists above $x_{0}$.

\item If there is a continuous function $g_{\infty}\colon \R_{+}\rightarrow\R$ that has at least one zero in $[0,N_{\infty}[$ and satisfies $\limsup_{x\rightarrow N_{\infty}} g_{\infty}(x) < 0$ and
\begin{equation*}
\limsup_{s\uparrow\infty}\sup_{x\in ]0,N(s)]:\,x > y_{0}} h\big(s,x,N(s),\mathcal{L}(I_{s})\big) - g_{\infty}(x) \leq 0
\end{equation*}
for the largest zero $y_{0}$ of $g_{\infty}$ in $[0,N_{\infty}[$, then $\liminf_{t\uparrow\infty} I_{t} \leq y_{0}$ a.s.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, if the requirements of~(i) and~(ii) are met and $x_{0} = y_{0}$, then persistence~\eqref{eq:persistence around a point} around $x_{0}$ holds for $I$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.100291+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f5fac8bbfcfa2a5dfa6f9688a8365046f268b6bfbeae7483d16bd883ae6d28e4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:subcritical of arXiv:2601.20502, 'Optimal matching under size priority'] Define the families of truncated decorated trees $\left(\mathbb{T}_H,o,(i_{-}^{H},Z_{-}^H),(i_{+}^{H},Z_{+}^H)\right)_{H \geq 0}$ as follows:
