{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.650454+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f6075e2bc5cb6915453b48e7f58e709ecf822b1229e160a0e90a20856cc50769","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:bw of arXiv:2607.06249, 'Extreme least singular values of Gaussian row submatrices and a phase retrieval stability '] Let $A_m\in\mathbb{R}^{(2m-1)\times m}$ have independent standard Gaussian entries. Then
\begin{equation}
  \frac{1}{m}\log\omega(A_m)\xrightarrow{P}-\log4,
\end{equation}
with the rate
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{P}\left\{\left|\frac{1}{m}\log\omega(A_m)+\log4\right|>\varepsilon\right\}=O_\varepsilon(m^{-1})
\end{equation}
for every fixed $\varepsilon>0$. Equivalently, $\omega(A_m)=4^{-m+o_P(m)}$.

Let $R_m=\max_{1\le i\le 2m-1}\|a_i\|$. For every $b>1/4$, $\mathbb{P}\{\omega(A_m)\le R_m b^m\}\to1$. For
every $b<1/4$ and every fixed $C<\infty$, $\mathbb{P}\{\omega(A_m)\le C R_m b^m\}\to0$. Thus the Gaussian
critical base in the Balan--Wang question is $1/4$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:38:13.641965+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f60f4826928bf4d69befd44b235aa5e167aabed9cef68fcb667ad585a1b2799a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2609.00702, 'On the Set-Valued Katugampola Fractional Integral: Properties and Regular Selections'] Let $F:I\rightrightarrows \mathbb{R}$ be a set-valued function and $\mathfrak{I}$ be the collection of all integrable selections of $F.$ The set-valued Katugampola fractional integral is defined as 
\begin{equation*}
    \begin{aligned}
        _a^\rho\mathfrak{D}^\nu F(t)=\bigg\{\frac{(\rho+1)^{1-\nu}}{\Gamma(\nu)}\int_a^t(t^{\rho+1}-x^{\rho+1})^{\nu-1}x^{\rho}f(x)dx:f\in\mathfrak{I}\bigg\}
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
where $\nu>0$ and $\rho \neq -1$ are real numbers.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:38:58.686915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f61693da9163467538a99faf40147b50e3443aa1f63db92a28897646403f84d4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:poiss-approx of arXiv:2510.18856, 'Evolution of recursive trees with limited memory'] Let $D_{j,n}$ be the degree of vertex \textcolor{black}{$j$} at time $n$.
    For any $\epsilon > 1$, there exists $j_0$ such that for all $j_0 \leqslant j \leqslant n$,  \begin{align*}
        &d_{TV}\left(D_{j,n}, \text{Poisson}(1)\right) \\
        &\leqslant \epsilon \left[1-(1+\epsilon j^{\beta-1})^{-\beta}\right] + \epsilon j^{-\beta} + \max(\epsilon-1,j^{-\beta})+ \mathds{1}\left\{N_j\geqslant n-j\right\} \abs{\frac{n-j}{j^\beta}-1}
    \end{align*}where $N_j = \abs{\left\{i\geqslant j:i-i^\beta \leqslant j\right\}}.$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:27.981298+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f619fbdec7b3beda5b4705226889b28b7afa9da60f2cbf710326a68a9cc84f48","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2607.13273, 'Asymptotics of transition densities for L\'evy processes with stochastic resetting'] Let $a, b >0$ and $f(s) = \frac{a}{s} + b s,\, s\in (0,\infty).$ Let $\gamma > 0,$ and a positive
    function $g\in C_b(\R_+)$ be such that for some $\kappa\in [0,\infty),$ and $g_0, g_\infty \in (0,\infty)$ we have
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{s\to 0^+} \frac{g(s)}{s^{\kappa}} = g_0, \quad \lim_{s \to \infty} g(s) = g_\infty.
    \end{equation}
    Then
    \begin{equation}
       \int_0^\infty s^{-\gamma}e^{-rf(s)}g\left(\frac{t^{2/\alpha}}{rs}\right)\, \dx[s]
       \sim \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{a}}\left(\frac{a}{b}\right)^{\frac34 - \frac{\gamma}{2}} \frac{e^{-2r\sqrt{ab}}}{\sqrt{r}} g\left(\frac{t^{2/\alpha}\sqrt{b}}{r\sqrt{a}}\right).
    \end{equation}
    Furthermore, the above asymptotic equality holds also for
    any positive function $g\in C_b(\R_+)$ such that $\lim_{s\to 0^+} \frac{g(s)}{s^\kappa} = g_0$ 
    (without assuming the existence of a positive limit in infinity) provided $\frac{t}{r^{\alpha/2}} <M$ for some constant $M>0.$","status":"unverified"}

