=\frac12\int_0^T\left(\mathbb{E}[N^1_s\lambda^2_s]-\mathbb{E}[N^2_s\lambda^1_s]\right)\,\mathrm{d} s,

\end{equation}
and the vector of moments $\mathbb{E}[N^i_s\lambda^j_s]$ closes linearly after adjoining the Hawkes memory variables.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.344395+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f6e0fba8d36273c78f8a5bf89b2654701c3ff31118f9797c2b5d2605e179008c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eqNullset of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] The measure space $ (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mu) $ is called complete (and $ \mu $ is said to be complete on $ \mathcal{F} $), if $ \mathcal{F} $ includes all subsets of null sets, i.e., 
