{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.615611+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f7c1a30130e3da3727c8cd5b99f8ba0af7b9ea6690e6491298d490ca38ed7775","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 (Markov property) of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] Suppose that~$x_1,\dots,x_m\in\T_n$ are vertices such that the subtrees $\T^{(1)},\dots,\T^{(m)}$ of~$\T_n$ rooted at these vertices are vertex-disjoint. Write~$n_i$ for the depth of~$\T^{(i)}$ and denote
\begin{equation}

\V(x_1,\dots,x_m):=\{x_1,\dots,x_m\}\cup\Bigl(\T_n\smallsetminus\bigcup_{i=1}^m\T^{(i)}\Bigr).
\end{equation}
Then, for   all   $t>0$, conditional on $\{L_t(x)\colon x\in\V(x_1,\dots,x_m)\}$, the families
\begin{equation}

\{L_t(x)\colon x\in\T^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^m
\end{equation}
are independent with the~$i$-th family distributed as $\{L_u(x)\colon x\in\T_{n_i}\}$ for~$u:=L_t(x_i)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.607777+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f7c36751c74819a9573e1d418ed4f4d3f6bd2f99fce6b330682b5861e7497f66","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 10 of arXiv:2607.08242, 'Convergence of random sums in non-commutative probability'] Suppose the assumptions of Theorem \ref{thm31}(ii) hold. 
    Suppose $\frac{N_n}{\eta_n}$ has a limiting probability measure $\mu$. Then, 
    \begin{equation*}
        \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\tilde{\varphi}(\tilde{\mathcal{S}}_{N_n}^m)=\varphi(s^m)\int_{0}^\infty x^{m/2}\,\mu(\mathrm{d}x),\ m\in\mathbb{N}.
        \end{equation*}
        In other words, $\tilde{\mathcal{S}}_{N_n}\stackrel{*}{\to}
        sy$ where $s$ and $y$ are independent variables, 

        and the $m$th moment of $y$ is given by $\int_0^\infty x^{m/2}\,\mu(\mathrm{d}x)$. If further $y$ has a probability measure $\mu_y$, and
        $\tilde{\mathcal{S}}_{N_n}$ has a probability measure $\mu_n$, then $\mu_n$ converges weakly to $\mu_s \star \mu_y$ where $\star$ denotes product convolution of two probability measures.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.726830+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f7ca7e9461aa52b4acd238084f2d08eb062cf3bf12b38823df0f7f85b43c804f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:AffentrangerWieacker of arXiv:2411.19163, '\bfseries Random polytopes in convex bodies:\\ Bridging the gap between extremal container'] Fix $m\in\mathbb{N}$, $m\geq 1$, and let $\alpha\geq 0$, $a_1\geq a_2\geq\ldots\geq a_m>0$ and $c\in(0,1]$. 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[(1)] Suppose that $a_{m-1}-a_m>0$. Then
    \begin{equation*}
        \int_{0}^1\ldots\int_{0}^1 (1-cx_1\cdots x_m)^{n-\alpha} x_1^{a_1}\cdots x_m^{a_m} \, \textup{d} x_m\ldots\textup{d} x_1
        = \frac{\Gamma(a_m+1) (cn)^{-(a_m+1)}}{(a_1-a_m)\cdots(a_{m-1}-a_m)} (1+ o_n(1)),
    \end{equation*}

        \item[(2)] Suppose that $\ell\in\{1,\ldots,m-1\}$ is the smallest index such that $a_\ell=\ldots=a_m$. Then
        \begin{equation*}
        \int_{0}^1\ldots\int_{0}^1 (1-cx_1\cdots x_m)^{n-\alpha} x_1^{a_1}\cdots x_m^{a_m} \, \textup{d} x_m\ldots\textup{d} x_1
        = {\Gamma(a_\ell+1)(cn)^{-(a_\ell+1)}(\log n)^{m-\ell}\over(a_1-a_\ell)\cdots(a_{\ell-1}-a_\ell)}(1+o_n(1)).
        \end{equation*}
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:17:17.096329+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f7cc909afa96e31d6fe4e2205e73109e03f32736329db3851ac5506cdca36072","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 (Exact tail-transform normalization) of arXiv:2608.20101, '\bfseries Largest bulk gap of the complex Ginibre ensemble'] Under \eqref{eq:Bassumption}, for every \(x\in\R\),
