{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.024420+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f81f3e7f2e6a1ced342cc1359ecd68ba62ceb31924e842d28ce11dfff5b61944","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 (\citeauthor{D65}, \citeyear{D65}, \S6.10) of arXiv:2606.06739, 'The relationship between the transition functions of the labeled and unlabeled versions of'] Let $E$ and $E_0$ be complete separable metric spaces, and let $\Phi:E\mapsto E_0$ be Borel measurable and surjective.  Let $P(t,x,\cdot)$ be the transition function for a Markov process $X$ in $E$. If for each $t>0$ the condition 
\begin{equation}
P(t,x,\Phi^{-1}(\cdot))\text{ depends on }x\text{ only through }\Phi(x)
\end{equation}
holds, then $Y:=\Phi\circ X$ is a Markov process in $E_0$ with transition function
\begin{equation}
Q(t,y,\cdot):=P(t,x,\Phi^{-1}(\cdot)),\text{ where }x\in\Phi^{-1}(y).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:14:37.618282+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f81f5c945e6d305b8346ba6639c78305a74ec4c67ac18d55aea0b5439ea6291e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 17 of arXiv:2608.19065, 'Boundedly exchangeable graphs'] Let $\mathbf{A} = (A, N)$ be a random element in $\Rcal$ such that $\pbb(N = n) > 0$ for every $n \in \nbb$. Then $\mathbf{A}$ satisfies
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item (Finite exchangeability) For all $n \geq 1$ and all permutations $\pi$ of $\{1,\dots,n\}$:
        \begin{equation*}
            A \mid (N = n) \overset{d}{=} A \circ \pi \mid (N = n)
        \end{equation*}
        \item (Compatibility) For all $n \geq 1$ and all $m \geq n$:
        \begin{equation*}
            A \mid (N = n) \overset{d}{=} A[n] \mid (N = m)
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate}
    if and only if there exist an infinite jointly exchangeable matrix $A^{(\infty)} \in \rbb^{\nbb \times \nbb}$ such that for all $n \geq 1$:
    \begin{equation} 
        A \mid (N = n) \overset{d}{=} A^{(\infty)}[n]
    \end{equation}
    In that case, $A^{(\infty)}$ is unique in distribution.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.317682+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8214779b5c585ce70b8ab15fe9f4247f84b5ef26dec1f0fd44eb292c016b14e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:592 of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] \textnormal{(i) (Lower bound)} There exist $c=c(u_1)>0$ and $n_1(u_1)>0$, such that for all $z\in S_i$ and $n>n_1(u_1)$, we have
