{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.787858+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f85c89663099d713135504d67e02f1ee85b4476e0cc3ca1e322019afe2a5c364","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:tailrl-threshold-reparameterization of arXiv:2609.02987, 'Tail Likelihood Reinforcement Learning'] Define
\begin{equation}
F_w(t)
:=
\int_0^t w(s)\,ds,
\qquad
\widetilde r(x,z):=F_w\!\left(r(x,z)\right).

\end{equation}
Then $F_w$ is a strictly increasing map from $[0,1]$ to $[0,1]$, and
\begin{equation}
J_w(\theta;x)
=
