{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.662330+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8664a34a65bd380c2198fbeeadcc293a5fa38c05adb1d86f2451220a0af1c92","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm5 of arXiv:2412.05521, 'Random attractors for the stochastic Nernst-Planck-Navier-Stokes system with multiplicativ'] Let $v^\varepsilon(0)$ $\in$  $H^1$ and $c_i(0) \in H^1$ be nonnegative with $c_i(0) \geq 0 $. Let $T > 0$. Then, there exists a unique solution $(v^\varepsilon,c_1,c_2)$ satisfying $v^\varepsilon \in 
		L^{\infty}(0,T;H^1) \cap L^2(0,T;H^2)$ and $c_i \in L^{\infty}(0,T;H^1) \cap L^2(0,T;H^2) $. Moreover $c_i(t) \geq 0$ holds on $[0,T]$. 
		\begin{proof}
			 By the local existence theorem (Theorem (thm2 of the paper)), there exists $T_0 \geq 0$ depending only on the norms of initial data in $H^1$ such that the solution exists and belongs to $H^1$. By Remark (Remark 1 of the paper), we find $c_i(t) \geq 0$. Moreover, the inequality (equation b33 of the paper) holds on $[0,T_0]$. It follows from Theorem (thm4 of the paper) that the solution is bounded in $H^1$. Applying the local existence theorem again and starting from $T_0$, we deduce that the solution can be extended for $T_1$ $\geq T_0$. The inequality (equation b33 of the paper) holds on $[0,T_1]$. Since the inequality holds as long as $c_i$ $\geq 0$, reasoning by contradiction we conclude that the solution can extend to the whole interval $[0,T]$.
		\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:34:20.739716+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f86942a2c59c0a606883139ae4499f90a8b556dcf19959879c9efff0d63ccfe6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 28 of arXiv:2609.02197, '2609.02197'] Let $\theta \in \left]-\infty,2-\frac{d}{2}\right[.$
