{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:45:32.100873+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8d6ffc28082ac6ffda745559ec0820c0f89ae4fa0b4a4f46748c13ca72a704d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma shifting RW of arXiv:2411.14350, 'Randomized Geodesic Flow on Hyperbolic Groups'] Let $(X_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ be a random walk on a hyperbolic group $G$ with symmetric step distribution $\mu$ whose support is finite and generates $G$ and starting point $X_0=o$. Fix $m\in \mathbb{N}$ and set
\begin{align*}
\bar Z^m_n &:= X_m^{-1}X_{m-n} &&\text{ for } n=0,...,m;\\
Z^m_n &:= X_m^{-1}X_{m+n} &&\text{ for } n\geq m\\
\end{align*}
Then the following hold:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\big(\bar Z^m_n\big)_{n=0}^m$ and $\big(Z^m_n\big)_{i=0}^{\infty}$ are independent for any $m\in\mathbb{N}$. 
\item Furthermore, they converge in distribution to independent random walks $\big(\bar Z_n\big)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ and $\big(Z_n\big)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ with step distribution $\mu$ and starting point $o$.
\item Finally, $\big(\bar Z_n\big)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ and $\big(Z_n\big)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ converge a.s to points $Z_{\infty}$ and $Z_{-\infty}$ on $\partial G$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.645348+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8d9fd3b1f1ce81f3a27a65dc393d072f056680a58a470740cabadca86d5017a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor joint-conv of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] In the above setup, the joint law
    \begin{equation}
        \big(h_\delta,\ \delta^{-1/8}\sigma_\delta,\ \delta^{-1/8}\tilde\sigma_\delta,\ \delta^{-1/4}\tau_\delta,\ \delta^{-1/8}\sigma_\delta^\dagger,\ \delta^{-1/8}\tilde\sigma_\delta^\dagger,\ \delta^{-1/4}\tau_\delta^\dagger\big)
    \end{equation}
    converges as $\delta\to0$ to
    \begin{equation}
        \big(1/(\pi\sqrt{2})h,\ \sigma,\ \tilde\sigma,\ \field{\cos((1/\sqrt{2})h)},\ \sigma^\dagger,\ \tilde\sigma^\dagger,\ \field{\sin((1/\sqrt{2})h)}\big),
    \end{equation}
    where we recall $h$ is a GFF with zero boundary conditions. Moreover, 
    \begin{equation}
        :\! \sigma\tilde\sigma \!: \ =\ \field{\cos(h/\sqrt{2})}\quad \text{and}\quad :\! \sigma^\dagger\tilde \sigma^\dagger \!: \ =\ \field{\sin(h/\sqrt{2})}.
    \end{equation}
   Here $:\! \sigma\tilde\sigma \!: $ denotes the Wick product of two Ising fields given by
   \begin{equation}
       (:\! \sigma\tilde\sigma \!: , f) = \lim_{\varepsilon\to0}(\sigma^\varepsilon\tilde\sigma^\varepsilon, f),
   \end{equation}
   where $\sigma^\varepsilon, \tilde\sigma^\varepsilon$ are smooth $\varepsilon$-mollifications of $\sigma, \tilde\sigma$ respectively. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:11.413413+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8da97809c9a6deece3a59140775bd2986853ab60ed899ed6ec5595ef239262b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thmd 13 of arXiv:2606.08115, '2606.08115'] Let $\ov{x} \in \mathcal{X}_V$. Assume that the following conditions are valid for a sequence of random variables $\left\{Y_k\right\}$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[1)] the random variable $Y_k$ is $\mathcal{F}_k$-measurable for every natural $k$;
\item[2)] $\left\{Y_n\right\}$ converges as $n \to \infty$ to some random variable $Y_{\infty}$ $\pr_{\bfx}^{+}$-a.s.;
\item[3)] $\left\{Y_n\right\}$ is a uniformly bounded sequence, it means that there exists a positive constant $C$ such that $|Y_k| \le C$ for any natural $k$.
\end{enumerate}
Then
$$\lim_{n \to \infty} {\bf{E}} \left( Y_n \left.\right| \tau(\ov{x}) > n \right) = {\bf{E}}_{\bfx}^{+} Y_{\infty}.$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:58.356663+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8e0937b8e24d8f100b523aea55095cdcb29b223d26c44af2acc183cb5955578","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2506.18558, '2506.18558'] {\rm(\textbf {Weak averaging principle})}
Suppose that Assumptions {\rm\ref{A1}}, {\rm\ref{A2}}, {\rm\ref{A3}} and {\rm\ref{A5}} hold. Then, for any initial value $(x,y)\in\RR^{n}\times\RR^{m}$ and $T>0$, $\{X^{\varepsilon}\}_{\varepsilon\in(0,1]}:=\{(X^{\varepsilon}_t)_{t\ge0}\}_{\varepsilon\in(0,1]}$ converges weakly to $(X_t)_{t\ge0}$ in $C([0,T];\RR^n)$ as $\varepsilon\to 0$, where $(X_t)_{t\ge0}$ is the unique strong solution to the following averaged SDE
\begin{align}
d X_{t}=\bar{b}(X_{t})dt+\Theta(X_t)d\bar{W}_t,\quad X_{0}=x\in \mathbb{R}^n,
\end{align}
where $\bar{b}$ is defined in  the SDE \eqref{e:ASDE1}, $\Theta(x):=\bar{\Sigma}^{1/2}(x)$ with $\bar{\Sigma}(x):=\displaystyle\int_{\RR^m}\Sigma(x,y)\,\mu^{x}(dy)$, and  $(\bar{W}_t)_{t\ge  0}$ is a standard $n$-dimensional Brownian motion. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:29:08.585356+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8e33f435f98d604224343febb2f3e500fec9073f201e01eb3ad372ab639c51a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def-quantum-cone of arXiv:2411.19810, 'Radial conformal welding in Liouville quantum gravity'] Fix $W > 0$ and let $\alpha = Q - \frac{W}{2\gamma}$. Sample independent standard Brownian motions $(B_t)_{t \geq 0}, (\tilde B_t)_{t \geq 0}$ conditioned on $\tilde B_{t} - (Q-\alpha) < 0$ for all $t>0$. Let
	\[Y_t := \left\{ \begin{array}{rcl} 
				B_{t}+(Q-\alpha) t & \mbox{for} & t\ge 0\\
				\tilde{B}_{-t} -(Q-\alpha) |t| & \mbox{for} & t<0
			\end{array} 
			\right. \]
	Let $\hat \psi = \psi_1 + \psi_2$ where $\psi_1$ is the distribution on $\mathcal{C}$ which is constant on each line segment $\{t\} \times [0,2\pi]$ satisfying $\psi_1(z) = Y_{\mathrm{Re}(z)}$, and $\psi_2$ is an independent copy of $h_\mathcal{C}^2$. The \emph{weight $W$ quantum cone} is $(\mathcal{C}, \hat \psi, -\infty, +\infty)/{\sim_\gamma}$, and we define the probability measure $\mathcal{M}^\mathrm{cone}(W)$ to be its law.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.553457+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8e3ff45edfcbcd5f33966e04f8be357b1dd6f09a913b9ac6e1e4ba3fc6ecf8e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 11 (Solution for $\theta=0$) of arXiv:2604.02129, 'Realisation of constraints in underdamped Langevin dynamics'] Let $c^2 = 4ab$ with $\theta$ defined in~\eqref{def:theta}. Then~\eqref{eq:lemsollangevin} admits the solution 
\begin{align*}
