{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.768207+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fac6a86fd8f29e67f5468ffd299014d6b3e017121216bb2659ef7a06833f9169","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 19 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Fix a set $\Sigma = \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket$ with $N \in \mathbb{N} \cup \{\infty\}$ and $T_0, T_1\in \mathbb{Z}$ with $T_0 \leq T_1$. A $\Sigma$-indexed geometric line ensemble $\mathfrak{L} : \Sigma \times \llbracket T_0, T_1 \rrbracket \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}$ is said to satisfy the {\em interlacing Gibbs property} if it is interlacing, meaning that $L_i \succeq L_{i+1}$ for all $i \in \llbracket 1, N-1 \rrbracket$, and for any finite $K = \llbracket k_1, k_2 \rrbracket \subset \llbracket 1, N - 1 \rrbracket$ and $a,b \in \llbracket T_0, T_1 \rrbracket$ with $a < b$ the following holds.  Suppose that $f, g$ are two increasing paths drawn in $\{ (r,z) \in \mathbb{Z}^2 : a \leq r \leq b\}$ and $\vec{x}, \vec{y} \in \mathfrak{W}_k$ with $k=k_2-k_1+1$ are such that $\mathbb{P}(A) > 0$, where $A$ denotes the event $$A =\{ \vec{x} = ({L}_{k_1}(a), \dots, {L}_{k_2}(a)), \vec{y} = ({L}_{k_1}(b), \dots, {L}_{k_2}(b)), L_{k_1-1} \llbracket a,b \rrbracket = f, L_{k_2+1} \llbracket a,b \rrbracket = g \},$$
where if $k_1 = 1$ we adopt the convention $f = \infty = L_0$. Then, writing $k = k_2 - k_1 + 1$, we have for any $\{ B_i \in \Omega(a, b, x_i , y_i) \}_{i = 1}^k$ that
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}\left( L_{i + k_1-1}\llbracket a,b \rrbracket = B_{i} \mbox{ for $i \in \llbracket 1, k \rrbracket$} \, \vert  A \, \right) = \mathbb{P}_{\ice, \operatorname{Geom}}^{a,b, \vec{x}, \vec{y}, f, g} \left( \cap_{i = 1}^k\{ Q_i = B_i \} \right),
\end{equation}
where $\mathfrak{Q} = \{Q_i\}_{i = 1}^k$ has law $\mathbb{P}_{\ice, \operatorname{Geom}}^{a,b, \vec{x}, \vec{y}, f, g}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:48:25.155354+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_facfb490385a4669c278c56f7f1150756286de340bb5421d92de1685584d0187","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:interp of arXiv:2607.05345, 'The local product condition implies cutoff'] Fix two measures $\mu,\pi\in\mathcal {P}({\mathbb{X}})$ with $\mathrm{d}_{\textsc{tv}}(\mu,\pi)<1$. Then, there is a   measure $\nu\in\mathcal {P}({\mathbb{X}})$ which is absolutely continuous w.r.t. both $\mu$ and $\pi$, such that
		\begin{eqnarray*}
			\left\|\frac{\mathrm{d} \nu}{\mathrm{d} \mu}\right\|_\infty\vee\ \ \left\|\frac{\mathrm{d}\nu}{\mathrm{d}\pi}\right\|_\infty	 & \leq & \frac{1}{1-\mathrm{d}_{\textsc{tv}}(\mu,\pi)}.
		\end{eqnarray*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.221793+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fadb9efbdb90777788491936aa75a76ab244901abbfbcba21ebf6397897ccd35","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:der-mart-lower of arXiv:2608.20537, 'Persistent anisotropy in multi-dimensional\\ branching Brownian motion'] For all $\underline{c} \in (0, 1]$, there exists $t_{\textnormal{max}} \leq -1$ such that the following holds.
  For all $\mathfrak{t} \leq t_{\textnormal{max}}$, there exists $\underline{T}(\mathfrak{t}) > \abs{\mathfrak{t}}$ and a function $\underline{u}(t,x; \mathfrak{t}) \geq 0$ defined where $t \in [-\underline{T}(\mathfrak{t}), \mathfrak{t}]$ and $x \geq 2 \log \left |t \right|$ satisfying the following properties:
  \begin{enumerate}[label = \textnormal{(\roman*)},itemsep = 2pt]
  \item $\underline{u} = 0$ where $x \geq \left |t \right|$;

  \item

    $\underline{u} \leq \underline{c} \mathrm{e}^{-x}$ where $t = -\underline{T}$ or $x = 2 \log \left |t \right|$;   

  \item $\underline{u}$ is a subsolution of (equation eq:ancient of the paper);

  \item $\partial_x \underline{u} \leq 0$; and

  \item for all $x \in [4 \log \abs{\mathfrak{t}}, \abs{\mathfrak{t}}/4]$,
    \begin{equation}

      \underline{u}(\mathfrak{t}, x; \mathfrak{t}) \geq 2^{-8} \underline{c} x \mathrm{e}^{-x}.
    \end{equation}
  \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.758744+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fae166bf50a3272f99228836b4f177f739dfee9a73f9eca5d56e2f8efae855f5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 6 of arXiv:2605.14254, '2605.14254'] The law of the supremum of $\xi^{\lambda,z}$ up to time $\sigma_z^{\lambda}$ is the same as the law of the supremum of $z+B^{-\lambda}$, i.e.,
		\begin{equation}
			\sup\limits_{t\leq \sigma_z^{\lambda}} \xi_t^{\lambda,z} \overset{\text{law}}{=} \sup\limits_{t\geq 0} (z+B_t^{-\lambda}).
		\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.772018+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fae4ccd0f76806ed9cb79dcc1226bb990bf8abba29dfc932245a6e3ba844447c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 51 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Assume the same notation as in Lemma \ref{Lem.PrelimitKernelBot} with $\mathcal{T} \subset (0, \infty)$, and recall the contours $\mathcal{C}_z^{\varphi}$ from Definition \ref{Def.S1Contours}. Fix $x_{\infty},y_{\infty} \in \mathbb{R}$, $s,t \in \mathcal{T}$ and sequences $x_N \in \Lambda_s(N)$, $y_N \in \Lambda_{t}(N)$ such that $\lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} x_N = x_{\infty}$ and $\lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} y_N = y_{\infty}$. Then, we have the following limits.
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&\lim_{N \rightarrow \infty}  (1-c)^{-2}\sigma_1^{-2}N^{-2/3} I^N_{11}(s,x_N;t,y_N) = I^{\infty}_{11}(s,x_{\infty};t,y_{\infty}) \mbox{, where } I^{\infty}_{11}(s,x;t,y) \\
& = \frac{1}{(2\pi \im)^{2}}\int_{\mathcal{C}^{\pi/3}_{\sigma_1}} dz\int_{\mathcal{C}^{\pi/3}_{\sigma_1}} dw e^{z^3/3 + w^3/3 - f_1 s z^2 - f_1 t w^2 - xz - yw  } \frac{z-w  }{zw (z + w)},
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&\lim_{N\rightarrow \infty} I^N_{12}(s,x_N;t,y_N) = I^{\infty}_{12}(s,x_{\infty};t,y_{\infty}) , \mbox{ where }  I^{\infty}_{12}(s,x;t,y) \\
&= \frac{1}{(2\pi \im)^{2}}\int_{\mathcal{C}^{\pi/3}_{\sigma_1}} dz\int_{\mathcal{C}^{2\pi/3}_{-\sigma_1}} dw e^{z^3/3 - w^3/3 - f_1 s z^2 + f_1 t w^2 - xz + yw  } \frac{z+w }{2z(z-w) },
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&\lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} (1-c)^{2}\sigma_1^{2}N^{2/3} I^N_{22}(s,x_N;t,y_N) =  I^{\infty}_{22}(s,x_{\infty};t,y_{\infty}) , \mbox{ where }  I^{\infty}_{22}(s,x;t,y)  \\
& = \frac{1}{(2\pi \im)^{2}}\int_{\mathcal{C}^{2\pi/3}_{-\sigma_1}} dz\int_{\mathcal{C}^{2\pi/3}_{-\sigma_1}} dw e^{-z^3/3 - w^3/3 + f_1 s z^2 + f_1 t w^2 + xz + y w  } \frac{z-w}{4(z+w) },
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation} 
\lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} R^N_{12}(s,x_N;t,y_N) = R^{\infty}_{12}(s,x_{\infty};t,y_{\infty}) ,\mbox{ where } R^{\infty}_{12}(s,x;t,y) = \frac{- {\bf 1}\{s < t\}}{\sqrt{4\pi f_1 (t-s)}} \cdot e^{-\frac{(y - x)^2}{4 f_1 (t-s)}}. 
\end{equation}
In addition,   we have 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} (1-c)^{2}\sigma_1^{2}N^{2/3} R^N_{22}(s,x_N;t,y_N) = R^{\infty}_{22}(s,x_{\infty};t,y_{\infty}),
\end{equation} 
where
\begin{equation}
R^{\infty}_{22}(s,x;t,y)=-\frac{1}{4\pi\im}\int_{\mathcal{C}^{2\pi/3}_{-\sigma_1}} dw \cdot w e^{f_1 (s + t)w^2+w(y - x )  } . 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.780988+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fae5f196a8d40e382a7a10dc1a917283a6e80a4bccc5e1deb7fd8167ef0f6836","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 of arXiv:2609.03054, 'The classes of bivariate Schur and Herglotz matrix-valued rational functions: realizations'] Let $F\in\Cmats{m}{n}(z_1,z_2)$ be a rational  matrix valued function. Then the following conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[\rm (i)]
\item $F$ has no poles in $\RHPtwo$, and $F(z_1,z_2)$ is a contraction for any $z_1,z_2\in\RHP$;
\item $F$ admits a realization of the form
\begin{equation}
F(z_1,z_2)= D+ C(z_1P_1+z_2P_2+A)^{-1}B,
\end{equation}
where the block matrix $\matr{A&B\\C&D} \in\Cmats{(k+m)}{(k+n)}$ is such that
\begin{equation}

\matr{
A+A^*- C^*C & C^*D+ B \\ D^* C+ B^*  & I - D^* D
} \geq 0,
\end{equation}
$P_1,P_2\in \sqmat{k}$ are positive semidefinite and $P_1+P_2=I_k$.
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, if any of the above conditions holds, then
the limit $\lim_{t\to\infty}F(t,t)$ exists.
Additionally, if $F\in\mathbb R^{m\times n }(z_1,z_2)$ then the matrices $A,B,C,D,P_1,P_2$ in {\rm (ii)} may be chosen as real matrices.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.938045+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_faec2c7cead59253e2d4f62a4aa500caf87246b46cd97f4817987d426b200057","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 23 of arXiv:2608.02927, 'Thinning Operation via the Poisson-F\"ollmer Process'] (Equality cases of Thinning Lemma). Let $X$ be a random variable with density $f$ with respect to $\pi_{T}.$ Assume that $f : \N \rightarrow ( 0, \infty)$ satisfies $H( X | \pi_{T}) < \infty.$ Then, equality holds in the Thinning Lemma, i.e.,
    \begin{displaymath}
        H( T_{a}(X) | \pi_{aT}) = a H(X | \pi_{T} )
    \end{displaymath}
    for some $a \in (0,1)$ if and only if $f(k)=e^{\beta k + \gamma}$ for some $\beta ,\gamma \in \R$ (that is $X$ is Poisson distributed).","status":"unverified"}

