{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:35:56.960266+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fcb1b2f004dde659ff7d1cbeaa572a6d9c8e6c626b1b0496e79f85d6e5ad0a05","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:E[sl] of arXiv:2604.05921, 'Simplicity of random hypergraphs'] Let $R(\cdot)$ be as in (equation def:B of the paper) and let $E^* = \{e \in E: \delta_e^{\textnormal{tail}} = \delta_e^{\textnormal{head}}\}$. We denote $\delta_e = \delta_e^{\textnormal{tail}}=\delta_e^{\textnormal{head}}$ for $e \in E^*$. Then, for a uniformly random directed hypergraph with degree sequence $\vb*{d}$,
    \begin{align}

         \mathds{E}[S_n] &= \sum_{e \in E^*} \frac{(n\mathds{E}[d_U^{\textnormal{in}}]-\delta_e)!(n\mathds{E}[d_U^{\textnormal{out}}]-\delta_e)!}{(n\mathds{E}[d_U^{\textnormal{in}}])!(n\mathds{E}[d_U^{\textnormal{out}}])!} \sum_{\substack{\vb*{a} \in \mathds{N}^{\delta_e}:\\\sum_{i=1}^{\delta_e} ia_i = \delta_e}}  \frac{\delta_e!^2}{\prod_{k=1}^{\delta_e} k!^{2a_k} } \sum_{\alpha(\cdot) \in R(\vb*{a})} (-1)^{\sum_{\vb*{x} \in \mathds{N}^{\delta_e}} (\sum_{i=1}^{\delta_e} x_i - 1)\alpha(\vb*{x})}\nonumber\\
     &\hspace{1cm}
    \times\prod_{\vb*{y} \in \mathds{N}^{\delta_e}} \Bigg( \frac{1}{\alpha(\vb*{y})!} \Big(\frac{n\mathds{E}\big[\prod_{i=1}^{\delta_e} (\mathds{1}_{\{d_U^{\textnormal{out}},d_U^{\textnormal{in}} \geq i\}}\frac{d_U^{\textnormal{out}}!}{(d_U^{\textnormal{out}}-i)!}\frac{d_U^{\textnormal{in}}!}{(d_U^{\textnormal{in}}-i)!})^{y_i} \big] \big(\sum_{i=1}^{\delta_e} y_i-1 \big)!}{\prod_{i=1}^{\delta_e} y_i!} \Big)^{\alpha(\vb*{y})} \Bigg).
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:06:42.719971+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fcbca423522759eb72388270079d9fbdf7f0ea932b315013e977d21f296514dc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: main of arXiv:2411.04157, 'Stochastic homogenization of dynamical discrete optimal transport'] Let $(\mathcal X_\omega,\mathcal E_\omega)_{\omega\in\Omega}$ be a random graph in $\mathbb{R}^n$ with random weights $(\sigma_\omega\colon \mathcal E_\omega\to[\lambda,\Lambda])_{\omega\in\Omega}$ and random means $(\theta^\omega)_{\omega\in\Omega}$ with $0<\lambda<\Lambda$, satisfying the graph Assumption (ass: graph of the paper) almost surely, such that $(\mathcal X_\omega,\mathcal E_\omega,\sigma_\omega,\theta_\omega)$ is stationary.

For every $\varepsilon>0$ let $({\mathcal X_{\omega,\varepsilon}},\mathcal E_{\omega,\varepsilon})$ be the rescaled random graph and $\mathcal{A}_{\omega,\varepsilon}$ be the rescaled action functional defined in Section (sec: rescaled graph of the paper). Let $A_\omega$ be the homogenized action functional given in (equation eq: hom action of the paper).
Then we have almost surely $\mathcal{A}_{\omega,\varepsilon}\stackrel{\Gamma}{\longrightarrow}_{\varepsilon\to 0} A_\omega$ in the sense that the following two conditions hold almost surely:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Lower bound: For every $m^\varepsilon \stackrel{*}{\rightharpoonup} \rho$ narrowly in $\mathcal{M}_+((0,T)\times \mathbb{R}^n)$ and $\iota J^\varepsilon \stackrel{*}{\rightharpoonup} j$ narrowly in $\mathcal{M}((0,T)\times \mathbb{R}^n;\mathbb{R}^n)$,
\begin{equation*}
    \liminf_{\varepsilon\to 0}  \mathcal{A}_{\omega,\varepsilon}(m^\varepsilon,J^\varepsilon) \geq  A_\omega(\rho,j).
\end{equation*}
    \item Upper bound:
    For every curve $(\rho,j)$ there exist sequences $m^\varepsilon \stackrel{*}{\rightharpoonup} \rho$ narrowly in $\mathcal{M}_+((0,T)\times \mathbb{R}^n)$ \text{ and } $\iota J^\varepsilon \stackrel{*}{\rightharpoonup} j$  narrowly in $\mathcal{M}((0,T)\times \mathbb{R}^n;\mathbb{R}^n)$ such that
\begin{equation*}
    \limsup_{\varepsilon\to 0}  \mathcal{A}_{\omega,\varepsilon}(m^\varepsilon,J^\varepsilon) \leq  A_\omega(\rho,j).
\end{equation*}
In addition, $\max_{t\in[0,1]}W_2(m^\varepsilon_t,\rho_t) \to 0$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.291739+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fcbec1fa0dbe008cefea1c25019abc0ae0e6a691f93695a68a172cfbc1e9c8f7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theo:1Gllaw of arXiv:2605.21265, 'The non-Hermitian minor process'] Let $X$ satisfy Assumption~(ass:chi of the paper)(i). Fix a (large) $L>0$ and a (small) $c_0>0$. There exists an $N$-independent constant $\tau>0$ such that
