{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:40.047324+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fee3e46bb81909f895c4ee1cb01d2748c77b890caf53442f00f28dc7d000b87d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2510.24562, 'Hard wall repulsion for the discrete Gaussian free field in random environment on $\mathbb'] For every $\delta>0$ there exists a constant $c_9=c_9(\delta)>0$ such that for $\bbQ$-a.a.\ $\omega \in \Omega_{\lambda,\Lambda}$ we can find $N_0(\omega,\delta) \in \bbN$ such that for all $N \geq N_0(\omega,\delta)$
    \begin{equation}
        \sup_{x \in V_N\setminus \partial_\mathrm{in} V_N}|\cL^\omega \bbE^{\omega,+}_N[\varphi_\cdot](x)| \leq \frac{c_9}{N^{2-\delta}} \,.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:42:16.243967+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fee80312b97bc18ae680080a630b8fa223965a1a6b7694c4707c492877506a9f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:poles of arXiv:2607.01463, 'Two Multi--Draw Coupon Collector models with different retention rules'] Set
\begin{equation}
\phi_{N}(s):=\Bigg(1-\frac{\binom{N-s}{d}}{\binom{N}{d}}\Bigg)^{-1}=\Bigg(1-\prod_{j=0}^{d-1}\left(1-\frac{s}{N-j}\right)\Bigg)^{-1}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation} 
Q_{N}(s):=1-\prod_{j=0}^{d-1}\left(1-\frac{s}{N-j}\right).
\end{equation}
Then, the poles of $\phi_N(s)$ are the zeros of the polynomial $Q_N(s)$. Clearly, $s=0$ is a simple pole of $\phi_{N}(s)$. Moreover, if \(d\) is odd, then the only real pole is \(s=0\). In case where \(d\) is even, then the only real poles are
    \[
    s=0
    \qquad\text{and}\qquad
    s=2N-d+1;
    \]
In particular, all poles of $\phi_N(s)$ are simple. The $d-1$ nonzero zeros of $Q_{N}(s)$ are denoted as $\omega_{1,N},\, \omega_{2,N},\cdots,\omega_{d-1,N}$ (for all positive integer values of $d$; when $d=1$ there are none, and $s=0$ is the only pole). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:12.186185+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_feea07beaa6439cc51e5fd35a8f00489975ebe32251608a217ce4712bb16dad9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 ({\cite[Lemma 5.3) of arXiv:2510.20492, 'Heterochromatic two-arm probabilities for metric graph Gaussian free fields'] {cai2024quasi}}]
 	For any $3\le d\le 5$, there exists $C>0$ such that for any $M\ge 1$, $v,w\in \widetilde{B}(M)$ and $A,D\subset [\widetilde{B}(CM)]^c$, 
 	\begin{equation}
 		\mathbb{P}^{D}\big(A\xleftrightarrow{\ge 0} v,A\xleftrightarrow{\ge 0} w \big)\lesssim  \big(|v-w|+1\big)^{-\frac{d}{2}+1}\mathbb{P}^{D}\big(A\xleftrightarrow{\ge 0} v \big). 
 	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.866186+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_feeab98bfb3a1d509b24a12a5e1ec9616a5e3f14f7d76996f1b7432f6d44ccee","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 23 of arXiv:2605.21638, 'Markov renewal theory for transfer operators and point processes on the line'] For all $x_0<0$ and bounded, local measurable $F\colon \mathbf{N} \rightarrow \R$, we have 
    \begin{equation*}
        \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty} \int_{\R^n} F(\delta_{x_0} + \dots + \delta_{x_n}) \dd \mu_{n,\beta}^{x_0} = \E_{\zeta}[F(\Theta_{x_0}\Phi^+ )],
    \end{equation*}
    where $\P_{\zeta}$ denote the distribution of the Markov chain $(Z_n)_{n\geq 0}$ with $Z_0 \sim \zeta$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:11:11.705773+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fef4688b2456adb307b3e4429929556fc53266b2bc4289e84f489589d21499ee","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem derivative of arXiv:2609.00672, 'Deciding superellipticity and computing the Weierstrass normal form'] Let $D$ be one of the divisors $F_\infty$ and $nQ$, $Q$ a point of $S$, and put $S'=S$, respectively $S'=S-Q$. Then

\[
