{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:22:08.367845+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ffde71ac72b1feaea6e0dddaf0ba0ffb2b61745a6c127bbc24a677f32fe9c018","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi def:wattsstrogatz of arXiv:2607.28817, 'Limiting spectral distribution for the adjacency matrix of the Watts--Strogatz random grap'] Let $n > 1$ be an integer, $K$ a positive even integer with $K<n$, and $p \in [0,1]$. The Watts--Strogatz random graph of size $n$ with parameters $(K,p)$, denoted $WS_n(K,p)$, is constructed as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Initial lattice.}  
    Let $V_n = \{1,2,\dots,n\}$ denote the set of vertices of $WS_n(K,p)$. Arrange the vertices on a clockwise cycle. Each vertex $i \in V_n$ is connected to its $K/2$ nearest neighbors on each side, forming a regular ring lattice of degree $K$.

    \item \textbf{Rewiring step.}  
    For $i = 1,2,\dots,n$ in increasing order, and for each edge $\{i,j\}$ such that $j$ is one of the $K/2$ rightmost neighbors of $i$ (taken in increasing order), independently with probability $p$, rewire the edge $\{i,j\}$ as follows: remove the edge $\{i,j\}$ and add the edge $\{i,k\}$, where $k$ is chosen uniformly at random from the set of vertices in $V_n \setminus \{i,j\}$ that are not adjacent to $i$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.129377+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ffe0d04fab0f5fc215076de13e61e9996d4abe0bd8db879775f71aa11092f654","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 1 of arXiv:2506.18207, 'Cartan's Path Development, the Logarithmic Signature and a Conjecture of Lyons-Sidorova'] For any simple path $\gamma = (x_t,y_t)$, if $\gamma|_{[0,1]}$ has infinite radius of convergence for its log-signature and $x_t\in[-1,1]$ for all $t\in[0,1]$, then looking at any fixed horizontal level $y=a$ of the image $\gamma([0,1])$, we only have the following two cases: 
\begin{itemize}
       \item The right street copies the left street. In the sense that for any $x\in(0,1)$, $(x,a)\in\gamma([0,1])$, we have $(x-1,a)\in\gamma([0,1])$. For any $x\in(-1,0)$, $(x,a)\in\gamma([0,1])$ we have $(x+1,a)\in\gamma([0,1])$.

       \item Both capture the missing points of the other. In the sense that for any $x\in(0,1)$, $(x,a)\notin\gamma([0,1])$, we have $(x-1,a)\in\gamma([0,1])$. For any $x\in(-1,0)$, $(x,a)\notin\gamma([0,1])$, we have $(x+1,a)\in\gamma([0,1])$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

